-- =====================================================================
-- Session 496 migration — 2026-11-09 — canon / Matthew 14–19 — Phase 2 REBUILD, batch b3: the orphan cards rewritten Come-and-See and threaded.
-- 294 cards rewritten in place (ids and verse pairs kept), 3 new thread(s), 3 unanchored thread(s) anchored, 294 thread members added.
-- Drafted on: Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5); read and corrected on Claude Fable 5.1; every note composed by script (assemble496.py): target verse whole
-- from parsed/canon.json (restored-names KJV, the canon convention), Matthew verse whole from the live text of the Nov 9 read-back, then the prose.
-- Safety (DO block, before any write): RAISES if a card id is gone or sits on another verse pair, if a Matthew verse's live text differs from the
-- read-back, if a card's live note is neither the note this was written against nor the new one, if a card is already in a thread this file does
-- not put it in, if a thread to join / an anchor verse is missing, or if a thread to anchor already has a different anchor.
-- Idempotent: a re-run reports 0 rewritten / 0 inserted / 0 anchored.  Nothing is deleted.
-- Run: python3 api/apply_migration.py data-schema/migrations/session496_matthew_b3_rebuild.sql
-- =====================================================================
\echo 'session496 — Matthew 14–19 rebuild starting...'
BEGIN;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b3_c (id BIGINT, src_ch INT, src_v INT, src_text TEXT, tgt_slug TEXT, tgt_ch INT, tgt_v INT, old_note TEXT, new_note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b3_t (slug TEXT, title TEXT, summary_md TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT, tier TEXT, sort_order INT) ON COMMIT DROP;

*When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13)

*And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* (Matthew 14:23)

Those two verses hold the same movement, the withdrawing to the Father after a death and after a crowd, and the mountains elsewhere in Matthew carry the same marks. At the opening of the Sermon the multitudes bring him up the hill and the disciples come to him there.

*And seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain: and when he was set, his disciples came unto him:* (Matthew 5:1)

Later he takes three of them away, and Moses and Elijah talk with him.

*And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* (Matthew 17:1)

On the night he is taken he leaves the disciples sitting and goes by himself to pray.

*Then cometh Yahusha (Jesus) with them unto a place called Gethsemane, and saith unto the disciples, Sit ye here, while I go and pray yonder.* (Matthew 26:36)

And at the end of the book the eleven are sent to a mountain he chose for them.

*Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16)

Each of these is a place where he is apart from the crowd and with the Father, whether he is praying alone, teaching his disciples, standing with Moses and Elijah, or sending out the eleven. The mountain in Matthew 14 is the first of these where he is wholly alone, and it comes straight after the feeding in the wilderness.$n$,14,22,23,'free',11397),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',$n$The plant my heavenly Father hath not planted, and the blind leaders$n$,$n$It ain't new. The Father has always had a planting, and the older scriptures already knew the difference between what he planted and what was sown over it. When his disciples tell him the Pharisees were offended, Yahusha (Jesus) says:

*But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father hath not planted, shall be rooted up.* (Matthew 15:13)

The plant he means is the one in his own parable of the field, where one man sowed and an enemy sowed after him.

*Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* (Matthew 13:24)

*But while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and went his way.* (Matthew 13:25)

When the disciples ask for the parable to be declared, he does not leave it a riddle.

*The field is the world; the good seed are the children of the kingdom; but the tares are the children of the wicked one;* (Matthew 13:38)

So a plant the Father did not plant is what the enemy sowed, and what is rooted up is judged at the harvest and not by us. Isaiah says what remains standing when it is done.

*Thy people also shall be all righteous: they shall inherit the land for ever, the branch of my planting, the work of my hands, that I may be glorified.* (Isaiah 60:21)

The next words in Matthew turn from the plants to the guides who lead people among them.

*Let them alone: they be blind leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both shall fall into the ditch.* (Matthew 15:14)

He had already said why such blindness stands.

*Therefore speak I to them in parables: because they seeing see not; and hearing they hear not, neither do they understand.* (Matthew 13:13)

And later he shows what the guiding looked like, in the oath rule that put the gold first.

*Woe unto you, ye blind guides, which say, Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor!* (Matthew 23:16)

It is the system and its rule that he names. The ones who follow it are the people in danger of the ditch, and that is why he speaks of them as he does.
$n$,15,13,14,'free',11424),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',$n$Binding and loosing on the word of two or three witnesses$n$,$n$It ain't new. The binding and loosing in this chapter is the court of Moses, and Yahusha (Jesus) is speaking from inside it. He has just said what is done when a brother will not hear the private word:

*But if he will not hear thee, then take with thee one or two more, that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established.* (Matthew 18:16)

That is Deuteronomy's own rule, and it was old when he said it:

*One witness shall not rise up against a man for any iniquity, or for any sin, in any sin that he sinneth: at the mouth of two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses, shall the matter be established.* (Deuteronomy 19:15)

In Moses the matter then goes before a court, where the two sides stand in the presence of Yahuah (the LORD) himself:

*Then both the men, between whom the controversy is, shall stand before Yahuah (LORD), before the priests and the judges, which shall be in those days;* (Deuteronomy 19:17)

Then Yahusha (Jesus) hands the verdict to the disciples together, in words he had spoken to Peter alone:

*Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever ye shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever ye shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* (Matthew 18:18)

So the binding and loosing is the judgment of a matter that has been heard on its witnesses, and it is carried by the ones he sends, as he says after the rising:

*Whose soever sins ye remit, they are remitted unto them; and whose soever sins ye retain, they are retained.* (John 20:23)

Then comes the verse that is so often lifted out of its place:

*For where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them.* (Matthew 18:20)

Read where he put it, these are the two or three witnesses of verse sixteen, standing where Moses stood the two men, before Yahuah (the LORD) and the judges, and he is in the midst of that hearing. Malachi shows the kind of people who are heard when they speak to one another:

*Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* (Malachi 3:16)

Nothing here turns a gathering into a room where anything asked is granted, and nothing gives men a power over heaven. A matter established on the mouth of witnesses is bound as the Torah already binds it, and the one who sits in the midst of it is the one who gave the rule.$n$,18,18,20,'free',367);
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b3_a (slug TEXT, book TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT) ON COMMIT DROP;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b3_m (thread_slug TEXT, card_id BIGINT, ord INT, member_note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;

CREATE OR REPLACE TEMP VIEW _s496_b3_lookup AS
SELECT b.slug AS book_slug, c.chapter_number, v.verse_number, v.id AS verse_id, v.text
  FROM verses v JOIN chapters c ON v.chapter_id = c.id JOIN books b ON c.book_id = b.id
  JOIN editions e ON b.edition_id = e.id
 WHERE e.slug = 'canon';

DO $s496$
DECLARE r RECORD; sid BIGINT; tid BIGINT; ltext TEXT; cur RECORD; n INT; av BIGINT; zv BIGINT;
        n_note INT := 0; n_note_same INT := 0; n_thr INT := 0; n_thr_same INT := 0; n_anc INT := 0; n_anc_same INT := 0; n_mem INT := 0; n_mem_same INT := 0; n_total INT;
BEGIN
  -- ---- guards: every row is checked before anything is written ----
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b3_c LOOP
    SELECT verse_id, text INTO sid, ltext FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.src_ch AND verse_number = r.src_v;
    IF sid IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: source verse matthew %:% missing', r.src_ch, r.src_v; END IF;
    IF ltext <> r.src_text THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: live text of matthew %:% differs from the read-back this migration was written against — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.src_ch, r.src_v; END IF;
    SELECT verse_id INTO tid FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.tgt_slug AND chapter_number = r.tgt_ch AND verse_number = r.tgt_v;
    IF tid IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: target verse % %:% missing', r.tgt_slug, r.tgt_ch, r.tgt_v; END IF;
    SELECT * INTO cur FROM cross_references WHERE id = r.id;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % is gone', r.id; END IF;
    IF cur.source_verse_id <> sid OR cur.target_verse_id <> tid THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % no longer sits on matthew %:% -> % %:%', r.id, r.src_ch, r.src_v, r.tgt_slug, r.tgt_ch, r.tgt_v; END IF;
    IF cur.note IS DISTINCT FROM r.old_note AND cur.note IS DISTINCT FROM r.new_note THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: the live note of card % differs from the read-back this migration was written against — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.id; END IF;
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.id = mm.thread_id
                WHERE mm.cross_reference_id = r.id AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b3_m m WHERE m.card_id = r.id AND m.thread_slug = th.slug)) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % already sits in a thread this migration does not put it in — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.id; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b3_t LOOP
    IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.a_vn)
    OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.z_vn) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: anchor %:%-% for new thread % missing', r.ch, r.a_vn, r.z_vn, r.slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b3_a LOOP
    SELECT verse_id INTO av FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.book AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.a_vn;
    SELECT verse_id INTO zv FROM _s496_b3_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.book AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.z_vn;
    IF av IS NULL OR zv IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: anchor verse % %:%-% for thread % missing', r.book, r.ch, r.a_vn, r.z_vn, r.slug; END IF;
    SELECT * INTO cur FROM cross_reference_threads WHERE slug = r.slug;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread to anchor % does not exist', r.slug; END IF;
    IF cur.anchor_verse_id_start IS NOT NULL AND (cur.anchor_verse_id_start <> av OR cur.anchor_verse_id_end IS DISTINCT FROM zv) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread % already carries a different anchor — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT DISTINCT thread_slug FROM _s496_b3_m LOOP
    IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_threads WHERE slug = r.thread_slug) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b3_t WHERE slug = r.thread_slug) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread to join % does not exist', r.thread_slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;

  -- ---- notes rewritten in place ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b3_c;
  UPDATE cross_references x SET note = c.new_note FROM _s496_b3_c c WHERE x.id = c.id AND x.note IS DISTINCT FROM c.new_note;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_note = ROW_COUNT; n_note_same := n_total - n_note;

  -- ---- new threads ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b3_t;
  SELECT t.slug, t.title, t.summary_md, sv.verse_id, ev.verse_id, t.tier::content_tier, t.sort_order
    FROM _s496_b3_t t
    JOIN _s496_b3_lookup sv ON sv.book_slug='matthew' AND sv.chapter_number=t.ch AND sv.verse_number=t.a_vn
    JOIN _s496_b3_lookup ev ON ev.book_slug='matthew' AND ev.chapter_number=t.ch AND ev.verse_number=t.z_vn
  ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_thr = ROW_COUNT; n_thr_same := n_total - n_thr;

  -- ---- anchors for the unanchored threads these cards join ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b3_a;
  UPDATE cross_reference_threads th SET anchor_verse_id_start = sv.verse_id, anchor_verse_id_end = ev.verse_id
    FROM _s496_b3_a a
    JOIN _s496_b3_lookup sv ON sv.book_slug=a.book AND sv.chapter_number=a.ch AND sv.verse_number=a.a_vn
    JOIN _s496_b3_lookup ev ON ev.book_slug=a.book AND ev.chapter_number=a.ch AND ev.verse_number=a.z_vn
   WHERE th.slug = a.slug AND th.anchor_verse_id_start IS NULL;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_anc = ROW_COUNT; n_anc_same := n_total - n_anc;

  -- ---- members: each card into its thread, after the thread's live maximum, in Matthew verse order ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b3_m;
  SELECT th.id, m.card_id,
         COALESCE((SELECT max(mm.sort_order) FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm WHERE mm.thread_id = th.id), 0)
           + row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY th.id ORDER BY m.ord, m.card_id),
         m.member_note
    FROM _s496_b3_m m JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.slug = m.thread_slug
   WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm WHERE mm.thread_id = th.id AND mm.cross_reference_id = m.card_id)
  ON CONFLICT (thread_id, cross_reference_id) DO NOTHING;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_mem = ROW_COUNT; n_mem_same := n_total - n_mem;

  -- ---- proof before commit: every card carries its new note and sits in its thread ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b3_c c JOIN cross_references x ON x.id = c.id WHERE x.note IS DISTINCT FROM c.new_note;
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % card(s) do not carry the new note', n; END IF;
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b3_m m WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.id = mm.thread_id AND th.slug = m.thread_slug WHERE mm.cross_reference_id = m.card_id);
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % member row(s) did not land', n; END IF;
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b3_a a JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.slug = a.slug WHERE th.anchor_verse_id_start IS NULL;
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % thread(s) still unanchored', n; END IF;

  RAISE NOTICE 'S496 (matthew b3) summary: notes rewritten % / already there %; threads inserted % / already there %; anchors set % / already there %; members inserted % / already there %',
    n_note, n_note_same, n_thr, n_thr_same, n_anc, n_anc_same, n_mem, n_mem_same;
END $s496$;
COMMIT;
\echo 'session496 — Matthew 14–19 rebuild done.'
