INSERT INTO _s496_b3_c VALUES
(2832,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'1-kings',21,17,$n$Eliyahu (Elijah) confronting Achav (Ahab) over the Navot (Naboth)-vineyard injustice — the Tanakh-pattern of the prophet's-confrontation of the unrighteous-ruler.$n$,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) Herod bound John for telling him the truth about his brother's wife, and the old story is the same. After Naboth's vineyard was taken, the word of Yahuah (the LORD) *came to Elijah the Tishbite* and sent him down to the king. John stands where Elijah stood, sent to a throne that had taken what was not its own.$n$),
(2833,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'1-kings',19,1,$n$Yezevel's (Jezebel's) death-threat against Eliyahu (Elijah); the Yezevel-pattern at the queen-orchestration-of-killing level.$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:1 — *And Ahab told Jezebel all that Elijah had done, and withal how he had slain all the prophets with the sword.* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) Ahab runs to his wife with the report of what Elijah had done, and the queen answers with a threat on the prophet's life. In Herod's house John is bound *for Herodias’ sake*, so the one who rebukes the king is again met by a woman at the king's side.$n$),
(2834,14,5,$n$And when he would have put him to death, he feared the multitude, because they counted him as a prophet.$n$,'matthew',21,26,$n$*They feared the multitude, because they counted him as a prophet* — the same fear-substance the Pharisaic-system holds.$n$,$n$Matthew 21:26 — *But if we shall say, Of men; we fear the people; for all hold John as a prophet.* *And when he would have put him to death, he feared the multitude, because they counted him as a prophet.* (Matthew 14:5) Herod wanted John dead and held back because the crowd *counted him as a prophet*. The men answering Yahusha (Jesus) here hold back for the same reason, since *all hold John as a prophet*. The people knew what their rulers would not say out loud.$n$),
(2835,14,1,$n$At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),$n$,'matthew',11,14,$n$*This is Eliyahu (Elijah), which was for to come* — the identification completed at the martyrdom.$n$,$n$Matthew 11:14 — *And if ye will receive it, this is Elijah, which was for to come.* *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* (Matthew 14:1) Herod hears the fame of Yahusha (Jesus) and takes him for John risen. Yahusha (Jesus) had already said who John was to those willing to hear it, *this is Elijah, which was for to come*. Herod's guess and the Master's word about John stand side by side.$n$),
(2836,14,1,$n$At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),$n$,'matthew',17,12,$n$The disciples' understanding *the Son of Adam shall also suffer of them* — the Eliyahu-pattern foreshadows the King's-own-suffering.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:12 — *But I say unto you, That Elijah is come already, and they knew him not, but have done unto him whatsoever they listed. Likewise shall also the Son of Adam suffer of them.* *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* (Matthew 14:1) John was done to as the rulers pleased, and Yahusha (Jesus) says so, *whatsoever they listed*. He then ties it to himself, *Likewise shall also the Son of Adam suffer of them*. What Herod did to John is the road ahead of the one Herod has just heard about.$n$),
(2837,14,1,$n$At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),$n$,'2-chronicles',24,20,$n$The killing of Zecharyahu (Zechariah) ben Yehoyada at the temple-court — the broader Tanakh-prophet-killing pattern.$n$,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *And the Spirit of Elohim (God) came upon Zechariah the son of Jehoiada the priest, which stood above the people, and said unto them, Thus saith Elohim (God), Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper? because ye have forsaken Yahuah (LORD), he hath also forsaken you.* *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* (Matthew 14:1) Zechariah spoke because the Spirit of Elohim (God) came upon him, and what he said was a charge about the commandments of Yahuah (the LORD). The people he faced stoned him in the court. John spoke the same kind of word to Herod about what was lawful, and Herod's hearing of Yahusha (Jesus) comes from a house that has done this before.$n$),
(2838,14,1,$n$At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),$n$,'matthew',23,34,$n$The King's-summary of the broader-Tanakh-prophet-killing-pattern.$n$,$n$Matthew 23:34 — *Wherefore, behold, I send unto you prophets, and wise men, and scribes: and some of them ye shall kill and crucify; and some of them shall ye scourge in your synagogues, and persecute them from city to city:* *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* (Matthew 14:1) Herod hears of Yahusha (Jesus) with John's blood fresh in his house. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the teachers before him that the sending of prophets goes on and that *some of them ye shall kill and crucify*. John was one killed, and he says plainly that more are coming.$n$),
(2839,14,1,$n$At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),$n$,'revelation',11,7,$n$The eschatological-two-witnesses killed — the Eliyahu-pattern's-second-fulfillment at the second-coming preparation.$n$,$n$Revelation 11:7 — *And when they shall have finished their testimony, the beast that ascendeth out of the bottomless pit shall make war against them, and shall overcome them, and kill them.* *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* (Matthew 14:1) John finished what he was sent to say to Herod and was killed for it. In Revelation the witnesses are overcome *when they shall have finished their testimony*, and again the ones who speak the word to the end are the ones put to death. Herod's act is not the last of its kind.$n$),
(2840,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'mark',6,14,$n$The Markan parallel with expanded-detail of Herod's-conflicted-attention to Yochanan (John).$n$,$n$Mark 6:14 — *And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) Matthew tells why John was bound, and Mark tells what it did to Herod. When the fame of Yahusha (Jesus) reached him, he said that John *was risen from the dead*. A king who jailed the prophet for a woman's sake could not hear of power without thinking of the man he had silenced.$n$),
(2841,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'exodus',16,14,$n$The wilderness-manna; the King's-recapitulation-and-exceeding.$n$,$n$Exodus 16:14 — *And when the dew that lay was gone up, behold, upon the face of the wilderness there lay a small round thing, as small as the hoar frost on the ground.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) When Yahusha (Jesus) goes out to a desert place and the people follow on foot, they are in a wilderness where bread was given before. In Exodus it lay *upon the face of the wilderness* after the dew went up, a gift from Yahuah (the LORD) to people who had nothing to eat in that place.$n$),
(2842,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'numbers',11,7,$n$The manna-substance extended in the wilderness journey.$n$,$n$Numbers 11:7 — *And the manna was as coriander seed, and the colour thereof as the colour of bdellium.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) A people with nothing to eat in a desert is not new. The manna that kept Yashar'el (Israel) alive *was as coriander seed*, plain food given day after day by Yahuah (the LORD). Here a crowd follows Yahusha (Jesus) out of the cities into a desert place and he feeds them.$n$),
(2843,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'2-kings',4,42,$n$Elisha's-feeding-of-the-hundred from twenty barley-loaves — the King-exceeds.$n$,$n$2 Kings 4:42 — *And there came a man from Baal-shalisha, and brought the man of Elohim (God) bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley, and full ears of corn in the husk thereof. And he said, Give unto the people, that they may eat.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) Elisha was handed twenty barley loaves and told, *Give unto the people, that they may eat*, and the bread proved more than it looked. Yahusha (Jesus) is handed five loaves and two fishes in a desert place and feeds a far larger crowd, so the greater prophet stands where the lesser once stood.$n$),
(2844,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'isaiah',25,6,$n$The Messianic-banquet on the mountain-of-Yahuah (the LORD); the eschatological-destination pre-figured.$n$,$n$Isaiah 25:6 — *And in this mountain shall Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) Isaiah names a day when Yahuah (the LORD) makes *a feast of fat things* on his mountain. Here the hungry sit down on the grass in a desert place and eat until they are filled, and the table in the wilderness shows the kind of host Isaiah was speaking of.$n$),
(2845,14,19,$n$And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.$n$,'matthew',26,26,$n$The Last Supper's-repetition of the same pattern.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:26 — *And as they were eating, Yahusha (Jesus) took bread, and blessed it, and brake it, and gave it to the disciples, and said, Take, eat; this is my body.* *And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 14:19) The same hands do the same acts in both. He *took* the bread, *blessed* it, *brake* it and gave it to the disciples, on the grass with a great crowd and then at the table with the twelve. The disciples are the ones who carry what he breaks out to the people.$n$),
(2846,14,20,$n$And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full.$n$,'matthew',19,28,$n$The twelve thrones at the regeneration — the twelve-tribes-architecture.$n$,$n$Matthew 19:28 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full.* (Matthew 14:20) Twelve baskets of fragments are taken up once the crowd is filled, and later he tells his followers they will sit upon *twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel)*. The number is the number of the tribes, and the flock he feeds in the desert is the flock those tribes make up.$n$),
(2847,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'john',6,1,$n$The Yochanan (John)-account with the Pesach (Passover)-timing and the Bread-of-Life discourse following.$n$,$n$John 6:1 — *After these things Yahusha (Jesus) went over the sea of Galilee, which is the sea of Tiberias.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) John sets the same crossing to a desert place beside a feast, *And the passover, a feast of the Yahudim (Jews), was nigh.* (John 6:4) The bread in the wilderness is given in the season when Yashar'el (Israel) remembers being brought out of Egypt.$n$),
(2848,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'john',6,31,$n$The Yochanan (John)-account with the Pesach (Passover)-timing and the Bread-of-Life discourse following.$n$,$n$John 6:31 — *Our fathers did eat manna in the desert; as it is written, He gave them bread from heaven to eat.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) John records the crowd themselves tying the bread to the old gift, *Our fathers did eat manna in the desert*. Matthew's desert place and the feeding fit those words, because the people knew whose bread this recalled.$n$),
(2849,14,14,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) went forth, and saw a great multitude, and was moved with compassion toward them, and he healed their sick.$n$,'ezekiel',34,11,$n$The shepherd-compassion-substance of Yahuah's (the LORD's)-own-shepherding-of-the-scattered-flock.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) went forth, and saw a great multitude, and was moved with compassion toward them, and he healed their sick.* (Matthew 14:14) Yahusha (Jesus) sees the great multitude and is *moved with compassion toward them*, and heals their sick. Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) says in Ezekiel that he will *search my sheep, and seek them out*, and here the shepherd has gone out to them himself.$n$),
(2850,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'matthew',15,32,$n$The second-feeding (the four-thousand) at Matt 15; the broader-pattern of wilderness-feeding-in-the-Galilee.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:32 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) called his disciples unto him, and said, I have compassion on the multitude, because they continue with me now three days, and have nothing to eat: and I will not send them away fasting, lest they faint in the way.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) Yahusha (Jesus) goes out to a desert place and the crowd follows, and later he says *I have compassion on the multitude* and will not send them away fasting. The shepherd feeds a crowd in the wilderness twice, and the second time his own words give the reason.$n$),
(2853,14,25,$n$And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.$n$,'habakkuk',3,15,$n$*Thou didst walk through the sea with thine horses.*$n$,$n$Habakkuk 3:15 — *Thou didst walk through the sea with thine horses, through the heap of great waters.* *And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.* (Matthew 14:25) Habakkuk says to Yahuah (the LORD), *Thou didst walk through the sea*, and in the fourth watch Yahusha (Jesus) is walking on it. The one the prophet spoke of as walking the waters is the one the disciples see coming toward them in the flesh.$n$),
(2855,14,27,$n$But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.$n$,'john',8,58,$n$*Before Avraham (Abraham) was, I am.*$n$,$n$John 8:58 — *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Before Abraham was, I am.* *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27) *It is I* is his answer to the frightened men in the boat. In John he says *Before Abraham was, I am*. The one who spoke with Abraham is the one speaking across the water, the Word who walked with the fathers and has come in the flesh.$n$),
(2856,14,27,$n$But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.$n$,'john',6,20,$n$The Yochanan (John)-parallel of the same scene.$n$,$n$John 6:20 — *But he saith unto them, It is I; be not afraid.* *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27) John carries the very words Matthew has, spoken to men afraid of what they saw on the water, *It is I; be not afraid*. They had cried out that it was a spirit, and what met their fear was not an argument but his voice telling them who was there.$n$),
(2857,14,33,$n$Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth you are the Son of Elohim (God).$n$,'matthew',16,16,$n$The Caesarea-Philippi confession the preliminary-confession here pre-figures.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:16 — *And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* *Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth you are the Son of Elohim (God).* (Matthew 14:33) In the boat they worshipped and said *thou art the Son of Elohim (God)*. Later Peter, asked, says *the Son of the living Elohim (God)*, and adds that he is the Messiah (Christ). The boat said it in awe, and Peter says it plainly.$n$),
(2858,14,22,$n$And straightway Yahusha (Jesus) constrained his disciples to get into a ship, and to go before him unto the other side, while he sent the multitudes away.$n$,'matthew',8,23,$n$The first-stilling-of-the-storm; the disciples' progression from amazement-at-the-storm-stilled to confession-of-the-Son-of-Elohim.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:23 — *And when he was entered into a ship, his disciples followed him.* *And straightway Yahusha (Jesus) constrained his disciples to get into a ship, and to go before him unto the other side, while he sent the multitudes away.* (Matthew 14:22) At the first storm the disciples go into a ship because they *followed him*, with the Master aboard. Here he constrains them to go before him and stays on the mountain while the wind is against them. In both boats they learn who it is they follow, and here the end of it is worship.$n$),
(3494,14,2,$n$And said unto his servants, This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead; and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.$n$,'matthew',16,14,$n$The broader-multitude's speculations of the King as Yochanan (John) or one of the prophets.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:14 — *And they said, Some say that thou art John the Baptist: some, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.* *And said unto his servants, This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead; and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* (Matthew 14:2) Herod says *This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead*, and the disciples later report that the people say the same, *Some say that thou art John the Baptist*. Herod's guess was the street's guess. They name John, Elijah or a prophet, and not the one in front of them.$n$),
(3495,14,2,$n$And said unto his servants, This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead; and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.$n$,'mark',6,16,$n$The Markan parallel of Herod's-superstitious-recognition.$n$,$n$Mark 6:16 — *But when Herod heard thereof, he said, It is John, whom I beheaded: he is risen from the dead.* *And said unto his servants, This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead; and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* (Matthew 14:2) Herod says the same thing in both tellings, that John is risen from the dead, and Mark adds the words that give him away, *whom I beheaded*. He does not speak as a man who thinks the matter is closed.$n$),
(3496,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'leviticus',18,16,$n$*Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother's wife.*$n$,$n$Leviticus 18:16 — *Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother’s wife: it is thy brother’s nakedness.* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) Herod had taken *his brother Philip’s wife*, and the commandment names exactly that, *thy brother’s wife*. When John told him it was not lawful he was not giving an opinion, he was reading Herod what Yahuah (the LORD) had given through Moses.$n$),
(3497,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'leviticus',20,21,$n$*If a man shall take his brother's wife, it is an unclean thing.*$n$,$n$Leviticus 20:21 — *And if a man shall take his brother’s wife, it is an unclean thing: he hath uncovered his brother’s nakedness; they shall be childless.* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) The second place the Torah speaks of it carries the penalty, that such a man *hath uncovered his brother’s nakedness*, and *they shall be childless*. Herod's marriage to Philip's wife is a thing the commandment calls unclean, and John would call it nothing else.$n$),
(3498,14,3,$n$For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.$n$,'1-kings',21,5,$n$The Yezevel-pattern of orchestrating-the-killing through false-witnesses.$n$,$n$1 Kings 21:5 — *But Jezebel his wife came to him, and said unto him, Why is thy spirit so sad, that thou eatest no bread?* *For Herod had laid hold on John, and bound him, and put him in prison for Herodias’ sake, his brother Philip’s wife.* (Matthew 14:3) Ahab sulks over a vineyard he cannot have, and his wife comes in to ask what is wrong, and then takes the matter into her own hands and writes the letters herself. In Herod's house John is bound *for Herodias’ sake*, and the king's own woman is the one who pushes the matter on.$n$),
(3499,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'matthew',14,23,$n$The same-evening's-mountain-prayer.$n$,$n$Matthew 14:23 — *And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) Twice in one chapter he goes apart. When John is dead he goes by ship into *a desert place apart*, and when the crowds have been fed and sent away he climbs the mountain *apart to pray* and is *there alone*.$n$),
(3500,14,13,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.$n$,'mark',6,31,$n$The Markan-detail of *come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while.*$n$,$n$Mark 6:31 — *And he said unto them, Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while: for there were many coming and going, and they had no leisure so much as to eat.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* (Matthew 14:13) Matthew says only that he went to a desert place apart. Mark gives his words to the disciples, *Come ye yourselves apart*, because they had no leisure even to eat. The withdrawing was for their sake too.$n$),
(3501,14,19,$n$And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.$n$,'luke',24,30,$n$The Emmaus-pattern repetition of the King's-recognized-at-the-bread-blessing.$n$,$n$Luke 24:30 — *And it came to pass, as he sat at meat with them, he took bread, and blessed it, and brake, and gave to them.* *And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 14:19) The same acts come again at Emmaus. At the table he *took bread, and blessed it, and brake*, and gave to them, and in Luke it is then that their eyes are opened and they know him. What the five thousand saw done on the grass, those two see done at a table.$n$),
(3502,14,20,$n$And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full.$n$,'revelation',21,14,$n$The twelve foundations of the wall of the new Yerushalayim (Jerusalem).$n$,$n$Revelation 21:14 — *And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.* *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full.* (Matthew 14:20) Twelve baskets are taken up after the crowd is filled, and the wall of the city stands on *twelve foundations* with the names of the twelve apostles. The twelve who carried the bread to the people are the twelve whose names are set in the wall, beside the gates with the names of the tribes.$n$),
(3503,14,23,$n$And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.$n$,'matthew',5,1,$n$The mountain-ascent for the Sermon.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:1 — *And seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain: and when he was set, his disciples came unto him:* *And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* (Matthew 14:23) Here he goes up *into a mountain* alone to pray. At the opening of the Sermon he climbs one too, with the disciples coming to him to hear. The mountain is where he prays to the Father and where he teaches the Father's commandments.$n$),
(3504,14,23,$n$And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.$n$,'matthew',17,1,$n$The mountain of the transfiguration.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* *And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* (Matthew 14:23) He goes up *into a mountain apart* to pray with no one, and later he takes three of the disciples up an high mountain apart, where Moses and Elijah appear talking with him. In both he leaves the crowd behind to be with the Father.$n$),
(3505,14,23,$n$And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.$n$,'matthew',26,36,$n$The Garden-of-Gethsemane prayer.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:36 — *Then cometh Yahusha (Jesus) with them unto a place called Gethsemane, and saith unto the disciples, Sit ye here, while I go and pray yonder.* *And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* (Matthew 14:23) Alone on the mountain he prays until evening, and at Gethsemane he leaves the disciples sitting and goes to *pray yonder*. On the night he is taken he does what he did here, he withdraws from the ones with him to pray to the Father.$n$),
(3506,14,23,$n$And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.$n$,'matthew',28,16,$n$The mountain of the commissioning.$n$,$n$Matthew 28:16 — *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* *And when he had sent the multitudes away, he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* (Matthew 14:23) He prays alone on a mountain here, and the book closes on a mountain too, the one in Galilee *where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them*. What he began apart at evening, with the crowds sent away, he finishes by gathering the eleven on a hill and sending them out.$n$),
(3507,14,27,$n$But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.$n$,'isaiah',41,10,$n$*Fear not* and *I will help thee* — the Tanakh-substance of Yahuah-encouragement.$n$,$n$Isaiah 41:10 — *Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy Elohim (God): I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness.* *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27) *Be not afraid* in the boat is what Elohim (God) says to his own people in Isaiah, *Fear thou not; for I am with thee*. He gives the reason before the command, and the reason is who is speaking, *I am thy Elohim (God)*.$n$),
(3508,14,27,$n$But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.$n$,'isaiah',41,13,$n$*Fear not* and *I will help thee* — the Tanakh-substance of Yahuah-encouragement.$n$,$n$Isaiah 41:13 — *For I Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) will hold thy right hand, saying unto thee, Fear not; I will help thee.* *But straightway Yahusha (Jesus) spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.* (Matthew 14:27) Isaiah gives the same comfort with a hand attached, *will hold thy right hand*, saying *Fear not*, and promising help. In the boat the words come over the water first, and the hand comes after.$n$),
(3509,14,33,$n$Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth you are the Son of Elohim (God).$n$,'psalms',2,7,$n$*Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee* — the Davidic-Messianic-Son-of-Elohim substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 2:7 — *I will declare the decree: Yahuah (LORD) hath said unto me, Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee.* *Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth you are the Son of Elohim (God).* (Matthew 14:33) In the boat they say *thou art the Son of Elohim (God)*, and in the psalm Yahuah (the LORD) says it first, *Thou art my Son*. What the men on the water spoke had already been declared.$n$),
(3510,14,36,$n$And besought him that they might only touch the hem of his garment: and as many as touched were made perfectly whole.$n$,'matthew',9,20,$n$The hemorrhaging-woman touching-the-hem.$n$,$n$Matthew 9:20 — *And, behold, a woman, which was diseased with an issue of blood twelve years, came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment:* *And besought him that they might only touch the hem of his garment: and as many as touched were made perfectly whole.* (Matthew 14:36) The woman with the issue of blood came behind him and touched *the hem of his garment*, and here the whole country asks to *only touch the hem of his garment*. What one woman reached for in secret, the region now asks for in the open.$n$),
(5521,14,25,$n$And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.$n$,'psalms',77,20,$n$thread:walking-on-the-sea-formed-cloud-rider | *Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Mosheh (Moses) and Aharon (Aaron).* The right-hand-of-Mosheh echoing Isaiah 63:12 — completes the Psalm 77:19-20 Tanakh witness to the walking-on-the-sea thread.$n$,$n$Psalm 77:20 — *Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.* *And in the fourth watch of the night Yahusha (Jesus) went unto them, walking on the sea.* (Matthew 14:25) The psalm that speaks of a way through the sea goes on, *Thou leddest thy people like a flock*, by the hand of Moses and Aaron. In the fourth watch Yahusha (Jesus) comes to his own over the water, the shepherd crossing the sea he once led them through.$n$),
(2859,15,8,$n$This people draws nigh unto me with their mouth, and honoureth me with their lips; but their heart is far from me.$n$,'isaiah',29,13,$n$Direct Tanakh-source.$n$,$n$Isaiah 29:13 — *Wherefore Yahuah (Lord) said, Forasmuch as this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me, but have removed their heart far from me, and their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men:* *This people draws nigh unto me with their mouth, and honoureth me with their lips; but their heart is far from me.* (Matthew 15:8) What Yahusha (Jesus) says Isaiah prophesied is Isaiah's own line, and the picture is the same: the mouth and the lips near, the heart far off. Isaiah goes one step further than Matthew, saying their fear of Yahuah (the LORD) was *taught by the precept of men*, which is the tradition laid over the commandment.$n$),
(2860,15,3,$n$But he answered and said unto them, Why do you also transgress the commandment of Elohim (God) by your tradition?$n$,'exodus',20,12,$n$The fifth-commandment the Corban-tradition violates.$n$,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* *But he answered and said unto them, Why do you also transgress the commandment of Elohim (God) by your tradition?* (Matthew 15:3) This is the commandment Yahusha (Jesus) sets down in front of them, the fifth of the ten, and it carries a promise of long days in the land. The rule of Corban let a man call his goods a gift and so keep them back from his father and mother, which is how a tradition comes to stand where the commandment stood.$n$),
(2862,15,6,$n$And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.$n$,'mark',7,9,$n$The Markan parallel with explicit *Corban*.$n$,$n$Mark 7:9 — *And he said unto them, Full well ye reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that ye may keep your own tradition.* *And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.* (Matthew 15:6) Mark carries the same charge and keeps the word Matthew only translates: *It is Corban, that is to say, a gift* (Mark 7:11). In the verse after this one he lets Moses speak the commandment itself, *Honour thy father and thy mother* (Mark 7:10), so the two sides are plain: the word of Elohim (God) on one hand, their own tradition kept at its cost on the other.$n$),
(2863,15,6,$n$And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.$n$,'deuteronomy',4,2,$n$The Torah's-prohibition against adding-or-diminishing.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 4:2 — *Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it, that ye may keep the commandments of Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) which I command you.* *And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.* (Matthew 15:6) Moses tells the people not to add to the word or take anything from it, and the elders had done both at once. A washing rule was piled on top, and the honour owed a father and mother was taken away by Corban. The charge in Matthew is not that they were strict but that they handled the commandment as if it could be changed.$n$),
(2864,15,6,$n$And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.$n$,'deuteronomy',12,32,$n$The same prohibition restated.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 12:32 — *What thing soever I command you, observe to do it: thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it.* *And honour not his father or his mother, he shall be free. Thus have you made the commandment of Elohim (God) of none effect by your tradition.* (Matthew 15:6) Moses says it a second time, and here it follows his warning not to ask *How did these nations serve their gods?* (Deuteronomy 12:30). Adding to what Yahuah (the LORD) commanded and borrowing someone else's way are set side by side, which is the same fault Yahusha (Jesus) finds in men's commandments taught as if they were doctrine.$n$),
(2865,15,9,$n$But in vain they do worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men.$n$,'daniel',7,25,$n$*Think to change times and laws* — the antichrist-system's-substance at the eschatological-level; the same architecture the Pharisaic-system embodies in its-first-century-costume.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:25 — *And he shall speak great words against the El Elyon (most High), and shall wear out the saints of the El Elyon (most High), and think to change times and laws: and they shall be given into his hand until a time and times and the dividing of time.* *But in vain they do worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men.* (Matthew 15:9) Yahusha (Jesus) says they teach the commandments of men as if they were doctrine, and Daniel sees the same habit grown to full size, a power that thinks *to change times and laws*. The Pharisees did it with a rule here and a rule there, and the people under that teaching were handed it by those before them.$n$),
(2866,15,9,$n$But in vain they do worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men.$n$,'1-john',2,3,$n$The contrary-to-Torah test as the master-filter (per the voice skill's 1234 of Truth) — the same diagnostic applied to all five Judaizer-costumes.$n$,$n$1 John 2:3 — *And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments.* *But in vain they do worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men.* (Matthew 15:9) Yahusha (Jesus) says they teach men's commandments as doctrine, and John gives the plain test for any teaching: whether it stands with his commandments or sets others in their place. The next verse says of the man who claims to know him and does not keep them, *the truth is not in him* (1 John 2:4).$n$),
(2867,15,1,$n$Then came to Yahusha (Jesus) scribes and Pharisees, which were of Jerusalem, saying,$n$,'matthew',23,16,$n$The King's-later-Matt 23-extended-rebuke of the Pharisaic-tradition's-vow-architecture (the swearing-by-the-temple vs swearing-by-the-gold-of-the-temple pattern) — the same architecture in a different-Pharisaic-tradition.$n$,$n$Matthew 23:16 — *Woe unto you, ye blind guides, which say, Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor!* *Then came to Yahusha (Jesus) scribes and Pharisees, which were of Jerusalem, saying,* (Matthew 15:1) The scribes and Pharisees come from Jerusalem with their question, and later in this gospel he names the kind of teaching they speak for: blind guides whose oath rules set the gold above the temple that makes it holy. It is the same ranking Corban made, a gift put above father and mother, and he asks of it, *whether is greater, the gift, or the altar that sanctifieth the gift?* (Matthew 23:19)$n$),
(2868,15,1,$n$Then came to Yahusha (Jesus) scribes and Pharisees, which were of Jerusalem, saying,$n$,'galatians',1,14,$n$Paul's-pre-conversion description of himself as *exceedingly zealous of the traditions of my fathers* — the same Pharisaic-traditions-architecture the King confronts here.$n$,$n$Galatians 1:14 — *And profited in the Yahudim's (Jews’) religion above many my equals in mine own nation, being more exceedingly zealous of the traditions of my fathers.* *Then came to Yahusha (Jesus) scribes and Pharisees, which were of Jerusalem, saying,* (Matthew 15:1) The men at the door of this chapter belong to the school Paul came out of, and he says what he was in it: *exceedingly zealous of the traditions of my fathers*. He writes of it without scorn, as a man who stood where they stand and knows from the inside how a tradition passes for the commandment.$n$),
(3513,15,8,$n$This people draws nigh unto me with their mouth, and honoureth me with their lips; but their heart is far from me.$n$,'colossians',2,8,$n$*Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men* — Paul's-apostolic-restatement.$n$,$n$Colossians 2:8 — *Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Messiah (Christ).* *This people draws nigh unto me with their mouth, and honoureth me with their lips; but their heart is far from me.* (Matthew 15:8) Paul gives the same warning in his own words, setting what is taught *after the tradition of men* against what is taught after Messiah (Christ). It is the line Isaiah drew and Yahusha (Jesus) repeats here, and Paul puts it beside philosophy and empty deceit, not beside the commandments of Elohim (God).$n$),
(2879,15,11,$n$Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.$n$,'mark',7,14,$n$The Markan parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 7:14 — *And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:* *Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.* (Matthew 15:11) Mark sets the call to the crowd right after the charge that the word of Elohim (God) was made *of none effect through your tradition* (Mark 7:13). So the saying about what defiles grows out of the argument over tradition, and Yahusha (Jesus) turns from the elders to the people to say it, asking every one of them to hear and understand.$n$),
(2880,15,18,$n$But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.$n$,'genesis',6,5,$n$*Every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually* — the Tanakh-anchor for the heart-substance.$n$,$n$Genesis 6:5 — *And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.* *But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.* (Matthew 15:18) Yahusha (Jesus) traces what defiles back to the heart, and Genesis shows that heart long before any washing rule, when *every imagination of the thoughts of his heart* was only evil continually. The mouth only lets out what is already held there, which is why a rinse of the hands cannot reach it.$n$),
(2881,15,18,$n$But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.$n$,'jeremiah',17,9,$n$*The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked* — the heart-substance the King restores.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* *But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.* (Matthew 15:18) Jeremiah says of the heart what Matthew says, that the trouble is inside, and then names the one who looks there: *I Yahuah (LORD) search the heart... according to the fruit of his doings* (Jeremiah 17:10). A man is weighed by what his heart brings forth, not by what he ate or whether he rinsed his hands.$n$),
(2882,15,18,$n$But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.$n$,'ezekiel',36,26,$n$The new-heart promise that is the framework's-answer to the heart's-substance.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.* *But those things which proceed out of the mouth come forth from the heart; and they defile the man.* (Matthew 15:18) If the heart is where defilement starts, Ezekiel promises the answer where it has to be made: Yahuah (the LORD) takes away the stony heart and gives a heart of flesh. He goes on to say what that heart does, *cause you to walk in my statutes, and ye shall keep my judgments, and do them* (Ezekiel 36:27).$n$),
(2883,15,11,$n$Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.$n$,'acts',10,14,$n$Kefa's (Peter's) post-resurrection *I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean* — direct evidence the King's-disciples did NOT-read Matt 15 as abolishing the dietary-categories.$n$,$n$Acts 10:14 — *But Peter said, Not so, Yahuah (Lord); for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean.* *Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.* (Matthew 15:11) Peter was the one who asked Yahusha (Jesus) to explain this saying, *Declare unto us this parable* (Matthew 15:15), and he heard it close on unwashen hands. Years afterward, hungry on a roof, he still answers the voice with *I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean*, so the disciple who heard the saying did not take it to open the food laws.$n$),
(3514,15,11,$n$Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.$n$,'acts',10,9,$n$Kefa's (Peter's) vision; the framework reads against the abolition-of-the-dietary-categories reading.$n$,$n$Acts 10:9 — *On the morrow, as they went on their journey, and drew nigh unto the city, Peter went up upon the housetop to pray about the sixth hour:* *Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.* (Matthew 15:11) A hungry man on a roof and a sheet let down is the scene people hang this saying on. Read in its place, Peter is left asking what it could mean, *doubted in himself what this vision which he had seen should mean* (Acts 10:17), while Matthew's saying never left the subject of bread and *unwashen hands* (Matthew 15:20).$n$),
(2884,15,11,$n$Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.$n$,'leviticus',11,1,$n$The Torah's-dietary-substance preserved.$n$,$n$Leviticus 11:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and to Aaron, saying unto them,* *Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.* (Matthew 15:11) The question in Matthew is whether to eat bread with unwashen hands, and the chapter ends on it: *to eat with unwashen hands defileth not a man* (Matthew 15:20). What may be eaten is a different subject with its own place, where Yahuah (the LORD) spoke to Moses and Aaron for the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and the saying about the mouth does not reach back and touch it.$n$),
(2885,15,11,$n$Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.$n$,'deuteronomy',14,1,$n$The Torah's-dietary-substance restated and extended.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 14:1 — *Ye are the children of Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God): ye shall not cut yourselves, nor make any baldness between your eyes for the dead.* *Not that which goes into the mouth defiles a man; but that which comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man.* (Matthew 15:11) Moses sets the law of what may be eaten inside a word about who the people are, *an holy people unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God)* (Deuteronomy 14:2), and then says *Thou shalt not eat any abominable thing* (Deuteronomy 14:3). Matthew's saying is about unwashen hands at bread and leaves that law where it stood.$n$),
(2886,15,13,$n$But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.$n$,'matthew',13,24,$n$The wheat-and-tares parable.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* *But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.* (Matthew 15:13) The plant he speaks of is the one in his own parable of the field, where a man sowed good seed and *his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat* (Matthew 13:25). So what the Father has not planted is what the enemy sowed, and the rooting up is the harvest the parable describes.$n$),
(2887,15,14,$n$Let them alone: they be blind leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both shall fall into the ditch.$n$,'matthew',13,13,$n$The Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 6:9-10 cause-and-effect-of-hearing diagnostic.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:13 — *Therefore speak I to them in parables: because they seeing see not; and hearing they hear not, neither do they understand.* *Let them alone: they be blind leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both shall fall into the ditch.* (Matthew 15:14) He calls them blind leaders of the blind, and in Matthew 13 he says why such blindness stands: *seeing see not; and hearing they hear not*. To his own hearers he says the opposite, *blessed are your eyes, for they see: and your ears, for they hear* (Matthew 13:16), and the leader who cannot see takes those who follow into the ditch with him.$n$),
(3515,15,13,$n$But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.$n$,'matthew',13,36,$n$The interpretation; the Adam-and-Tares architecture.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:36 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) sent the multitude away, and went into the house: and his disciples came unto him, saying, Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field.* *But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.* (Matthew 15:13) When the disciples ask to have the parable declared, he says it plainly: *the good seed are the children of the kingdom; but the tares are the children of the wicked one* (Matthew 13:38), and *the enemy that sowed them is the devil* (Matthew 13:39). That is what a plant the Father did not plant means in his own mouth.$n$),
(3516,15,13,$n$But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.$n$,'isaiah',60,21,$n$*Thy people also shall be all righteous ... the branch of my planting, the work of my hands.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 60:21 — *Thy people also shall be all righteous: they shall inherit the land for ever, the branch of my planting, the work of my hands, that I may be glorified.* *But he answered and said, Every plant, which my heavenly Father has not planted, shall be rooted up.* (Matthew 15:13) If every plant the Father did not plant is rooted up, Isaiah says what is left standing: *the branch of my planting, the work of my hands*. The rooting up is for the sake of the planting, a people all righteous who inherit the land for ever.$n$),
(3517,15,14,$n$Let them alone: they be blind leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both shall fall into the ditch.$n$,'matthew',23,16,$n$The blind-guides language in the chapter-23-woes.$n$,$n$Matthew 23:16 — *Woe unto you, ye blind guides, which say, Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor!* *Let them alone: they be blind leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both shall fall into the ditch.* (Matthew 15:14) He calls them blind leaders here and blind guides there, and in Matthew 23 he says what the guiding was: an oath rule that counted the gold above the temple that makes it holy, so that *the gold, or the temple that sanctifieth the gold* (Matthew 23:17) was turned upside down. It is the system's rule he names, and the people led by it are the ones in danger of the ditch.$n$),
(2871,15,21,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.$n$,'matthew',8,5,$n$The centurion-pericope as parallel test-case — individual-nations-figure with God-fearer-Yashar'el (Israel)-attachment receives-mercy.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:5 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) was entered into Capernaum, there came unto him a centurion, beseeching him,* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* (Matthew 15:21) On the coast of Tyre and Sidon a woman will call out to him, and at Capernaum a centurion has already come, *beseeching him* (Matthew 8:5). Both are far from the assembly in the land and both come asking for someone at home, and both are sheep of the scattered house who heard the Shepherd where they were.$n$),
(2872,15,21,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.$n$,'ruth',1,1,$n$Ruth the Moabite as Tanakh-precedent of individual-nations-figure incorporated through-marriage-into-the-covenant-line (Davidic-ancestry).$n$,$n$Ruth 1:1 — *Now it came to pass in the days when the judges ruled, that there was a famine in the land. And a certain man of Beth-lehem-judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab, he, and his wife, and his two sons.* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* (Matthew 15:21) Yahusha (Jesus) goes out of the land to the coast of Tyre and Sidon, and Ruth begins with a man of Beth-lehem-judah who had gone out of it the same way, *to sojourn in the country of Moab*. His sons took wives of Moab, and Ruth became one with the line by marrying Boaz. That is the marriage road, and it is not the woman's, who is already one of the sheep.$n$),
(2873,15,21,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.$n$,'1-kings',17,8,$n$The widow-of-Tsarephath (Zarephath) as Tanakh-precedent of individual-nations-figure receiving-mercy from-Yahuah-through-Eliyahu.$n$,$n$1 Kings 17:8 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came unto him, saying,* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* (Matthew 15:21) The word of Yahuah (the LORD) sends Elijah to *Zarephath, which belongeth to Zidon* (1 Kings 17:9), the same coast Yahusha (Jesus) enters here. A widow there had already been commanded to sustain him, so the word had reached her before the prophet did, and she was one of his sheep in the place she had been scattered to.$n$),
(2874,15,21,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.$n$,'2-kings',5,1,$n$Naaman the Syrian as Tanakh-precedent of individual-nations-figure receiving-mercy from-Yahuah-through-Elisha.$n$,$n$2 Kings 5:1 — *Now Naaman, captain of the host of the king of Syria, was a great man with his master, and honourable, because by him Yahuah (LORD) had given deliverance unto Syria: he was also a mighty man in valour, but he was a leper.* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* (Matthew 15:21) Naaman is a Syrian commander in a land set against Yashar'el (Israel), yet the Syrians had *brought away captive out of the land of Yashar'el (Israel) a little maid* (2 Kings 5:2), and it is her word that sends him to the prophet. Like the woman on this coast, he is found in the place of the scattering, reached through a daughter of the house.$n$),
(2875,15,21,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.$n$,'luke',4,25,$n$The King's-own-citation of the widow-of-Tsarephath and Naaman precedents at his Natsareth (Nazareth)-sermon — the framework-precedent the King articulates.$n$,$n$Luke 4:25 — *But I tell you of a truth, many widows were in Yashar'el (Israel) in the days of Elijah, when the heaven was shut up three years and six months, when great famine was throughout all the land;* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* (Matthew 15:21) Yahusha (Jesus) names this coast himself at Nazareth: of all the widows in Elijah's day, *unto none of them was Elijah sent, save unto Sarepta, a city of Sidon* (Luke 4:26), and the one leper cleansed was Naaman the Syrian. The synagogue was *filled with wrath* (Luke 4:28), and now he stands on that same coast.$n$),
(2877,15,28,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto her, O woman, great is your faith: be it unto you even as you will. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.$n$,'hebrews',11,31,$n$Rachav's-faith honored — the same individual-faith-honored-substance the Canaanite-woman demonstrates.$n$,$n$Hebrews 11:31 — *By faith the harlot Rahab perished not with them that believed not, when she had received the spies with peace.* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto her, O woman, great is your faith: be it unto you even as you will. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.* (Matthew 15:28) Hebrews honors Rahab's faith, *perished not*, as Yahusha (Jesus) honors this woman's, *great is thy faith*. They are not the same road, though. Rahab was spared at Jericho and then became one with the line by marrying Salmon, a man of Yahudah (Judah), while the woman on this coast is a sheep he was sent to, and he never says she became anything.$n$),
(2878,15,28,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto her, O woman, great is your faith: be it unto you even as you will. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.$n$,'matthew',8,10,$n$*I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel)* — the centurion-pericope's-faith-honoring substance parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto her, O woman, great is your faith: be it unto you even as you will. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.* (Matthew 15:28) What he says to the woman he had already said of the centurion, *so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel)*, and he goes on to say that many will *sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven* (Matthew 8:11). Both are sheep of the scattered house who heard him from afar, and the faith shows it.$n$),
(3520,15,27,$n$And she said, Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.$n$,'matthew',8,10,$n$The centurion-pericope's-faith-honoring-substance parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* *And she said, Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.* (Matthew 15:27) She answers with *Truth*, taking the order of the house as it is, and still asks for what falls from the table. The centurion did the same, *I am not worthy that thou shouldest come under my roof* (Matthew 8:8), and he marvelled at both. Neither argues the order, and both ask from inside it.$n$),
(3521,15,27,$n$And she said, Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.$n$,'ruth',1,1,$n$Ruth the Moabite Tanakh-precedent.$n$,$n$Ruth 1:1 — *Now it came to pass in the days when the judges ruled, that there was a famine in the land. And a certain man of Beth-lehem-judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab, he, and his wife, and his two sons.* *And she said, Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.* (Matthew 15:27) She does not ask to be seated at the table, only for what falls from it, and she agrees the children come first. Ruth's road to that house began where Ruth begins, with a family of Beth-lehem-judah in Moab and a woman of Moab taken as a wife, and it ended with her made one with Boaz by marriage. The woman at Tyre comes by no such door, but as a sheep.$n$),
(3518,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'ezekiel',34,11,$n$*Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out* — the Tanakh-shepherd-substance.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) When he says he is sent to the lost sheep, this is the Shepherd of Ezekiel speaking: *will both search my sheep, and seek them out*. His seeking reaches into *all places where they have been scattered* (Ezekiel 34:12), and here it reaches a coast far from the land.$n$),
(3519,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'jeremiah',50,6,$n$*My people hath been lost sheep* — the Tanakh-anchor.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 50:6 — *My people hath been lost sheep: their shepherds have caused them to go astray, they have turned them away on the mountains: they have gone from mountain to hill, they have forgotten their restingplace.* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) Jeremiah gives the lost sheep their history: *their shepherds have caused them to go astray*, and they have *forgotten their restingplace*. Yahusha (Jesus) names them as the ones he is sent to, and the verses before show what their coming back looks like, *the children of Yashar'el (Israel) shall come, they and the children of Yahudah (Judah) together* (Jeremiah 50:4).$n$),
(2869,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'matthew',10,5,$n$The apostolic-mission-scope statement; this verse confirms-and-reinforces.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:5 — *These twelve Yahusha (Jesus) sent forth, and commanded them, saying, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not:* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) Before he says it to the woman he had said it to the twelve: not the way of the Gentiles and not into a Samaritan city, but *go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Matthew 10:6). It is one boundary, so when he tells her he is sent only to those sheep, he is not making a new rule, and her answer shows she was inside it.$n$),
(2870,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'romans',15,8,$n$Paul's-apostolic-restatement of the King's-mission-scope.$n$,$n$Romans 15:8 — *Now I say that Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) was a minister of the circumcision for the truth of Elohim (God), to confirm the promises made unto the fathers:* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) Paul says it of Yahusha (Jesus) in his own words: he was *a minister of the circumcision* so as *to confirm the promises made unto the fathers*. That is the same boundary as *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*, a sending to the people the promises were given to, and the promises are confirmed, not replaced.$n$),
(2876,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'matthew',28,19,$n$The territorial-extension of the apostolic-mission to the dispersion-territories — NOT the categorical-extension to Three-Categories-#3 nations per Red Line #7.$n$,$n$Matthew 28:19 — *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit):* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) Before the cross he says he is sent only to *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*, and after it the disciples are sent to teach *all nations*. The sending widens in place and not in people, for the sheep were scattered into every nation and the word has to go where they are.$n$),
(177460,15,24,$n$But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'romans',11,25,$n$*I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Matthew 15:24) — said to the woman of Canaan, then her daughter healed. She is a lost sheep wearing the name of the coast she was scattered to, not a stranger let in. The fulness of the Gentiles (Romans 11:25) is exactly this: the lost sheep found in every sphere of the scattering, gathered home.$n$,$n$Romans 11:25 — *For I would not, brethren, that ye should be ignorant of this mystery, lest ye should be wise in your own conceits; that blindness in part is happened to Yashar'el (Israel), until the fulness of the Gentiles be come in.* *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:24) The sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) are scattered, and the woman of Canaan is one of them, called by the name of the coast she was scattered to. What Paul calls *the fulness of the Gentiles* is those same sheep found in every place they were driven and gathered home, not strangers added to the house.$n$),
(3522,15,31,$n$Insomuch that the multitude wondered, when they saw the dumb to speak, the maimed to be whole, the lame to walk, and the blind to see: and they glorified the Elohim (God) of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'matthew',11,5,$n$The Isaiah-35 Messianic-credential answer to Yochanan (John).$n$,$n$Matthew 11:5 — *The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached to them.* *Insomuch that the multitude wondered, when they saw the dumb to speak, the maimed to be whole, the lame to walk, and the blind to see: and they glorified the Elohim (God) of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 15:31) John sent to ask *Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?* (Matthew 11:3), and Yahusha (Jesus) answered with what could be seen and heard. On the mountain by the sea of Galilee the crowd sees those same works for itself and gives its own answer, glorifying the Elohim (God) of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
(3524,15,36,$n$And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.$n$,'matthew',14,19,$n$The first-feeding's-take-bless-break-distribute pattern.$n$,$n$Matthew 14:19 — *And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* *And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 15:36) The hands and the pattern are the same as at the first feeding, but the numbers change: seven loaves here and *seven baskets full* (Matthew 15:37), five loaves there and *twelve baskets full* (Matthew 14:20). And this time he gives his reason in his own words, *I will not send them away fasting* (Matthew 15:32).$n$),
(3525,15,36,$n$And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.$n$,'matthew',26,26,$n$The Last Supper's-pattern.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:26 — *And as they were eating, Yahusha (Jesus) took bread, and blessed it, and brake it, and gave it to the disciples, and said, Take, eat; this is my body.* *And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* (Matthew 15:36) These are the same acts that fed the crowd in the wilderness, done now at the table on the night he is betrayed, with words said over the bread. The meal also looks ahead, *until that day when I drink it new with you in my Father’s kingdom* (Matthew 26:29).$n$),
(2888,16,17,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'matthew',11,25,$n$The Father-Son-revelation-economy.$n$,$n$Matthew 11:25 — *At that time Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Yahuah (Lord) of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 16:17) Peter did not work this out for himself, and Yahusha (Jesus) says so. In the same way he thanks the Father that these things are hidden from *the wise and prudent* and shown to *babes*. Who the Son is gets seen only where the Father opens the eyes.$n$),
(2889,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'deuteronomy',32,4,$n$The Tanakh-substance of the Rock.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 32:4 — *He is the Rock, his work is perfect: for all his ways are judgment: a Elohim (God) of truth and without iniquity, just and right is he.* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) Moses' song names the one who is *the Rock*, and says his way is just and right. A hearer who knew that song would not hear *upon this rock* as a man's name but as the one Peter had just confessed, the Son of the living Elohim (God).$n$),
(2890,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'psalms',18,2,$n$*Yahuah (the LORD) is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer.*$n$,$n$Psalm 18:2 — *Yahuah (LORD) is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer; my Elohim (God), my strength, in whom I will trust; my buckler, and the horn of my salvation, and my high tower.* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) David ran to Yahuah (the LORD) as his *rock*, his fortress and his deliverer. When Yahusha (Jesus) says *upon this rock I will build*, he speaks of the same strong place, the one Peter had just named, and not of Peter himself.$n$),
(2891,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'1-corinthians',10,4,$n$*That Rock was the Messiah.*$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 10:4 — *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ).* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) Paul says the Rock that followed Yashar'el (Israel) through the wilderness was Messiah (Christ). So the Rock Moses sang of and the Rock of Caesarea Philippi are one, the one the fathers were given to drink from, now standing in the flesh before Peter.$n$),
(2892,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'1-peter',2,4,$n$Kefa's (Peter's)-own-identification of the King as the cornerstone.$n$,$n$1 Peter 2:4 — *To whom coming, as unto a living stone, disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of Elohim (God), and precious,* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) Peter himself later writes of *a living stone*, refused by men and chosen of Elohim (God). He does not point at himself. He points at the one he came to, which tells us what he understood Yahusha (Jesus) to mean by the rock.$n$),
(2893,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'ephesians',2,20,$n$The King-as-the-chief-corner-stone.$n$,$n$Ephesians 2:20 — *And are built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) himself being the chief corner stone;* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) The house is built on the apostles and the prophets, with Yahusha (Jesus) as the corner stone that holds it. Because the prophets are in the foundation too, what Yahusha (Jesus) builds is not a new house begun at Caesarea Philippi but the old one.$n$),
(2895,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'psalms',118,22,$n$*The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner* — the rejected-stone-becoming-the-cornerstone substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 118:22 — *The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner.* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) The builders refused a stone that became the head of the corner. Peter has just named that stone, and Yahusha (Jesus) goes on at once to speak of the elders and chief priests and scribes who will cast him off, the builders of this psalm.$n$),
(2896,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'isaiah',38,10,$n$The gates of Sheol Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 38:10 — *I said in the cutting off of my days, I shall go to the gates of the grave: I am deprived of the residue of my years.* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) Hezekiah speaks of the *gates of the grave* as the place where a man is shut in at the end of his days. These are the gates Yahusha (Jesus) means, and he says they will not prevail against what he builds.$n$),
(2897,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'psalms',9,13,$n$The gates of death.$n$,$n$Psalm 9:13 — *Have mercy upon me, O Yahuah (LORD); consider my trouble which I suffer of them that hate me, thou that liftest me up from the gates of death:* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) The psalmist cries to Yahuah (the LORD), who *liftest me up from the gates of death*. The gates are real and they hold the dead, but they are not the last word for the one he lifts.$n$),
(2900,16,19,$n$And I will give unto you the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.$n$,'matthew',18,18,$n$The binding-and-loosing extended to the assembly.$n$,$n$Matthew 18:18 — *Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever ye shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever ye shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* *And I will give unto you the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* (Matthew 16:19) The same words that went to Peter go to all the disciples here, and they come right after the matter of a brother who will not hear and a word that must be settled *in the mouth of two or three witnesses* (Matthew 18:16). The keys are a stewardship of the house, used the way Moses taught a matter to be judged, under the Father and not over him.$n$),
(2901,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'isaiah',53,1,$n$The suffering-servant Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:1 — *Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of Yahuah (LORD) revealed?* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) Isaiah asks to whom the arm of Yahuah (the LORD) is revealed. Peter had just been shown who the Son is by the Father; now the same arm is shown in suffering, and Peter turns from it. The prophet already knew few would believe that report.$n$),
(2902,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'zechariah',13,7,$n$The smitten-shepherd.$n$,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *Awake, O sword, against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts): smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) The one who must suffer is the Shepherd, and in Zechariah it is Yahuah (the LORD) of hosts himself who wakes the sword against him. The sheep are scattered when he is smitten, which is the road Yahusha (Jesus) says he must go.$n$),
(2903,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'zechariah',12,10,$n$*They shall look upon me whom they have pierced* — the Tanakh-anchor for the second-coming-recognition-substance per `_COMMENTARY_SUMMATION.md` §V.$n$,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *And I will pour upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of supplications: and they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son, and shall be in bitterness for him, as one that is in bitterness for his firstborn.* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) Yahusha (Jesus) says he must go to Jerusalem and be killed, and Zechariah names the very place. The one speaking says *look upon me whom they have pierced*, and the house of David and Jerusalem mourn him *as one mourneth for his only son*.$n$),
(2904,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'matthew',17,22,$n$The second-passion-prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:22 — *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) He says it again while they are still in Galilee, and now adds that the Son of Adam will be *betrayed into the hands of men*. The ending is the same, killed and raised the third day, and he does not soften it for them by saying it only once.$n$),
(2905,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'matthew',20,17,$n$The third-passion-prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 20:17 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) going up to Jerusalem took the twelve disciples apart in the way, and said unto them,* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) What he first said at Caesarea Philippi is now being walked. Here he is *going up to Jerusalem* with the twelve taken apart, and for the third time he tells them plainly what waits for him there.$n$),
(2906,16,22,$n$Then Peter took him, and began to rebuke him, saying, Be it far from you, Lord: this shall not be unto you.$n$,'matthew',4,8,$n$The wilderness-temptation-responses' parallel; the same kingdoms-without-the-cross / no-passion architectural-substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* *Then Peter took him, and began to rebuke him, saying, Be it far from you, Lord: this shall not be unto you.* (Matthew 16:22) On the high mountain the devil offered all the kingdoms and their glory with no cross in the way. Peter's *this shall not be unto thee* offers the same thing again through a friend's mouth, and the answer is the same, *Get thee behind me, Satan* (Matthew 16:23).$n$),
(2907,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'galatians',3,13,$n$*The Messiah hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us* — the framework's curse-of-the-law-as-Deuteronomy-28-exile-judgment reading.$n$,$n$Galatians 3:13 — *Messiah (Christ) hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written, Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree:* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) The tree is the way he is killed, and Paul says he was made a curse for us there. That curse is the judgment the covenant itself laid on those who broke it, the scattering. It is not the commandments, which are what he brings the scattered home to.$n$),
(2908,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'romans',6,14,$n$The door-home and the consequence-system-still-operative; the framework's-grace-architecture.$n$,$n$Romans 6:14 — *For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace.* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) Sin has no dominion over the one raised with him, because the road home through his death is open. That is grace as the way back to the commandments. Paul asks the next question himself: *shall we sin, because we are not under the law, but under grace? Elohim (God) forbid.* (Romans 6:15)$n$),
(2909,16,24,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'matthew',10,38,$n$The first-statement of the cross-bearing-discipleship-cost.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:38 — *And he that taketh not his cross, and followeth after me, is not worthy of me.* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Matthew 16:24) He said it first when he sent the twelve out, and it comes back now with Jerusalem and the cross in view. *Take up his cross* is not new to them, but after the passion foretold they can hear where it leads.$n$),
(2910,16,25,$n$For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it.$n$,'john',12,25,$n$*He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal.*$n$,$n$John 12:25 — *He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal.* *For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it.* (Matthew 16:25) Matthew sets it as a trade of one life for another, *lose it* and *find it*. John says which life is given up, the one loved *in this world*, and which is kept, the life that has no end.$n$),
(2911,16,26,$n$For what is a man profited, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?$n$,'matthew',4,8,$n$The kingdoms-of-the-world wilderness-temptation.$n$,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* *For what is a man profited, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?* (Matthew 16:26) In the wilderness the devil offered *the whole world* and the glory of it, and Yahusha (Jesus) refused it. That is why he can ask what a man profits who gains the whole world and loses his own soul.$n$),
(2912,16,27,$n$For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.$n$,'matthew',25,31,$n$*When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31). He comes *in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works* (Matthew 16:27). The throne of his glory is where the works are weighed: the gathered seed sifted, the sheep from the goats.$n$,$n$Matthew 25:31 — *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27) The same Son of Adam comes with the angels, and Matthew 25 shows where the reward is given. He sits on the throne of his glory and divides the sheep from the goats, so reward according to works is the sorting itself, *he shall separate them one from another, as a shepherd divideth his sheep from the goats* (Matthew 25:32).$n$),
(2913,16,27,$n$For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.$n$,'matthew',13,36,$n$The wheat-and-tares post-harvest-sifting.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:36 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) sent the multitude away, and went into the house: and his disciples came unto him, saying, Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field.* *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27) The Son of Adam who rewards each by his works is the one who sowed the good seed, *He that soweth the good seed is the Son of Adam* (Matthew 13:37). The reapers of that harvest are the angels who come with him, and the reward is the sorting of wheat and tares.$n$),
(2914,16,28,$n$Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.$n$,'matthew',17,1,$n$The transfiguration-foretaste immediately-following.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* *Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.* (Matthew 16:28) He had said some standing there would not taste of death first, and six days later he takes three of them up the mountain. What they see there is a foretaste, *his face did shine as the sun* (Matthew 17:2), and the coming in glory itself is still ahead.$n$),
(2915,16,28,$n$Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.$n$,'2-peter',1,16,$n$Kefa's (Peter's)-own-naming of the transfiguration as the *eyewitnesses of his majesty.*$n$,$n$2 Peter 1:16 — *For we have not followed cunningly devised fables, when we made known unto you the power and coming of our Lord Yahusha HaMashiach (Lord Jesus Christ), but were eyewitnesses of his majesty.* *Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.* (Matthew 16:28) Peter was one of those standing there, and he writes that they did not follow *cunningly devised fables* but were witnesses of his majesty. He calls it the power and coming of Yahusha (Jesus), so what they saw on the mountain was real and pointed to the coming itself.$n$),
(3526,16,4,$n$A wicked and adulterous generation seeks after a sign; and there shall no sign be given unto it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah. And he left them, and departed.$n$,'matthew',12,39,$n$The first-statement of the same diagnostic.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *But he answered and said unto them, An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah:* *A wicked and adulterous generation seeks after a sign; and there shall no sign be given unto it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah. And he left them, and departed.* (Matthew 16:4) He gave the scribes and Pharisees these same words earlier, and when the Pharisees and Sadducees ask again for a sign from heaven he gives the same answer. The people who ask for signs are *adulterous*, unfaithful to the covenant, and the one sign is Jonah, who went down into the deep and came up.$n$),
(3527,16,4,$n$A wicked and adulterous generation seeks after a sign; and there shall no sign be given unto it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah. And he left them, and departed.$n$,'isaiah',1,21,$n$The Tanakh-prophetic-vocabulary for covenant-faithlessness.$n$,$n$Isaiah 1:21 — *How is the faithful city become an harlot! it was full of judgment; righteousness lodged in it; but now murderers.* *A wicked and adulterous generation seeks after a sign; and there shall no sign be given unto it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah. And he left them, and departed.* (Matthew 16:4) Isaiah is where the word *adulterous* comes from, the faithful city turned harlot, once full of judgment and now full of murderers. Yahusha (Jesus) puts the generation that demands a sign in that line, because Yahuah (the LORD) is the husband and they have left him.$n$),
(3528,16,16,$n$And Simon Peter answered and said, You are the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).$n$,'matthew',14,33,$n$The disciples' preliminary-confession after the walking-on-the-sea.$n$,$n$Matthew 14:33 — *Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth thou art the Son of Elohim (God).* *And Simon Peter answered and said, You are the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* (Matthew 16:16) The men in the boat said it in awe after the wind stopped. Peter says it at Caesarea Philippi with no storm quieted, and the Father had shown it to him. It is the same title, spoken once at a wonder and once at a revealing.$n$),
(3531,16,17,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'john',6,44,$n$*No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him.*$n$,$n$John 6:44 — *No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him: and I will raise him up at the last day.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 16:17) Peter did not reason his way to this. The Father who sent the Son is the one who draws, and Peter's answer is that drawing showing in his mouth. Those drawn are drawn to something, for the Son says he will *raise him up at the last day*.$n$),
(3532,16,17,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'galatians',1,15,$n$Paul's-apostolic-restatement of the Father's-revealing pattern.$n$,$n$Galatians 1:15 — *But when it pleased Elohim (God), who separated me from my mother’s womb, and called me by his grace,* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto him, Blessed are you, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood has not revealed it unto you, but my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 16:17) Paul had been taught zealously by men and persecuted the assembly. What turned him was no teacher but Elohim (God), who separated him from the womb and was pleased *to reveal his Son in me* (Galatians 1:16), the same Father's revealing Peter received.$n$),
(3533,16,18,$n$And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.$n$,'1-corinthians',15,54,$n$The death-conquered through-the-resurrection apostolic-restatement.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 15:54 — *So when this corruptible shall have put on incorruption, and this mortal shall have put on immortality, then shall be brought to pass the saying that is written, Death is swallowed up in victory.* *And I say also unto you, That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.* (Matthew 16:18) The gates of hell are the gates of death, and Paul says death is *swallowed up in victory* when the dead are raised incorruptible. That is why those gates will not prevail against what Yahusha (Jesus) builds.$n$),
(3534,16,21,$n$From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.$n$,'matthew',12,39,$n$The sign-of-Yonah resurrection-credential pre-announcement.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *But he answered and said unto them, An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah:* *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21) He had given the Pharisees only the sign of Jonah. Now he tells his own disciples what that sign is: that he must be killed and raised the third day.$n$),
(3535,16,24,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'luke',14,27,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 14:27 — *And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Matthew 16:24) Luke gives the same saying in other words, *cannot be my disciple*. Bearing the cross is not an extra for the very devoted but the plain mark of the one who comes after him.$n$),
(3536,16,24,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'john',12,25,$n$The lose-life-find-life substance in Yochanan (John)'s gospel.$n$,$n$John 12:25 — *He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal.* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Matthew 16:24) Just before it, John has the grain of wheat that *fall into the ground and die* (John 12:24), and that is the cross the disciple is told to take up. The grain dies and bears much fruit, and the one who gives up his life in this world keeps it unto eternal life.$n$),
(3538,16,27,$n$For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.$n$,'matthew',7,21,$n$The *anomia*-diagnostic and the kingdom-entry-criterion.$n$,$n$Matthew 7:21 — *Not every one that saith unto me, Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord), shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.* *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27) Reward according to works is not strange to the Sermon. There the question is who *doeth the will of my Father*, and the ones who only say *Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord)* are told, *depart from me, ye that work iniquity* (Matthew 7:23). The Son of Adam rewards what a man did.$n$),
(3539,16,27,$n$For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.$n$,'psalms',62,12,$n$*Thou renderest to every man according to his work* — the Tanakh-anchor.$n$,$n$Psalm 62:12 — *Also unto thee, O Yahuah (Lord), belongeth mercy: for thou renderest to every man according to his work.* *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27) The Son of Adam says nothing new here. David already said that mercy belongs to Yahuah (the LORD) and that he *renderest to every man according to his work*. Mercy and reward by works sit in the same verse.$n$),
(3540,16,27,$n$For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.$n$,'romans',2,6,$n$Paul's apostolic-restatement.$n$,$n$Romans 2:6 — *Who will render to every man according to his deeds:* *For the Son of Adam shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels; and then he shall reward every man according to his works.* (Matthew 16:27) Paul says it as plainly as the Son of Adam does, that Elohim (God) *will render to every man according to his deeds*. The man who wrote so much about faith did not make the reward by deeds go away.$n$),
(124247,16,28,$n$Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.$n$,'zechariah',14,5,$n$*And ye shall flee to the valley of the mountains; for the valley of the mountains shall reach unto Azal: yea, ye shall flee, like as ye fled from before the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Yahudah (Judah): and Yahuah Elohai (the LORD my God) shall come, and all the saints with thee.* (Zechariah 14:5). The Son of Adam coming in his kingdom comes *with his angels* (Matthew 16:27), and Zechariah names who else comes with him: *all the saints*. The saints are not the angels. They are the seed of promise — the righteous raised and the scattered gathered to him — coming with him into the land.$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:5 — *And ye shall flee to the valley of the mountains; for the valley of the mountains shall reach unto Azal: yea, ye shall flee, like as ye fled from before the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Yahudah (Judah): and Yahuah Elohai (the LORD my God) shall come, and all the saints with thee.* *Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.* (Matthew 16:28) Matthew says the Son of Adam comes with his angels, and Zechariah names who else comes with him, *all the saints*. They are a people with him and not angels, the righteous raised and the scattered gathered to him, coming at the place Zechariah names.$n$),
(3541,17,1,$n$And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,$n$,'matthew',16,28,$n$The *some standing here ... till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom* saying immediately preceding — the transfiguration as foretaste.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:28 — *Verily I say unto you, There be some standing here, which shall not taste of death, till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.* *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,* (Matthew 17:1) Matthew counts the days for us. Six days after he said that some standing there would not taste of death till they saw the Son of Adam *coming in his kingdom*, he takes three of them up a high mountain apart, and his face shines as the sun before them. The promise is hardly cold when they are shown his glory.$n$),
(3542,17,1,$n$And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,$n$,'exodus',24,15,$n$The six-days-before-Yahuah-called-Mosheh-out-of-the-cloud pattern.$n$,$n$Exodus 24:15 — *And Moses went up into the mount, and a cloud covered the mount.* *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,* (Matthew 17:1) Matthew says *after six days*, and Sinai keeps the same count: *the cloud covered it six days: and the seventh day he called unto Moses out of the midst of the cloud* (Exodus 24:16). Here again a man goes up a mountain after six days, and again a cloud comes and a voice speaks out of it.$n$),
(3543,17,1,$n$And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,$n$,'exodus',19,10,$n$The two-days-and-the-third-day pattern at Sinai — *be ready against the third day.*$n$,$n$Exodus 19:10 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Moses, Go unto the people, and sanctify them to day and to morrow, and let them wash their clothes,* *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) takes Peter, James, and John his brother, and brings them up into an high mountain apart,* (Matthew 17:1) At Sinai the people were told to wash their clothes and be ready, because Yahuah (the LORD) would come down on the mountain in the sight of all, and they were kept back from it. On this mountain three go up with Yahusha (Jesus), and what shines is not their clothes but his own, white as the light.$n$),
(3546,17,2,$n$And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.$n$,'daniel',10,5,$n$The man-clothed-in-linen vision.$n$,$n$Daniel 10:5 — *Then I lifted up mine eyes, and looked, and behold a certain man clothed in linen, whose loins were girded with fine gold of Uphaz:* *And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.* (Matthew 17:2) Daniel lifts up his eyes and sees a man clothed in linen with *his face as the appearance of lightning* (Daniel 10:6), and no strength is left in him. The three on the mountain are shown a face shining as the sun on the man standing before them, and when the voice comes they are on the ground. It is the same kind of sight.$n$),
(3547,17,2,$n$And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.$n$,'revelation',1,13,$n$The *one like the Son of Adam ... countenance as the sun shineth* substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 1:13 — *And in the midst of the seven candlesticks one like unto the Son of Adam, clothed with a garment down to the foot, and girt about the paps with a golden girdle.* *And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.* (Matthew 17:2) On the mountain *his face did shine as the sun*, and John, who was one of the three, later sees *one like unto the Son of Adam* whose *countenance was as the sun shineth in his strength* (Revelation 1:16). It is the face the three saw shine, and John knew whose it was when he turned to look.$n$),
(3548,17,2,$n$And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.$n$,'philippians',2,6,$n$The form-of-Elohim (God) the King laid-aside at the incarnation.$n$,$n$Philippians 2:6 — *Who, being in the form of Elohim (God), thought it not robbery to be equal with Elohim (God):* *And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.* (Matthew 17:2) Paul says he was in the form of Elohim (God) and then *took upon him the form of a servant* (Philippians 2:7), made in the likeness of men. On the mountain the three are given a look at what the servant's form was covering, his face as the sun and his raiment white as the light.$n$),
(3549,17,3,$n$And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.$n$,'deuteronomy',34,5,$n$Mosheh's-death and burial.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 34:5 — *So Moses the servant of Yahuah (LORD) died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of Yahuah (LORD).* *And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.* (Matthew 17:3) Moses died in Moab and was buried where *no man knoweth of his sepulchre* (Deuteronomy 34:6). Here the three see him standing and talking with Yahusha (Jesus), in what Yahusha (Jesus) afterward calls the vision. The man whose grave nobody can find is in the picture, and he is not silent.$n$),
(3550,17,3,$n$And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.$n$,'2-kings',2,11,$n$Eliyahu's-translation in the whirlwind.$n$,$n$2 Kings 2:11 — *And it came to pass, as they still went on, and talked, that, behold, there appeared a chariot of fire, and horses of fire, and parted them both asunder; and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.* *And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.* (Matthew 17:3) Elisha watched Elijah go up by a whirlwind and *he saw him no more* (2 Kings 2:12). Moses was buried and Elijah was taken up, and now the two who left the world by such different roads stand together on a mountain, talking with Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
(3552,17,3,$n$And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.$n$,'luke',9,31,$n$The vision-conversation about the King's-decease at Yerushalayim (Jerusalem).$n$,$n$Luke 9:31 — *Who appeared in glory, and spake of his decease which he should accomplish at Jerusalem.* *And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.* (Matthew 17:3) Matthew says only that they talked with him. Luke says what the talk was about, *his decease which he should accomplish at Jerusalem*. The Torah and the prophets are speaking with him about the very road he has told his disciples he must take, and they are not turning him from it.$n$),
(3553,17,3,$n$And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.$n$,'hebrews',11,39,$n$The forefathers awaiting the first-resurrection per Red Line #11.$n$,$n$Hebrews 11:39 — *And these all, having obtained a good report through faith, received not the promise:* *And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elijah talking with him.* (Matthew 17:3) Moses and Elijah are among the men this verse speaks of, who obtained a good report through faith and *received not the promise*. They are seen talking with him, and the promise itself still waits, for Elohim (God) provided something better *that they without us should not be made perfect* (Hebrews 11:40).$n$),
(3554,17,4,$n$Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.$n$,'leviticus',23,33,$n$The Sukkot (Tabernacles) Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Leviticus 23:33 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* *Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.* (Matthew 17:4) Peter says *three tabernacles*, and that is the Torah's own word for a feast: *The fifteenth day of this seventh month shall be the feast of tabernacles for seven days unto Yahuah (LORD)* (Leviticus 23:34). He reaches for booths on a mountain where the glory of Yahuah (the LORD) has just shown itself.$n$),
(3555,17,4,$n$Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.$n$,'zechariah',14,16,$n$The eschatological-Sukkot fulfillment in the Messianic-age.$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:16 — *And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* *Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.* (Matthew 17:4) The booths Peter wanted belong to a feast with a future. Zechariah has those left of the nations that came against Jerusalem going up *from year to year to worship the King* and *to keep the feast of tabernacles*, in the day when Yahuah (the LORD) is King over all the earth.$n$),
(3556,17,4,$n$Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.$n$,'john',1,14,$n$The Word *tabernacled (eskēnōsen) among us* — the King-as-the-Sukkot-substance.$n$,$n$John 1:14 — *And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, (and we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father,) full of grace and truth.* *Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.* (Matthew 17:4) Peter wants to build three dwellings. John, who stood beside him, later writes that *the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us* and that *we beheld his glory*. The tabernacle was already standing in front of Peter, in the man whose face was shining.$n$),
(3557,17,4,$n$Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.$n$,'revelation',21,3,$n$*The tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men, and he will dwell with them.* The final-substance of the Sukkot-pattern.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:3 — *And I heard a great voice out of heaven saying, Behold, the tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people, and Elohim (God) himself shall be with them, and be their Elohim (God).* *Then answered Peter, and said unto Yahusha (Jesus), Lord, it is good for us to be here: if you will, let us make here three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.* (Matthew 17:4) Peter offered to put up three booths on the mountain, and what is promised at the end is a dwelling that Elohim (God) brings himself: *the tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men*, and *they shall be his people*. The feast Peter reached for points to that day.$n$),
(3561,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'matthew',3,17,$n$The Yarden-baptism voice — same combined-citation.$n$,$n$Matthew 3:17 — *And lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) These are the words that came from heaven at the Jordan, spoken again out of the cloud, with something added at the end. *Hear ye him* is the Father's word to the three who have just watched his Son talking with Moses and Elijah.$n$),
(3562,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'exodus',13,21,$n$The cloud-and-fire-pillar leading Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,$n$Exodus 13:21 — *And Yahuah (LORD) went before them by day in a pillar of a cloud, to lead them the way; and by night in a pillar of fire, to give them light; to go by day and night:* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) In the wilderness Yahuah (the LORD) went in front of Yashar'el (Israel) by day in a pillar of cloud as their guide. A cloud comes over the three on the mountain and a voice comes out of it, and again the cloud is where the voice is found.$n$),
(3563,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'exodus',40,34,$n$The *kavod* (glory) filling the Tabernacle.$n$,$n$Exodus 40:34 — *Then a cloud covered the tent of the congregation, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) filled the tabernacle.* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) When the cloud covered the tent, the glory of Yahuah (the LORD) filled the tabernacle, and *Moses was not able to enter into the tent of the congregation, because the cloud abode thereon* (Exodus 40:35). On the mountain a bright cloud overshadows them, and they fall on their faces.$n$),
(3564,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'1-kings',8,10,$n$The cloud filling the Temple at the dedication.$n$,$n$1 Kings 8:10 — *And it came to pass, when the priests were come out of the holy place, that the cloud filled the house of Yahuah (LORD),* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) At the dedication the cloud filled the house of Yahuah (the LORD), so that *the priests could not stand to minister because of the cloud* (1 Kings 8:11). The cloud that overshadows the three is that cloud, and they too cannot stand before it.$n$),
(3565,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'isaiah',4,5,$n$The eschatological-cloud-and-fire-of-glory upon Mount Tsion (Zion).$n$,$n$Isaiah 4:5 — *And Yahuah (LORD) will create upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, and upon her assemblies, a cloud and smoke by day, and the shining of a flaming fire by night: for upon all the glory shall be a defence.* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) Isaiah promises a cloud by day and a flaming fire by night upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, in the day when the one who is left there *shall be called holy, even every one that is written among the living in Jerusalem* (Isaiah 4:3). The bright cloud over the three is that cloud, shown beforehand.$n$),
(3566,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'acts',3,22,$n$Kefa's apostolic application of Devarim (Deuteronomy) 18:15.$n$,$n$Acts 3:22 — *For Moses truly said unto the fathers, A prophet shall Yahuah Elohaychem (the Lord your God) raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me; him shall ye hear in all things whatsoever he shall say unto you.* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) Peter, who heard the voice on the mountain, later tells the people what it meant. Moses said a prophet like himself would be raised up from among the brethren, and *him shall ye hear in all things*. So *hear ye him* is no new command. It is Moses' own promise, and the man he promised is standing there with Moses himself beside him.$n$),
(3567,17,5,$n$While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.$n$,'2-peter',1,17,$n$Kefa's apostolic-witness of the transfiguration-voice.$n$,$n$2 Peter 1:17 — *For he received from Elohim (God) the Father honour and glory, when there came such a voice to him from the excellent glory, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.* *While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear you him.* (Matthew 17:5) Peter says he was there. He calls himself and the others *eyewitnesses of his majesty* (2 Peter 1:16), and the voice he heard come *from the excellent glory* is the voice Matthew places in the bright cloud, spoken when they were with him on the holy mountain.$n$),
(3568,17,6,$n$And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.$n$,'daniel',8,17,$n$Daniel falls on his face.$n$,$n$Daniel 8:17 — *So he came near where I stood: and when he came, I was afraid, and fell upon my face: but he said unto me, Understand, O son of Adam: for at the time of the end shall be the vision.* *And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.* (Matthew 17:6) Daniel falls on his face when the messenger comes near, and then *he touched me, and set me upright* (Daniel 8:18). The three fall on their faces in the same way, and Yahusha (Jesus) comes and touches them: *Arise, and be not afraid* (Matthew 17:7).$n$),
(3569,17,6,$n$And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.$n$,'ezekiel',1,28,$n$Yechezk'el falls upon his face.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 1:28 — *As the appearance of the bow that is in the cloud in the day of rain, so was the appearance of the brightness round about. This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of Yahuah (LORD). And when I saw it, I fell upon my face, and I heard a voice of one that spake.* *And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.* (Matthew 17:6) Ezekiel sees the brightness round about *as the appearance of the bow that is in the cloud*, falls on his face, and hears a voice that speaks. The three on the mountain hear a voice out of a bright cloud and fall on their faces. It is the same glory, and the same fall.$n$),
(3570,17,6,$n$And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.$n$,'isaiah',6,5,$n$Yeshayahu's *woe is me!*$n$,$n$Isaiah 6:5 — *Then said I, Woe is me! for I am undone; because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people of unclean lips: for mine eyes have seen the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* *And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.* (Matthew 17:6) Isaiah sees the King and cries that he is undone, a man of unclean lips among a people of unclean lips. The three disciples fall down for the same reason, they have just heard the voice out of the glory, and the nearness of it lays a man flat before he can speak.$n$),
(3571,17,6,$n$And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.$n$,'revelation',1,17,$n$Yochanan falls as dead.$n$,$n$Revelation 1:17 — *And when I saw him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying unto me, Fear not; I am the first and the last:* *And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.* (Matthew 17:6) John falls at his feet as dead, and he lays his right hand on him with *Fear not*. John was one of the three who fell on the mountain, and the hand and the words are the same: Yahusha (Jesus) *came and touched them, and said, Arise, and be not afraid* (Matthew 17:7).$n$),
(3572,17,9,$n$And as they came down from the mountain, Yahusha (Jesus) charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of Adam be risen again from the dead.$n$,'matthew',12,39,$n$The sign-of-Yonah resurrection-credential.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *But he answered and said unto them, An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah:* *And as they came down from the mountain, Yahusha (Jesus) charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of Adam be risen again from the dead.* (Matthew 17:9) The sign of the prophet Jonah is the sign of his rising: *so shall the Son of Adam be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth* (Matthew 12:40). That is why the vision is to be kept quiet until the Son of Adam is risen from the dead.$n$),
(3573,17,9,$n$And as they came down from the mountain, Yahusha (Jesus) charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of Adam be risen again from the dead.$n$,'matthew',16,20,$n$The Caesarea-Philippi tell-no-man + first-passion-prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:20 — *Then charged he his disciples that they should tell no man that he was Yahusha (Jesus) the Messiah (Christ).* *And as they came down from the mountain, Yahusha (Jesus) charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of Adam be risen again from the dead.* (Matthew 17:9) He silences them twice. Once he charges them to tell no man he was the Messiah (Christ), and then *he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things* (Matthew 16:21). Coming down the mountain he gives the date the silence ends, *until the Son of Adam be risen again from the dead*.$n$),
(3575,17,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.$n$,'matthew',11,14,$n$*This is Eliyahu (Elijah), which was for to come.*$n$,$n$Matthew 11:14 — *And if ye will receive it, this is Elijah, which was for to come.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.* (Matthew 17:11) He said it of John before: *this is Elijah, which was for to come*. Yet on the way down he says Elijah *shall first come, and restore all things*, in the future. So John came in the pattern of Elijah, and the restoring is still in front of us.$n$),
(3576,17,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.$n$,'matthew',14,1,$n$The Eliyahu-pattern-martyrdom of Yochanan (John).$n$,$n$Matthew 14:1 — *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.* (Matthew 17:11) Elijah's work cost him the favour of a king's house, and John's did too. He told Herod *It is not lawful for thee to have her* (Matthew 14:4), and Herod bound him. The one who comes in Elijah's pattern to restore is met by a ruler who does not want it restored.$n$),
(3577,17,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.$n$,'luke',1,17,$n$Gavri'el's-naming of Yochanan (John) as coming *in the spirit and power of Eliyahu (Elijah).*$n$,$n$Luke 1:17 — *And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for Yahuah (Lord).* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.* (Matthew 17:11) Gabriel said John would go before him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*, turning the hearts of fathers back toward their children. John is Elijah's pattern at work, which is why Yahusha (Jesus) can say Elijah is come already and still speak of Elijah who shall first come and restore all things.$n$),
(3578,17,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.$n$,'revelation',11,3,$n$The two-witnesses second-fulfillment of the Eliyahu-pattern.$n$,$n$Revelation 11:3 — *And I will give power unto my two witnesses, and they shall prophesy a thousand two hundred and threescore days, clothed in sackcloth.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.* (Matthew 17:11) Revelation gives the two witnesses Elijah's own marks: *These have power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy* (Revelation 11:6), and fire out of their mouth. Yahusha (Jesus) says Elijah shall first come, and here is that pattern at work again further on.$n$),
(3579,17,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.$n$,'acts',3,21,$n$*The times of restitution of all things, which Elohim (God) hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began.*$n$,$n$Acts 3:21 — *Whom the heaven must receive until the times of restitution of all things, which Elohim (God) hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Elijah truly shall first come, and restore all things.* (Matthew 17:11) Yahusha (Jesus) says Elijah shall *restore all things*, and Peter says heaven must receive him *until the times of restitution of all things*, which Elohim (God) has spoken by all his prophets. Peter stood on that mountain. The restoring is the same, and it is still waiting.$n$),
(3580,17,17,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.$n$,'deuteronomy',32,5,$n$Mosheh's-naming of the perverse-and-crooked generation.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 32:5 — *They have corrupted themselves, their spot is not the spot of his children: they are a perverse and crooked generation.* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.* (Matthew 17:17) Perverse is Moses' word from the song he taught Yashar'el (Israel), where a generation that corrupted itself, unmindful of the Rock that begat it, is named *a perverse and crooked generation*. Yahusha (Jesus) says it to the crowd at the foot of the mountain, and it grieves him.$n$),
(3581,17,17,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.$n$,'deuteronomy',32,20,$n$Mosheh's-naming of the perverse-and-crooked generation.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 32:20 — *And he said, I will hide my face from them, I will see what their end shall be: for they are a very froward generation, children in whom is no faith.* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.* (Matthew 17:17) *Faithless* here is this verse's *children in whom is no faith*, where Yahuah (the LORD) says he will hide his face to see their end. Now he is among them in the flesh, face and all, and still he asks how long he must be with them.$n$),
(3582,17,17,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.$n$,'psalms',78,8,$n$*A stubborn and rebellious generation.*$n$,$n$Psalm 78:8 — *And might not be as their fathers, a stubborn and rebellious generation; a generation that set not their heart aright, and whose spirit was not stedfast with Elohim (God).* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.* (Matthew 17:17) Moses' word is not alone. The psalm says what the fault was: *They kept not the covenant of Elohim (God), and refused to walk in his law* (Psalm 78:10). The rebuke is aimed at a generation that turned from the Torah, never at the Torah.$n$),
(3583,17,17,$n$Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.$n$,'psalms',95,10,$n$*Forty years long was I grieved with this generation.*$n$,$n$Psalm 95:10 — *Forty years long was I grieved with this generation, and said, It is a people that do err in their heart, and they have not known my ways:* *Then Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.* (Matthew 17:17) *How long shall I suffer you* is the grief of the wilderness: *Forty years long was I grieved with this generation*, and the trouble was that they had not known his ways. The one who led them there is the one standing at the foot of the mountain asking how long.$n$),
(3584,17,20,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.$n$,'matthew',13,31,$n$The mustard-seed parable.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:31 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and sowed in his field:* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.* (Matthew 17:20) In the parable the kingdom is a grain of mustard seed, *the least of all seeds* (Matthew 13:32), which grows into a tree. Here faith is that seed, and the smallest of faith is enough to move a mountain. The smallness is the point in both places.$n$),
(3585,17,20,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.$n$,'matthew',21,21,$n$The fig-tree-and-the-mountain restatement.$n$,$n$Matthew 21:21 — *Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said unto them, Verily I say unto you, If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; it shall be done.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.* (Matthew 17:20) He says it a second time to the same men, at the fig tree, and gives the other half. Here the failure is *your unbelief*, and there the condition is *If ye have faith, and doubt not*. A mountain moved in both, but only by faith that does not waver.$n$),
(3586,17,20,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.$n$,'1-corinthians',13,2,$n$Paul's *all faith ... remove mountains.*$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 13:2 — *And though I have the gift of prophecy, and understand all mysteries, and all knowledge; and though I have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.* (Matthew 17:20) Paul takes up the same picture, faith enough to remove mountains, and says that without charity it is nothing. So the faith Yahusha (Jesus) asks for is no show of power. It is the kind of faith that sits beside love and is not mistaken for it.$n$),
(3587,17,21,$n$Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.$n$,'matthew',6,16,$n$The King's-Sermon teaching on fasting.$n$,$n$Matthew 6:16 — *Moreover when ye fast, be not, as the hypocrites, of a sad countenance: for they disfigure their faces, that they may appear unto men to fast. Verily I say unto you, They have their reward.* *Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.* (Matthew 17:21) He says *when ye fast*, as something his hearers already do, and the Sermon says how it is done: not to be seen of men, but unto the Father, who *seeth in secret, shall reward thee openly* (Matthew 6:18). The fasting that goes with prayer is of that kind.$n$),
(3588,17,21,$n$Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.$n$,'isaiah',58,6,$n$The acceptable-fast Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 58:6 — *Is not this the fast that I have chosen? to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke?* *Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.* (Matthew 17:21) Isaiah says what fast Yahuah (the LORD) chose: *to loose the bands of wickedness* and *to let the oppressed go free*. A boy who falls into fire and water is held by such a band, and the fast that goes with prayer is the kind that looses.$n$),
(3589,17,21,$n$Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.$n$,'acts',13,2,$n$The apostolic-assembly fasting at the commissioning.$n$,$n$Acts 13:2 — *As they ministered to Yahuah (Lord), and fasted, the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul for the work whereunto I have called them.* *Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.* (Matthew 17:21) The practice he names to the disciples goes on after him. At Antioch they ministered to Yahuah (the LORD) and fasted, and in that time the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) spoke and set Barnabas and Saul apart for the work.$n$),
(3590,17,21,$n$Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.$n$,'acts',14,23,$n$The apostolic-elder-appointment with fasting.$n$,$n$Acts 14:23 — *And when they had ordained them elders in every church, and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to Yahuah (Lord), on whom they believed.* *Howbeit this kind goes not out but by prayer and fasting.* (Matthew 17:21) Prayer and fasting go together again here. When Paul and Barnabas appointed elders in each place, they *prayed with fasting* and committed them to Yahuah (the LORD), so the two Yahusha (Jesus) joined on the mountain road are still joined in the work.$n$),
(3591,17,22,$n$And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:$n$,'matthew',16,21,$n$The first-passion-prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* (Matthew 17:22) He said it first plainly: he must go to Jerusalem, suffer many things, be killed, and be raised the third day. Now in Galilee he says it again, with the new word *betrayed*, and the whole company is exceeding sorry. The road has not changed since the mountain.$n$),
(3592,17,22,$n$And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:$n$,'matthew',20,17,$n$The third-passion-prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 20:17 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) going up to Jerusalem took the twelve disciples apart in the way, and said unto them,* *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* (Matthew 17:22) Each telling adds a piece. Here it is that the Son of Adam will be betrayed into the hands of men, and on the way up it is named whose hands, *the chief priests and unto the scribes* (Matthew 20:18). The third day he shall rise is in both.$n$),
(3593,17,22,$n$And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:$n$,'isaiah',53,6,$n$*Yahuah (the LORD) hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.* The delivered-up Suffering-Servant Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:6 — *All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and Yahuah (LORD) hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.* *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* (Matthew 17:22) Men hand him over, and Isaiah shows who is behind the handing over: Yahuah (the LORD) laid on him the iniquity of us all. And those it was laid for are *all we like sheep*, gone astray, each to his own way, the lost sheep he came to find.$n$),
(3594,17,22,$n$And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:$n$,'zechariah',13,7,$n$The smitten-shepherd.$n$,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *Awake, O sword, against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts): smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* (Matthew 17:22) The shepherd is struck and the sheep scattered, and the betrayal is the striking. But Zechariah does not stop there, for the next verse says *I will bring the third part through the fire, and will refine them as silver is refined* (Zechariah 13:9). The scattering is not the end.$n$),
(3595,17,24,$n$And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?$n$,'exodus',30,11,$n$The half-shekel atonement-money.$n$,$n$Exodus 30:11 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* *And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?* (Matthew 17:24) The collectors want the half shekel, and the money comes from this passage: every one numbered gives half a shekel after the shekel of the sanctuary, and *an half shekel shall be the offering of Yahuah (LORD)* (Exodus 30:13). He does not set the Torah aside, for he says the children are free and then has it paid.$n$),
(3596,17,24,$n$And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?$n$,'jonah',1,17,$n$The great-fish appointed-by-Yahuah.$n$,$n$Jonah 1:17 — *Now Yahuah (LORD) had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.* *And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?* (Matthew 17:24) Yahuah (the LORD) prepared a great fish for Jonah, and at Capernaum a fish is ready with a coin in its mouth. Peter is told to *take up the fish that first cometh up* (Matthew 17:27), and it comes to his hook as it came for Jonah. The fish does what it is told in both places.$n$),
(3597,17,24,$n$And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?$n$,'john',8,35,$n$*If the Son therefore shall make you free, ye shall be free indeed.*$n$,$n$John 8:35 — *And the servant abideth not in the house for ever: but the Son abideth ever.* *And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?* (Matthew 17:24) The kings' children are free, and a servant does not abide in the house for ever but the Son does. He says in John, *If the Son therefore shall make you free, ye shall be free indeed* (John 8:36). The freedom is the Son's place in his Father's house, and he pays the half shekel to that house anyway.$n$),
(3598,17,24,$n$And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?$n$,'romans',14,13,$n$The voluntary-restraint-for-the-sake-of-others apostolic-substance.$n$,$n$Romans 14:13 — *Let us not therefore judge one another any more: but judge this rather, that no man put a stumblingblock or an occasion to fall in his brother’s way.* *And when they were come to Capernaum, they that received tribute money came to Peter, and said, Does not your master pay tribute?* (Matthew 17:24) The children are free, and yet he sends Peter for the coin *lest we should offend them* (Matthew 17:27). Paul gives the same reason, that nobody is to set *a stumblingblock or an occasion to fall* before his brother. Free, and giving up the right so as not to trip someone.$n$),
(3599,18,1,$n$At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?$n$,'matthew',19,14,$n$*Of such is the kingdom of heaven.*$n$,$n$Matthew 19:14 — *But Yahusha (Jesus) said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven.* *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* (Matthew 18:1) The disciples who ask *Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* are the same ones who stand in the way when children are brought to him, because *the disciples rebuked them* (Matthew 19:13). Yahusha (Jesus) answers the question a second time, and again he points to the little ones and says the kingdom belongs to such.$n$),
(3600,18,1,$n$At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?$n$,'psalms',131,1,$n$*My soul is even as a weaned child.*$n$,$n$Psalm 131:1 — *Yahuah (LORD), my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty: neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me.* *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* (Matthew 18:1) The disciples are busy with greatness, and the psalmist says the opposite: *neither do I exercise myself in great matters*. He has quieted himself *as a child that is weaned of his mother* (Psalm 131:2), and the psalm ends by turning Yashar'el (Israel) toward hope in Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
(3601,18,1,$n$At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?$n$,'jeremiah',31,18,$n$*Turn thou me, and I shall be turned.* The Tanakh-*teshuvah* substance.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 31:18 — *I have surely heard Ephraim bemoaning himself thus; Thou hast chastised me, and I was chastised, as a bullock unaccustomed to the yoke: turn thou me, and I shall be turned; for thou art Yahuah Elohai (the LORD my God).* *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* (Matthew 18:1) His answer to the disciples begins with turning, *Except ye be converted, and become as little children* (Matthew 18:3). Ephraim, the scattered northern house, asks for the same thing, *turn thou me, and I shall be turned*, and Yahuah (the LORD) answers by calling him a child, *Is Ephraim my dear son? is he a pleasant child?* (Jeremiah 31:20).$n$),
(3602,18,4,$n$Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'proverbs',22,4,$n$*By humility and the fear of Yahuah (the LORD) are riches, and honour, and life.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 22:4 — *By humility and the fear of Yahuah (LORD) are riches, and honour, and life.* *Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 18:4) The proverb answers the question the disciples asked. *Riches, and honour, and life* come by humility joined to the fear of Yahuah (the LORD), and the child set in the midst of them is that humility standing in the room.$n$),
(3603,18,4,$n$Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'isaiah',57,15,$n$The Father's-dwelling with the contrite-and-humble substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 57:15 — *For thus saith the high and lofty One that inhabiteth eternity, whose name is Holy; I dwell in the high and holy place, with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble, and to revive the heart of the contrite ones.* *Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 18:4) The greatest, he says, is the one who humbles himself like the child. Isaiah shows where such a person sits, since the high and lofty One who inhabits eternity says he dwells in the high and holy place *with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit*. The humble are his near neighbours.$n$),
(3604,18,4,$n$Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'1-peter',5,5,$n$The apostolic-restatement.$n$,$n$1 Peter 5:5 — *Likewise, ye younger, submit yourselves unto the elder. Yea, all of you be subject one to another, and be clothed with humility: for Elohim (God) resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble.* *Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 18:4) Peter was among the disciples who first heard this answer, and years later he writes it to the elders and the younger, *be clothed with humility*. His reason is the child's greatness turned the other way round, that Elohim (God) *resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble*.$n$),
(3605,18,6,$n$But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.$n$,'matthew',5,29,$n$The Sermon parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:29 — *And if thy right eye offend thee, pluck it out, and cast it from thee: for it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell.* *But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.* (Matthew 18:6) Both sayings use the same word, *offend*, a stone laid in the road to trip a walker. In the Sermon the stone is your own eye, set right beside *Thou shalt not commit adultery* (Matthew 5:27), the commandment of Moses he is guarding. Here the stone is laid before a little one, and the cost is the sea.$n$),
(3606,18,6,$n$But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.$n$,'mark',9,42,$n$The Markan parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 9:42 — *And whosoever shall offend one of these little ones that believe in me, it is better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he were cast into the sea.* *But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.* (Matthew 18:6) Mark carries the same millstone, but he sets it right after the cup of water given in Yahusha's (Jesus's) name, of which he says *he shall not lose his reward* (Mark 9:41). What is done to the little ones is counted either way, the kindness and the stone laid in their road.$n$),
(3607,18,6,$n$But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.$n$,'luke',17,1,$n$The Lukan parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 17:1 — *Then said he unto the disciples, It is impossible but that offences will come: but woe unto him, through whom they come!* *But whoso shall offend one of these little ones which believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and that he were drowned in the depth of the sea.* (Matthew 18:6) Luke gives the woe first, *It is impossible but that offences will come*, and puts the millstone in the next verse. Right behind it comes the other half of Matthew's chapter, *If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him* (Luke 17:3).$n$),
(3610,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'isaiah',66,24,$n$*Their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 66:24 — *And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me: for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh.* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) The fire Yahusha (Jesus) names is the fire Isaiah saw at the end of his book, and Isaiah says what lies in it, *the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me*. The same chapter has all flesh coming to worship *from one sabbath to another* (Isaiah 66:23), so the fire sits at the edge of a kingdom that keeps his days.$n$),
(3611,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'jeremiah',7,31,$n$The valley-of-slaughter Gehinnom substance.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 7:31 — *And they have built the high places of Tophet, which is in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to burn their sons and their daughters in the fire; which I commanded them not, neither came it into my heart.* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) The valley Yahusha (Jesus) names is a real one, the valley of the son of Hinnom, where the children of Yahudah (Judah) built *the high places of Tophet* and burned their sons and daughters, which Yahuah (the LORD) says *I commanded them not*. He renames it in the next verse, *but the valley of slaughter: for they shall bury in Tophet, till there be no place* (Jeremiah 7:32). That valley is the name behind *hell fire* (Matthew 18:9).$n$),
(3608,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'matthew',5,29,$n$The Sermon parallel sin-amputation.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:29 — *And if thy right eye offend thee, pluck it out, and cast it from thee: for it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell.* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) The Sermon and this chapter weigh it the same way, one member against the whole body. In the Sermon *one of thy members should perish* so that the whole body is not cast into hell, and here the hand or foot is cut off so that a man enters life *halt or maimed*. Either way he goes in with the body he has, short of a member.$n$),
(3609,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'mark',9,43,$n$The Markan parallel with worm-and-fire substance.$n$,$n$Mark 9:43 — *And if thy hand offend thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter into life maimed, than having two hands to go into hell, into the fire that never shall be quenched:* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) Mark says it with the hand alone, *enter into life maimed*, and then spells out the other end, the fire *that never shall be quenched*. The next verse repeats the line, *Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched* (Mark 9:44), so life is entered in the body one has, short of a hand, and the fire is where the whole body goes otherwise.$n$),
(133759,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'john',20,27,$n$John 20:27 — *...behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side...* The kept wounds are the pattern: the body is raised in its form, the change reserved for the appointed day.$n$,$n$John 20:27 — *Then saith he to Thomas, Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side: and be not faithless, but believing.* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) The one who says it is better to enter life maimed shows Thomas his own hands after the rising. *Behold my hands*, he says, and *thrust it into my side*, so the wounds are kept, and the body that comes up is the body that went down.$n$),
(133760,18,8,$n$Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.$n$,'isaiah',56,5,$n$Isaiah 56:5 — *...a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters... an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.* The eunuch cut off from sons is the maimed entering life: the form is not the disqualification.$n$,$n$Isaiah 56:5 — *Even unto them will I give in mine house and within my walls a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.* *Wherefore if your hand or your foot offend you, cut them off, and cast them from you: it is better for you to enter into life halt or maimed, rather than having two hands or two feet to be cast into everlasting fire.* (Matthew 18:8) A body short of something is not shut out of the house. Yahuah (the LORD) speaks to *the eunuchs that keep my sabbaths* (Isaiah 56:4) and gives them in his house *a name better than of sons and of daughters*. The man cut off from offspring is kept and named, and so the one who enters life maimed is not turned away for the form he carries.$n$),
(3612,18,10,$n$Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'psalms',91,11,$n$*He shall give his angels charge over thee.*$n$,$n$Psalm 91:11 — *For he shall give his angels charge over thee, to keep thee in all thy ways.* *Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:10) Yahusha (Jesus) says the angels of the little ones are always before the Father's face. The psalm says what such angels are for, *charge over thee, to keep thee in all thy ways*, said of the man who has made Yahuah (the LORD) his refuge. So the little one he warns against despising is under a guard.$n$),
(3613,18,10,$n$Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'daniel',10,13,$n$The Michael-and-Persia angelic-substance.$n$,$n$Daniel 10:13 — *But the prince of the kingdom of Persia withstood me one and twenty days: but, lo, Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me; and I remained there with the kings of Persia.* *Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:10) Angels in Daniel have names and ranks and take sides. The messenger is held up by *the prince of the kingdom of Persia*, and *Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me*. When Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of angels standing before the Father for the little ones, it is the same kind of host Daniel saw at work for his people.$n$),
(3614,18,10,$n$Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'daniel',10,21,$n$The Michael-and-Persia angelic-substance.$n$,$n$Daniel 10:21 — *But I will shew thee that which is noted in the scripture of truth: and there is none that holdeth with me in these things, but Michael your prince.* *Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:10) Daniel is told that Michael is *your prince*, an angel who stands for his people and holds with the messenger in what is written in the scripture of truth. That is the kind of thing Yahusha (Jesus) says of a little one, that his angels are in heaven before the Father.$n$),
(3615,18,10,$n$Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'hebrews',1,14,$n$*Ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation.*$n$,$n$Hebrews 1:14 — *Are they not all ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation?* *Take heed that you despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:10) Hebrews gives the work of such angels in a line. They are *ministering spirits*, sent out to serve those who inherit, and the verse before says none of them was ever told *Sit on my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool* (Hebrews 1:13). The angels who see the Father's face in Matthew are the ones sent out from it.$n$),
(3616,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'ezekiel',34,11,$n$The Tanakh-Yahuah-shepherd-substance — *I will both search my sheep, and seek them out.*$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) The man who leaves the ninety and nine for the strayed one is Yahuah (the LORD) in Ezekiel, who says of the shepherds that failed *Behold, I am against the shepherds* (Ezekiel 34:10) and then takes the search on himself, *will both search my sheep, and seek them out*. His sheep are the ones *that are scattered* (Ezekiel 34:12).$n$),
(3617,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'psalms',119,176,$n$*I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant.*$n$,$n$Psalm 119:176 — *I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant; for I do not forget thy commandments.* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) The sheep that has gone astray in the psalm is still holding the commandments, for his last word is *I do not forget thy commandments*. His prayer is *seek thy servant*, and that is the errand of the shepherd in Matthew. The one he goes after has strayed and has not been forgotten.$n$),
(3618,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'isaiah',53,6,$n$*All we like sheep have gone astray.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:6 — *All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and Yahuah (LORD) hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) The sheep that have gone astray here are *all we*, and the same chapter says whose they are, *for the transgression of my people was he stricken* (Isaiah 53:8). The Shepherd who goes after the strayed one in Matthew is the one who carries what put them out on the mountain, since Yahuah (the LORD) *hath laid on him the iniquity of us all*.$n$),
(3619,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'jeremiah',50,6,$n$*My people hath been lost sheep: their shepherds have caused them to go astray.*$n$,$n$Jeremiah 50:6 — *My people hath been lost sheep: their shepherds have caused them to go astray, they have turned them away on the mountains: they have gone from mountain to hill, they have forgotten their restingplace.* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) The lost sheep here are *my people*, and the verses around them say who, *the children of Yashar'el (Israel) shall come, they and the children of Yahudah (Judah) together* (Jeremiah 50:4). Their own shepherds *have caused them to go astray*, so the one that wandered in Matthew's parable is a sheep of that flock, turned away on the mountains by those who should have kept it.$n$),
(3620,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'luke',15,3,$n$The Lukan-parallel applied to publicans-and-sinners.$n$,$n$Luke 15:3 — *And he spake this parable unto them, saying,* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) Luke tells the same story to other hearers. There it answers the Pharisees and scribes who murmured, *This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them* (Luke 15:2), and in Matthew it is told to the disciples about the little ones. Either way the shepherd's answer is the same, that he goes after the one.$n$),
(3621,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'2-peter',3,9,$n$*Not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.*$n$,$n$2 Peter 3:9 — *Yahuah (Lord) is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) Matthew closes the parable with the Father's will, *it is not the will of your Father which is in heaven, that one of these little ones should perish* (Matthew 18:14). Peter says the same will in his own words, that Yahuah (the LORD) is *longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish*. What some count as slowness is the shepherd still out on the mountain.$n$),
(3622,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'matthew',10,6,$n$The lost-sheep-of-the-house-of-Yashar'el (Israel) mission-scope.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:6 — *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) When he sent out the twelve he named whom the sheep are, *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*. The sheep that has gone astray in this parable is one of them, a member of that house, and the shepherd going after it is on the same errand he gave the twelve.$n$),
(3623,18,12,$n$How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?$n$,'matthew',15,24,$n$The King's-own mission-scope restated.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *How think you? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, does he not leave the ninety and nine, and goes into the mountains, and seeks that which is gone astray?* (Matthew 18:12) He says it in his own mouth, *not sent but unto the lost sheep*, and it is the shepherd of this parable speaking. The woman who hears those words had come crying *thou Son of David* (Matthew 15:22), the title of that house's king, and she answers by worshipping and asking again, *Adonai (Lord), help me* (Matthew 15:25).$n$),
(3624,18,15,$n$Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.$n$,'leviticus',19,17,$n$The Tanakh-substance of private-confrontation.$n$,$n$Leviticus 19:17 — *Thou shalt not hate thy brother in thine heart: thou shalt in any wise rebuke thy neighbour, and not suffer sin upon him.* *Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.* (Matthew 18:15) The first step is already Moses'. *Thou shalt not hate thy brother in thine heart* is set right beside *rebuke thy neighbour, and not suffer sin upon him*, so the word spoken to him alone is how a brother is not hated in silence and his sin is not left sitting on him. It is the commandment kept, with gaining the brother as its aim.$n$),
(3625,18,15,$n$Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.$n$,'deuteronomy',19,15,$n$The two-or-three-witnesses standard.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 19:15 — *One witness shall not rise up against a man for any iniquity, or for any sin, in any sin that he sinneth: at the mouth of two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses, shall the matter be established.* *Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.* (Matthew 18:15) When the private word fails, he reaches for the rule Moses gave. *In the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established* (Matthew 18:16) is Deuteronomy's own rule, that one witness cannot rise against a man, and a matter is settled only *at the mouth of two witnesses*.$n$),
(3626,18,15,$n$Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.$n$,'1-corinthians',5,1,$n$Paul's apostolic application.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 5:1 — *It is reported commonly that there is fornication among you, and such fornication as is not so much as named among the Gentiles, that one should have his father’s wife.* *Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.* (Matthew 18:15) Here the matter is already public, *reported commonly*, and Paul writes to the assembly itself, which is the third step of Matthew's order. His complaint is that they had not *rather mourned, that he that hath done this deed might be taken away from among you* (1 Corinthians 5:2).$n$),
(3627,18,15,$n$Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.$n$,'galatians',6,1,$n$The restoration-disposition.$n$,$n$Galatians 6:1 — *Brethren, if a man be overtaken in a fault, ye which are spiritual, restore such an one in the spirit of meekness; considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted.* *Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.* (Matthew 18:15) The aim of every step is that *thou hast gained thy brother*, and Paul says how it is done by those who are spiritual, *restore such an one in the spirit of meekness*, *considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted*. The word to a brother who has fallen is for bringing him back.$n$),
(3628,18,15,$n$Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.$n$,'1-timothy',5,19,$n$The two-or-three-witnesses standard applied to leadership.$n$,$n$1 Timothy 5:19 — *Against an elder receive not an accusation, but before two or three witnesses.* *Moreover if your brother shall trespass against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone: if he shall hear you, you have gained your brother.* (Matthew 18:15) Paul gives Timothy the same rule for an elder, no accusation *but before two or three witnesses*. It is the rule of Moses that Yahusha (Jesus) repeated at the second step, and it holds even for a man whose place would make him easy to accuse.$n$),
(3629,18,18,$n$Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.$n$,'matthew',16,19,$n$The first-statement of the binding-and-loosing to Kefa.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:19 — *And I will give unto thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* *Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* (Matthew 18:18) Here he gives the keys to Peter, and in this chapter the same words go to all the disciples together, with a brother's case and two or three witnesses just before them. So the binding and loosing is a trust held by many and used on a matter that has been established, and not the power of one man over heaven.$n$),
(3630,18,18,$n$Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.$n$,'john',20,21,$n$The post-resurrection apostolic-commissioning.$n$,$n$John 20:21 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) to them again, Peace be unto you: as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you.* *Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* (Matthew 18:18) After the rising he sends them as he was sent, *as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you*, and in the verse after he says *Whose soever sins ye remit, they are remitted unto them* (John 20:23). It is the same binding and loosing, carried by those he sends and given by the Father's own sending.$n$),
(3631,18,18,$n$Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.$n$,'acts',15,1,$n$The Yerushalayim Council apostolic-action.$n$,$n$Acts 15:1 — *And certain men which came down from Judæa taught the brethren, and said, Except ye be circumcised after the manner of Moses, ye cannot be saved.* *Verily I say unto you, Whatsoever you shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* (Matthew 18:18) When men from Judæa taught *Except ye be circumcised after the manner of Moses*, the brethren did not settle it on one voice. There was *no small dissension and disputation* (Acts 15:2), and the matter went up to the apostles and elders at Jerusalem, many mouths and not one, which is the way a hard matter is to be weighed.$n$),
(3632,18,19,$n$Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'malachi',3,16,$n$*They that feared Yahuah (the LORD) spake often one to another.*$n$,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* *Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:19) The two who agree are people like these in Malachi, who feared Yahuah (the LORD) and *spake often one to another*. He *hearkened, and heard it*, and a book of remembrance was written, and the same chapter ends with him telling the righteous from the wicked, *between him that serveth Elohim (God) and him that serveth him not* (Malachi 3:18).$n$),
(3633,18,19,$n$Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'matthew',28,20,$n$*Lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world.*$n$,$n$Matthew 28:20 — *Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.* *Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:19) The one who says there *am I in the midst of them* (Matthew 18:20) says at the end *lo, I am with you alway*. The presence is tied to a task, *Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you*, so the two or three who stand in his name are the ones keeping what he commanded.$n$),
(3634,18,19,$n$Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'john',5,14,$n$*If we ask any thing according to his will, he heareth us.*$n$,$n$John 5:14 — *Afterward Yahusha (Jesus) findeth him in the temple, and said unto him, Behold, thou art made whole: sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee.* *Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:19) The man he made whole is told *sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee*. What the Father does at the Son's hand comes with the commandment, *My Father worketh hitherto, and I work* (John 5:17), so what two agree to ask is not a blank cheque for anything at all.$n$),
(3635,18,19,$n$Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.$n$,'hebrews',13,5,$n$*I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.*$n$,$n$Hebrews 13:5 — *Let your conversation be without covetousness; and be content with such things as ye have: for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.* *Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.* (Matthew 18:19) The two or three of the next verse have him *there am I in the midst of them* (Matthew 18:20), and Hebrews gives that presence its promise, *I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee*. The writer rests it on the one who does not change, *Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) the same yesterday, and to day, and for ever* (Hebrews 13:8).$n$),
(3636,18,21,$n$Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?$n$,'genesis',4,23,$n$Lamekh's (Lamech's)-seventy-and-sevenfold vengeance — the Tanakh-substance inverted.$n$,$n$Genesis 4:23 — *And Lamech said unto his wives, Adah and Zillah, Hear my voice; ye wives of Lamech, hearken unto my speech: for I have slain a man to my wounding, and a young man to my hurt.* *Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21) Lamech boasts that Cain's sevenfold vengeance is small beside his own, *truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold* (Genesis 4:24). Peter offers seven times, and Yahusha (Jesus) answers *Until seventy times seven* (Matthew 18:22), taking the number out of Lamech's mouth and spending it on forgiveness.$n$),
(3637,18,21,$n$Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?$n$,'luke',17,3,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 17:3 — *Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him.* *Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21) Luke has the same count, and he keeps the order Moses set, *rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him*. The next verse puts Peter's seven into the brother's mouth, *seven times in a day, and seven times in a day turn again to thee, saying, I repent* (Luke 17:4), and the answer is still to forgive.$n$),
(3638,18,21,$n$Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?$n$,'psalms',103,8,$n$The Father's-forgiveness-substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 103:8 — *Yahuah (LORD) is merciful and gracious, slow to anger, and plenteous in mercy.* *Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21) Peter asks how often a brother may be forgiven, and the psalm gives the pattern behind the answer, Yahuah (the LORD) who is *slow to anger, and plenteous in mercy*. Two verses on it says *He hath not dealt with us after our sins* (Psalm 103:10), which is the king's pardon before the servant went out and took his fellow by the throat.$n$),
(3639,18,21,$n$Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?$n$,'micah',7,18,$n$The Father's-pardoning-substance.$n$,$n$Micah 7:18 — *Who is a Elohim (God) like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage? he retaineth not his anger for ever, because he delighteth in mercy.* *Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?* (Matthew 18:21) Micah asks who is like Yahuah (the LORD), *that pardoneth iniquity*, and the pardon is for *the remnant of his heritage*, his own people. The brother Peter is asked to forgive is one of them, and the king in the parable does as Micah says, passing by the debt because he *delighteth in mercy*.$n$),
(3640,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'matthew',6,14,$n$The Lord's-Prayer's-forgiveness-condition.$n$,$n$Matthew 6:14 — *For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you:* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) He gave the lesson in one line in the Sermon, *your heavenly Father will also forgive you* when men forgive each other, and the next verse says the reverse. The king and his two servants are that line told as a story, and it ends the same way, *So likewise shall my heavenly Father do also unto you* (Matthew 18:35).$n$),
(3641,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'matthew',7,1,$n$The *judge not* substance from the Sermon.$n$,$n$Matthew 7:1 — *Judge not, that ye be not judged.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) The Sermon's warning, *Judge not, that ye be not judged*, is told here as a story. The servant who would not forgive a hundred pence meets the rule in *with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again* (Matthew 7:2), and the debt he was forgiven comes back on him.$n$),
(3642,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'james',2,13,$n$*He shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy.*$n$,$n$James 2:13 — *For he shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy; and mercy rejoiceth against judgment.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) James puts the parable in one sentence, *judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy*, and he sets it under law, since the verse before speaks of being judged *by the law of liberty* (James 2:12). The servant gets that judgment, and the verse ends on the other side of it, *mercy rejoiceth against judgment*.$n$),
(3643,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'ephesians',4,32,$n$*Be ye kind one to another ... forgiving one another.*$n$,$n$Ephesians 4:32 — *And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as Elohim (God) for Messiah's (Christ’s) sake hath forgiven you.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) Paul's *even as* is the lord's question in the parable, *even as I had pity on thee* (Matthew 18:33). Be *tenderhearted*, he says, forgiving one another because Elohim (God) has forgiven, which is the order of the story, mercy received first and then mercy owed.$n$),
(3644,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'colossians',3,13,$n$*Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another.*$n$,$n$Colossians 3:13 — *Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Messiah (Christ) forgave you, so also do ye.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) Paul has a quarrel in view, as the parable does, and he tells those who have one to bear with each other and forgive, *even as Messiah (Christ) forgave you, so also do ye*. That *so also do ye* is the king's verdict on the servant, who was forgiven much and would not forgive a hundred pence.$n$),
(3645,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'proverbs',21,13,$n$*Whoso stoppeth his ears at the cry of the poor, he also shall cry himself, but shall not be heard.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 21:13 — *Whoso stoppeth his ears at the cry of the poor, he also shall cry himself, but shall not be heard.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) The servant who would not hear his fellow's plea, *Have patience with me* (Matthew 18:29), is the man in the proverb who *stoppeth his ears*. The proverb says he will cry himself and *shall not be heard*, and the parable shows the same turn, the lord who heard him once now handing him over.$n$),
(3646,18,23,$n$Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.$n$,'james',5,16,$n$The apostolic-confession-and-prayer substance.$n$,$n$James 5:16 — *Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much.* *Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened unto a certain king, which would take account of his servants.* (Matthew 18:23) In the parable each servant falls down and asks, and one is heard and the other refused. James tells brothers to *Confess your faults one to another* and *pray one for another, that ye may be healed*, and the verse before says *if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him* (James 5:15).$n$),
(3647,19,1,$n$And it came to pass, that when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished these sayings, he departed from Galilee, and came into the coasts of Judaea beyond Jordan;$n$,'matthew',4,23,$n$The Galilean-multitudes-following pattern continued.$n$,$n$Matthew 4:23 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease among the people.* *And it came to pass, that when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished these sayings, he departed from Galilee, and came into the coasts of Judaea beyond Jordan;* (Matthew 19:1) The one who went about all Galilee teaching and healing has not changed his work by crossing over into Judaea. The synagogues are behind him now, but the kingdom is still what he preaches, and the sick are still what he heals as he goes.$n$),
(3648,19,1,$n$And it came to pass, that when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished these sayings, he departed from Galilee, and came into the coasts of Judaea beyond Jordan;$n$,'joshua',1,1,$n$The Yashar'el-entry-into-the-Land from-beyond-the-Yarden Tanakh-substance the King's-route recapitulates.$n$,$n$Joshua 1:1 — *Now after the death of Moses the servant of Yahuah (LORD) it came to pass, that Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Joshua the son of Nun, Moses’ minister, saying,* *And it came to pass, that when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished these sayings, he departed from Galilee, and came into the coasts of Judaea beyond Jordan;* (Matthew 19:1) Joshua was told to arise and go over this Jordan into the land given to the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and Yahusha (Jesus) bears the same name. Matthew has him come into the coasts of Judaea *beyond Jordan*, on the road Joshua first walked.$n$),
(3649,19,4,$n$And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,$n$,'genesis',1,27,$n$*Male and female created he them.*$n$,$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.* *And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4) Asked about putting away a wife, he goes back past the schools to the first making. *Made them male and female* is the line in Genesis, and the whole answer to the Pharisees is going to stand on it.$n$),
(3650,19,4,$n$And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,$n$,'genesis',2,24,$n$*They shall be one flesh.*$n$,$n$Genesis 2:24 — *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* *And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4) He answers the Pharisees with the words of the creation itself, the man leaving father and mother, cleaving to his wife, and the two becoming one flesh. That is the beginning he sends them back to, and a bill of divorcement was never where the matter started.$n$),
(3651,19,4,$n$And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,$n$,'malachi',2,14,$n$*Yahuah (the LORD) hateth putting away.*$n$,$n$Malachi 2:14 — *Yet ye say, Wherefore? Because Yahuah (LORD) hath been witness between thee and the wife of thy youth, against whom thou hast dealt treacherously: yet is she thy companion, and the wife of thy covenant.* *And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4) Malachi sets the wife of a man's youth beside the altar. Yahuah (the LORD) stands *witness between thee and the wife of thy youth*, and she is *the wife of thy covenant*. The same chapter says he *hateth putting away* (Malachi 2:16), which is the weight behind Yahusha's (Jesus's) answer.$n$),
(3652,19,4,$n$And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,$n$,'1-corinthians',6,16,$n$*He which is joined to an harlot is one body? for two, saith he, shall be one flesh.* Paul's apostolic-application of Bereshit (Genesis) 2:24.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 6:16 — *What? know ye not that he which is joined to an harlot is one body? for two, saith he, shall be one flesh.* *And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4) Paul reaches for the same creation word Yahusha (Jesus) uses and shows how heavy it is. The two becoming one flesh is a thing done in the body, so heavy that even a joining to a harlot makes *one body*.$n$),
(3653,19,4,$n$And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,$n$,'ephesians',5,31,$n$*For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother ... this is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Messiah (Christ) and the assembly.*$n$,$n$Ephesians 5:31 — *For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh.* *And he answered and said unto them, Have you not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4) Paul takes the same sentence from Genesis, *they two shall be one flesh*, and writes it into his teaching to husbands and wives. Then he calls it *This is a great mystery* (Ephesians 5:32) when he turns to the Messiah (Christ). The words he rests on are the words Yahusha (Jesus) rested on.$n$),
(3654,19,7,$n$They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?$n$,'deuteronomy',24,1,$n$The bill-of-divorce concession-substance.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 24:1 — *When a man hath taken a wife, and married her, and it come to pass that she find no favour in his eyes, because he hath found some uncleanness in her: then let him write her a bill of divorcement, and give it in her hand, and send her out of his house.* *They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?* (Matthew 19:7) This is the bill the Pharisees hold up. Moses is writing around something already happening, and the paper he asks for goes into the woman's hand. Yahusha (Jesus) names it plainly, *because of the hardness of your hearts suffered you to put away your wives* (Matthew 19:8), a concession and not a word set above the beginning.$n$),
(3655,19,7,$n$They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?$n$,'deuteronomy',17,14,$n$The king-substance Mosheh-concedes — same architectural-pattern of concession-to-hardness-of-heart-substance.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 17:14 — *When thou art come unto the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee, and shalt possess it, and shalt dwell therein, and shalt say, I will set a king over me, like as all the nations that are about me;* *They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?* (Matthew 19:7) Moses speaks of a king the same way. The people will say they want a king *like as all the nations that are about me*, and Moses writes the rules for that king before there is one. The hand that wrote the law of divorce wrote the law of the king, and neither was where it began.$n$),
(3656,19,7,$n$They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?$n$,'1-samuel',8,5,$n$*They have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me.* The Tanakh-king-concession parallel-substance.$n$,$n$1 Samuel 8:5 — *And said unto him, Behold, thou art old, and thy sons walk not in thy ways: now make us a king to judge us like all the nations.* *They say unto him, Why did Moses then command to give a writing of divorcement, and to put her away?* (Matthew 19:7) When the elders asked for *a king to judge us like all the nations*, Yahuah (the LORD) told Samuel, *they have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me* (1 Samuel 8:7). It was granted, and still it was a turning from the way. That is the shape of the bill of divorcement, *but from the beginning it was not so* (Matthew 19:8).$n$),
(3657,19,9,$n$And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.$n$,'matthew',5,31,$n$The Sermon-parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:31 — *It hath been said, Whosoever shall put away his wife, let him give her a writing of divorcement:* *And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.* (Matthew 19:9) On the mountain it was *It hath been said*, and here the Pharisees bring it, but the line is the same bill of divorcement. Both times he is dealing with the easy exit men had made of it, and here he names *except it be for fornication* as the one exception.$n$),
(3658,19,9,$n$And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.$n$,'mark',10,11,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 10:11 — *And he saith unto them, Whosoever shall put away his wife, and marry another, committeth adultery against her.* *And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.* (Matthew 19:9) Mark gives the same saying and says plainly where the wrong lands. The man who puts away his wife and marries another commits adultery *against her*. The weight is on what is done to the wife.$n$),
(3659,19,9,$n$And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.$n$,'luke',16,18,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 16:18 — *Whosoever putteth away his wife, and marrieth another, committeth adultery: and whosoever marrieth her that is put away from her husband committeth adultery.* *And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.* (Matthew 19:9) Luke puts this saying right after *easier for heaven and earth to pass, than one tittle of the law to fail* (Luke 16:17). So the word on divorce comes as the law standing, not as the law set aside.$n$),
(3660,19,9,$n$And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.$n$,'1-corinthians',7,10,$n$The Pauline-apostolic-application.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 7:10 — *And unto the married I command, yet not I, but Yahusha (Lord), Let not the wife depart from her husband:* *And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, commits adultery: and whoso marrieth her which is put away does commit adultery.* (Matthew 19:9) Paul hands the saying back to the one who said it. *Let not the wife depart from her husband* is not his own opinion, he says, it is Yahusha's (Jesus's) word to the married, and he goes on to say *let not the husband put away his wife* (1 Corinthians 7:11).$n$),
(3661,19,10,$n$His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.$n$,'isaiah',56,3,$n$The eunuchs-who-keep-my-sabbaths Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 56:3 — *Neither let the son of the stranger, that hath joined himself to Yahuah (LORD), speak, saying, Yahuah (LORD) hath utterly separated me from his people: neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree.* *His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.* (Matthew 19:10) The disciples say it is not good to marry, and Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of eunuchs. Isaiah had already spoken to the eunuch who says *I am a dry tree*, and Yahuah (the LORD) gives the one who keeps his sabbaths *a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters* (Isaiah 56:5).$n$),
(3662,19,10,$n$His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.$n$,'1-corinthians',7,7,$n$The Pauline-celibacy-for-undistracted-kingdom-substance.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 7:7 — *For I would that all men were even as I myself. But every man hath his proper gift of Elohim (God), one after this manner, and another after that.* *His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.* (Matthew 19:10) When the disciples say it is not good to marry, he answers *All men cannot receive this saying, save they to whom it is given* (Matthew 19:11). Paul says the same from his own place, that *every man hath his proper gift of Elohim (God)*, one this way and another that. The gift is given, not claimed.$n$),
(3663,19,10,$n$His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.$n$,'1-corinthians',7,32,$n$The Pauline-celibacy-for-undistracted-kingdom-substance.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 7:32 — *But I would have you without carefulness. He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to Yahusha (Lord), how he may please Yahusha (Lord):* *His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.* (Matthew 19:10) Paul gives the reason a man might hold back from marrying, *He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to* Yahusha (Jesus). It is the ground Yahusha (Jesus) gives for those who *have made themselves eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven’s sake* (Matthew 19:12), a choice for the work and not a charge against marriage.$n$),
(3664,19,10,$n$His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.$n$,'revelation',14,4,$n$*These are they which were not defiled with women; for they are virgins.* The 144,000 firstfruits-substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 14:4 — *These are they which were not defiled with women; for they are virgins. These are they which follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth. These were redeemed from among men, being the firstfruits unto Elohim (God) and to the Lamb.* *His disciples say unto him, If the case of the man be so with his wife, it is not good to marry.* (Matthew 19:10) Revelation shows a company kept single for him, *These are they which follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth*, firstfruits *redeemed from among men*. It stands beside the eunuchs Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of as a calling given to some.$n$),
(3665,19,13,$n$Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.$n$,'matthew',18,1,$n$The disciples'-greatness-question and the King's-child-substance answer.$n$,$n$Matthew 18:1 — *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13) In chapter 18 the disciples asked *Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* and he stood a little child in front of them. Now they are rebuking the people who bring the children, as though the answer had never been given.$n$),
(3666,19,13,$n$Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.$n$,'mark',10,13,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 10:13 — *And they brought young children to him, that he should touch them: and his disciples rebuked those that brought them.* *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13) Mark tells it almost word for word. They brought young children so that he would *touch* them, and the disciples rebuked those who brought them. Matthew says he was to *put his hands on them, and pray*, and the point is the same, the children are carried to him and the disciples stand in the way.$n$),
(3667,19,13,$n$Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.$n$,'luke',18,15,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 18:15 — *And they brought unto him also infants, that he would touch them: but when his disciples saw it, they rebuked them.* *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13) Luke says *infants*, where Matthew says *little children*, so the ones carried to him were very small. The people bring them to be touched, and the disciples again turn them back.$n$),
(3668,19,13,$n$Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.$n$,'psalms',8,2,$n$*Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength.*$n$,$n$Psalm 8:2 — *Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength because of thine enemies, that thou mightest still the enemy and the avenger.* *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13) The psalm says Yahuah (the LORD) ordains strength *Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings*, and the disciples are rebuking the babes. What they are keeping from him is what the psalm says his strength is built from.$n$),
(3669,19,13,$n$Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.$n$,'isaiah',11,6,$n$*A little child shall lead them.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 11:6 — *The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them.* *Then were there brought unto him little children, that he should put his hands on them, and pray: and the disciples rebuked them.* (Matthew 19:13) Isaiah's picture of the kingdom ends with *a little child shall lead them*. The disciples treat the children as something in the way of Yahusha (Jesus), while Isaiah sets a child at the head of the wolf and the lamb under the one who judges with righteousness.$n$),
(3670,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'deuteronomy',30,15,$n$*I have set before thee this day life and good ... keep his commandments.*$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 30:15 — *See, I have set before thee this day life and good, and death and evil;* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) The young man asks what he must do to have life, and Moses had already set the answer before the people, *life and good, and death and evil*. So when Yahusha (Jesus) says *if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments* (Matthew 19:17), he is not handing out something new, he is pointing at the old road to the same life.$n$),
(3671,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'leviticus',18,5,$n$*Which if a man do, he shall live in them.*$n$,$n$Leviticus 18:5 — *Ye shall therefore keep my statutes, and my judgments: which if a man do, he shall live in them: I am Yahuah (LORD).* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) The young man asks what to do in order to live, and Leviticus has the plain answer, the statutes and judgments, *which if a man do, he shall live in them*. Yahusha (Jesus) does not send him anywhere else. He says to keep the commandments.$n$),
(3672,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'ezekiel',20,11,$n$*Which if a man do, he shall even live in them.*$n$,$n$Ezekiel 20:11 — *And I gave them my statutes, and shewed them my judgments, which if a man do, he shall even live in them.* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) Ezekiel has Yahuah (the LORD) say it himself of the day he brought them out of Egypt, that the statutes he gave are ones a man does and *shall even live in them*. The young man asked what to do for life, and this is what was given for life.$n$),
(3673,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'proverbs',4,13,$n$*Take fast hold of instruction; let her not go: keep her; for she is thy life.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 4:13 — *Take fast hold of instruction; let her not go: keep her; for she is thy life.* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) Proverbs says of instruction, *keep her; for she is thy life*. The young man wants life and Yahusha (Jesus) points him to the commandments, so the two answers stand together. What Yahuah (the LORD) gave to keep is the life the man was asking for.$n$),
(3674,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'proverbs',7,2,$n$*Keep my commandments, and live.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 7:2 — *Keep my commandments, and live; and my law as the apple of thine eye.* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) *Keep my commandments, and live* is Yahusha's (Jesus's) whole answer in a single line of Proverbs. The young man asked what good thing to do for life, and the proverb says what: keep the commandments, and hold the law *as the apple of thine eye*.$n$),
(3675,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'john',2,3,$n$The 1234 of Truth.$n$,$n$John 2:3 — *And when they wanted wine, the mother of Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, They have no wine.* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) The match is in the next verse, not this one. At Cana his mother tells the servants *Whatsoever he saith unto you, do it* (John 2:5), and the young man is asking the same Master what to do. What he says to him is to keep the commandments.$n$),
(3676,19,16,$n$And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?$n$,'revelation',22,14,$n$The blessed-are-they-that-do-his-commandments substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 22:14 — *Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city.* *And, behold, one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?* (Matthew 19:16) Revelation closes where the young man began. He asked how to enter into life, and its last chapter blesses *they that do his commandments*, with *right to the tree of life*. Yahusha (Jesus) said to keep the commandments, and the end of the book still says it.$n$),
(3677,19,18,$n$He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,$n$,'exodus',20,12,$n$The Decalogue-commandments 5-9.$n$,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* *He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,* (Matthew 19:18) Every commandment he names to the young man is a word from Sinai, and this is the one he adds after the four, *Honour thy father and thy mother*. When he says to keep the commandments, these are the commandments he means, the ones spoken by Yahuah (the LORD), not a new list.$n$),
(3678,19,18,$n$He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,$n$,'leviticus',19,18,$n$*Love thy neighbour as thyself.*$n$,$n$Leviticus 19:18 — *Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people, but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am Yahuah (LORD).* *He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,* (Matthew 19:18) He ends his list with love of neighbour, and he takes it from Leviticus, *thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself*, where it stands among the statutes and closes with *I am Yahuah (LORD)*. The young man is given Moses from the first word to the last.$n$),
(3679,19,18,$n$He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,$n$,'deuteronomy',5,16,$n$The Decalogue-restatement.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 5:16 — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee; that thy days may be prolonged, and that it may go well with thee, in the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* *He says unto him, Which? Yahusha (Jesus) said, You shall do no murder, You shall not commit adultery, You shall not steal, You shall not bear false witness,* (Matthew 19:18) Deuteronomy gives the fifth word again as Moses repeats it to the people, *as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee*. The commandments Yahusha (Jesus) lists are the ones the Torah says twice, and he sets them before the young man as they stand.$n$),
(3680,19,21,$n$Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.$n$,'matthew',5,48,$n$The *teleios* (*tamim*)-substance of the Sermon.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:48 — *Be ye therefore perfect, even as your Father which is in heaven is perfect.* *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21) Perfect is the same word in both places. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the young man *if thou wilt be perfect*, and in the Sermon it is *Be ye therefore perfect*. In both it is the whole heart toward the Father, standing on the commandments, never above them or past them.$n$),
(3681,19,21,$n$Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.$n$,'genesis',17,1,$n$*Walk before me, and be thou perfect (tamim).*$n$,$n$Genesis 17:1 — *And when Abram was ninety years old and nine, Yahuah (LORD) appeared to Abram, and said unto him, I am the El Shaddai (Almighty God); walk before me, and be thou perfect.* *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21) *Walk before me, and be thou perfect* is what Yahuah (the LORD) said to Abram as he was about to make his covenant with him, long before Sinai. So Yahusha's (Jesus's) *if thou wilt be perfect* is not a step above the commandments. It is the fathers' own call, to walk whole before him.$n$),
(3682,19,21,$n$Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.$n$,'deuteronomy',18,13,$n$*Thou shalt be perfect (tamim) with Yahuah (the LORD) thy Elohim (God).*$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 18:13 — *Thou shalt be perfect with Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God).* *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21) Moses puts it in one line, *Thou shalt be perfect with Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God)*, and puts it among the commandments to Yashar'el (Israel), not over them. So when Yahusha (Jesus) says *if thou wilt be perfect*, he is using Moses' word.$n$),
(3683,19,21,$n$Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.$n$,'luke',12,33,$n$*Sell that ye have, and give alms.*$n$,$n$Luke 12:33 — *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21) He says to his disciples what he said to the young man, *Sell that ye have, and give alms*, with the same reward, *a treasure in the heavens*. It was not a trap set for one rich man, and Luke puts the heart right behind it, *For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also* (Luke 12:34).$n$),
(3684,19,21,$n$Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.$n$,'acts',4,32,$n$The apostolic-assembly's voluntary-substance-sharing.$n$,$n$Acts 4:32 — *And the multitude of them that believed were of one heart and of one soul: neither said any of them that ought of the things which he possessed was his own; but they had all things common.* *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, If you will be perfect, go and sell that you have, and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me.* (Matthew 19:21) In Acts the multitude who believed were *of one heart and of one soul* and had *all things common*. What he asked of the young man, to sell and give, was done freely by many who followed him.$n$),
(3685,19,23,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'proverbs',30,8,$n$*Give me neither poverty nor riches.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 30:8 — *Remove far from me vanity and lies: give me neither poverty nor riches; feed me with food convenient for me:* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:23) Yahusha (Jesus) says a rich man shall *hardly enter*, and Proverbs has a man praying against that weight, *give me neither poverty nor riches*. He asks for enough and no more.$n$),
(3686,19,23,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'jeremiah',9,23,$n$*Let not the rich man glory in his riches.*$n$,$n$Jeremiah 9:23 — *Thus saith Yahuah (LORD), Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, neither let the mighty man glory in his might, let not the rich man glory in his riches:* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:23) Jeremiah sets the rich man beside the wise man and the mighty man and says none of them should boast. Yahusha's (Jesus's) word about the rich man is the same warning, *let not the rich man glory in his riches*, said sharper, and the needle shows how close the glory sits to the heart.$n$),
(3687,19,23,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'psalms',49,6,$n$*They that trust in their wealth.*$n$,$n$Psalm 49:6 — *They that trust in their wealth, and boast themselves in the multitude of their riches;* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:23) The psalm names the man in Yahusha's (Jesus's) saying, those who *trust in their wealth*, and then says what the wealth cannot buy, *None of them can by any means redeem his brother, nor give to Elohim (God) a ransom for him* (Psalm 49:7). Riches do not carry a man into the kingdom, and that is the trouble the disciples feel.$n$),
(3688,19,23,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'james',5,1,$n$*Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries.* The apostolic-Tanakh-prophetic-substance restatement.$n$,$n$James 5:1 — *Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries that shall come upon you.* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:23) James speaks to the rich the way the prophets did, *weep and howl for your miseries*. Yahusha (Jesus) says it will be hard for a rich man to enter, and James names the sorrow coming on those who stayed there, the same warning in a plainer voice.$n$),
(3689,19,23,$n$Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.$n$,'1-timothy',6,9,$n$*They that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare.*$n$,$n$1 Timothy 6:9 — *But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition.* *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, Verily I say unto you, That a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.* (Matthew 19:23) Paul says why. *They that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare*, into lusts that *drown men in destruction and perdition*. He does not blame the money but the wanting of it, and Yahusha's (Jesus's) rich man with his needle has the same trouble.$n$),
(3690,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'genesis',18,14,$n$*Is any thing too hard for Yahuah (the LORD)?*$n$,$n$Genesis 18:14 — *Is any thing too hard for Yahuah (LORD)? At the time appointed I will return unto thee, according to the time of life, and Sarah shall have a son.* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) To the disciples' *Who then can be saved?* the answer is an old one. Sarah was told she would have a son, and the word that went with it was *Is any thing too hard for Yahuah (LORD)?* What man cannot do, he has done before.$n$),
(3691,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'jeremiah',32,17,$n$*Behold ... there is nothing too hard for thee.*$n$,$n$Jeremiah 32:17 — *Ah Yahuah (Lord) GOD! behold, thou hast made the heaven and the earth by thy great power and stretched out arm, and there is nothing too hard for thee:* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) Jeremiah prays it as a confession, *there is nothing too hard for thee*, and gives the proof, the heaven and the earth made by *great power and stretched out arm*. The disciples' question is answered by the one who made everything.$n$),
(3692,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'jeremiah',32,27,$n$*Is there any thing too hard for me?*$n$,$n$Jeremiah 32:27 — *Behold, I am Yahuah (LORD), the Elohim (God) of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) Jeremiah prayed that nothing was too hard for him, and the answer now comes from Yahuah (the LORD) himself, *the Elohim (God) of all flesh*, asking it back, *is there any thing too hard for me?* The disciples' question has met the one for whom it is not impossible.$n$),
(3693,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'job',42,2,$n$*I know that thou canst do every thing.*$n$,$n$Job 42:2 — *I know that thou canst do every thing, and that no thought can be withholden from thee.* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) Job comes out of his trial saying *I know that thou canst do every thing*, which is where the disciples have to arrive. The question of who can be saved is not answered by what a man can do.$n$),
(3694,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'luke',1,37,$n$*For with Elohim (God) nothing shall be impossible.*$n$,$n$Luke 1:37 — *For with Elohim (God) nothing shall be impossible.* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) Luke puts the same sentence at the opening of his gospel, *For with Elohim (God) nothing shall be impossible*, before anything he did on earth was seen. Yahusha (Jesus) says it to the disciples after the camel and the needle, and it is the same promise.$n$),
(3695,19,25,$n$When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?$n$,'ezekiel',36,26,$n$*A new heart also will I give you ... I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes.* The Father's-substance-enabling-the-substance the natural-substance-cannot.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.* *When his disciples heard it, they were exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?* (Matthew 19:25) The impossible thing is answered by a gift. Ezekiel has Yahuah (the LORD) take away *the stony heart* and give *an heart of flesh*, so the one who could not keep the commandments is made able to, as the next verse says, *cause you to walk in my statutes* (Ezekiel 36:27).$n$),
(3696,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'daniel',7,13,$n$*One like the Son of Adam* (kaph-comparative preserved per Red Line #12) coming-with-the-clouds + everlasting-kingdom.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *I saw in the night visions, and, behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days, and they brought him near before him.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Daniel sees *one like the Son of Adam* brought near the Ancient of days, and the next verse gives him a kingdom, *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away* (Daniel 7:14). When Matthew's Son of Adam sits *in the throne of his glory*, it is that kingdom given, with the twelve seated under him.$n$),
(3697,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'matthew',25,31,$n$*When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory ... then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory.* Direct-Matthean parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 25:31 — *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Matthew gives the throne a second time, *the Son of Adam shall come in his glory*, and then *sit upon the throne of his glory*. It is the same throne the twelve thrones stand beside in this chapter, and the judging rests on it.$n$),
(3698,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'isaiah',65,17,$n$*Behold, I create new heavens and a new earth.* The *palingenesia* substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 65:17 — *For, behold, I create new heavens and a new earth: and the former shall not be remembered, nor come into mind.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) *In the regeneration* is the word in Matthew, and Isaiah is the promise behind it, *I create new heavens and a new earth*. The next verse names the people in it, *I create Jerusalem a rejoicing, and her people a joy* (Isaiah 65:18). The throne is set in that renewed world.$n$),
(3699,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'isaiah',66,22,$n$*The new heavens and the new earth, which I will make.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 66:22 — *For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me, saith Yahuah (LORD), so shall your seed and your name remain.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Yahuah (the LORD) says the new heavens and the new earth *shall remain before me*, and then *so shall your seed and your name remain*. The renewing Matthew speaks of is bound up with a seed that stays, and the twelve tribes under the thrones are that seed.$n$),
(3700,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'acts',3,21,$n$*The times of restitution of all things.*$n$,$n$Acts 3:21 — *Whom the heaven must receive until the times of restitution of all things, which Elohim (God) hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Acts says the same day in other words. The heaven must keep him *until the times of restitution of all things*, which Elohim (God) *hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets*. Matthew's regeneration is that restoring, and the prophets were already speaking of it.$n$),
(3701,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'romans',8,18,$n$*Creation itself also shall be delivered from the bondage of corruption.*$n$,$n$Romans 8:18 — *For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Paul counts the sufferings of this time small beside *the glory which shall be revealed in us*, and says creation waits for *the manifestation of the sons of Elohim (God)* (Romans 8:19). Those who left all to follow him in Matthew are looking at the same day.$n$),
(3702,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'revelation',21,1,$n$*I saw a new heaven and a new earth ... the tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men.* The consummated-kingdom-substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:1 — *And I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth were passed away; and there was no more sea.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) In Revelation John sees what the regeneration leads to, *a new heaven and a new earth*, with the first ones passed away. Matthew gives the moment the Son of Adam takes his throne, and Revelation shows the world made new.$n$),
(3703,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'revelation',21,12,$n$*Twelve gates ... names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel).*$n$,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel):* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) The twelve thrones sit over the twelve tribes, and in Revelation the city has *twelve gates*, with the *names of the twelve tribes* written on them. The tribes are still named tribes at the end, as in Matthew, and never a word for something else.$n$),
(3704,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'revelation',21,14,$n$*Twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.*$n$,$n$Revelation 21:14 — *And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) The city that carries the tribes' names on its gates carries *the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb* on its foundations. It sets the twelve who followed him beside the twelve tribes they judge, as Matthew does, one set of twelve beside the other and neither taking the other's place.$n$),
(3705,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'revelation',4,4,$n$*Four and twenty elders sitting* (twelve-tribes elders + twelve-apostles elders).$n$,$n$Revelation 4:4 — *And round about the throne were four and twenty seats: and upon the seats I saw four and twenty elders sitting, clothed in white raiment; and they had on their heads crowns of gold.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) John sees *four and twenty seats* round a throne, with elders sitting on them *clothed in white raiment*, crowned. Seats round a throne is the picture Matthew gives of the twelve, though how many of these seats are the tribes and how many the apostles is something the verse does not say.$n$),
(3706,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'ezekiel',37,15,$n$*I will make them one nation in the land ... they shall be no more two nations.* The two-sticks-becoming-one twelve-tribes-restoration substance.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 37:15 — *The word of Yahuah (LORD) came again unto me, saying,* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) This verse only opens the word, and the word is the two sticks, *take thee one stick, and write upon it, For Yahudah (Judah)* (Ezekiel 37:16), and the other for Joseph, and both *become one in thine hand* (Ezekiel 37:17). The twelve tribes the twelve are set over are the two houses made one.$n$),
(3707,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'isaiah',11,11,$n$*He shall set up an ensign for the goyim (nations), and shall assemble the outcasts of Yashar'el (Israel), and gather together the dispersed of Yahudah (Judah) from the four corners of the earth.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 11:11 — *And it shall come to pass in that day, that Yahuah (Lord) shall set his hand again the second time to recover the remnant of his people, which shall be left, from Assyria, and from Egypt, and from Pathros, and from Cush, and from Elam, and from Shinar, and from Hamath, and from the islands of the sea.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Isaiah has Yahuah (the Lord) set his hand again *to recover the remnant of his people* from Assyria, Egypt and the islands of the sea, the whole scattering named. Those he recovers are the ones the twelve thrones are set over, so the tribes in Matthew are people brought home, not a figure of speech.$n$),
(3708,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'jeremiah',31,31,$n$The new-covenant-with-both-houses substance.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 31:31 — *Behold, the days come, saith Yahuah (LORD), that I will make a new covenant with the house of Yashar'el (Israel), and with the house of Yahudah (Judah):* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) The new covenant is made *with the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* and with the house of Yahudah (Judah), both houses. The twelve tribes in Matthew are the same two houses, and the covenant that brings them in is the one Jeremiah says is made with them.$n$),
(3709,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'ezekiel',47,1,$n$The twelve-tribes-land-allotment in the consummated-kingdom.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 47:1 — *Afterward he brought me again unto the door of the house; and, behold, waters issued out from under the threshold of the house eastward: for the forefront of the house stood toward the east, and the waters came down from under from the right side of the house, at the south side of the altar.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Ezekiel's closing vision starts at the house in a restored land, where *waters issued out from under the threshold of the house eastward*. The twelve thrones belong to that same renewed day, and it is the same restored land the tribes come home to.$n$),
(3710,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'james',1,1,$n$*To the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad.* Apostolic-greeting-substance assuming-the-twelve-tribes' continued-substance.$n$,$n$James 1:1 — *James, a servant of Elohim (God) and of the Lord Yahusha HaMashiach (Lord Jesus Christ), to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) James writes his letter *to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad*, so the apostles after the resurrection still speak of twelve tribes, scattered, as a people to be written to. They are the tribes the thrones are set over, real and still scattered, which is why the gathering matters.$n$),
(3711,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'acts',26,7,$n$*Unto which promise our twelve tribes, instantly serving Elohim (God) day and night, hope to come.* Paul's-Tanakh-twelve-tribes-hope-substance.$n$,$n$Acts 26:7 — *Unto which promise our twelve tribes, instantly serving Elohim (God) day and night, hope to come. For which hope’s sake, king Agrippa, I am accused of the Yahudim (Jews).* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Paul says *our twelve tribes* hope to come to the promise, and he says it before a king as the hope he is accused for. The twelve tribes are a living people in his mouth, *instantly serving Elohim (God) day and night*, and Matthew's thrones are set over them.$n$),
(3712,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'1-corinthians',6,2,$n$*Do ye not know that the saints shall judge the world?* The apostolic-saints-judging-substance.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 6:2 — *Do ye not know that the saints shall judge the world? and if the world shall be judged by you, are ye unworthy to judge the smallest matters?* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Paul asks *Do ye not know that the saints shall judge the world?* as something the people ought already to know. It is the judging of the thrones in Matthew carried into his letter, those who followed him given a part in the judging.$n$),
(3713,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'revelation',20,4,$n$*I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them.* The reigning-with-the-King substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:4 — *And I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them: and I saw the souls of them that were beheaded for the witness of Yahusha (Jesus), and for the word of Elohim (God), and which had not worshipped the beast, neither his image, neither had received his mark upon their foreheads, or in their hands; and they lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) John sees *thrones* with those seated on them *and judgment was given unto them*, and those who sat held to the witness of Yahusha (Jesus), refused the mark, and reigned with Messiah (Christ). That is the seated company Matthew promises to those who followed him.$n$),
(3714,19,28,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'luke',22,28,$n$The Lukan-parallel — *I appoint unto you a kingdom ... ye shall sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).*$n$,$n$Luke 22:28 — *Ye are they which have continued with me in my temptations.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That you which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, you also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28) Luke has the same promise and gives the reason for it, *Ye are they which have continued with me in my temptations*. The thrones come after the staying with him, *sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Luke 22:30), and in both gospels it is the twelve tribes who are judged.$n$),
(3715,19,29,$n$And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.$n$,'matthew',20,1,$n$The laborers-in-the-vineyard parable that-extends-the-substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 20:1 — *For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.* *And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.* (Matthew 19:29) The promise of the hundredfold is followed straight away by the householder who went out early to hire labourers, and the parable takes up the saying that closes the chapter, *many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first* (Matthew 19:30). The reward is promised, and the vineyard shows whose hand it comes from.$n$),
(3716,19,29,$n$And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.$n$,'mark',10,29,$n$The Markan-parallel with *now in this time ... with persecutions; and in the world to come eternal life.*$n$,$n$Mark 10:29 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, Verily I say unto you, There is no man that hath left house, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my sake, and the gospel’s,* *And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.* (Matthew 19:29) Mark lists the same losses, house, brethren, father, mother, wife, children, lands, and sets a second reason beside Matthew's *my name’s sake*, *for my sake, and the gospel’s*. It is the same promise to anyone who leaves what he has for the one who calls.$n$),
(3717,19,29,$n$And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.$n$,'luke',18,29,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 18:29 — *And he said unto them, Verily I say unto you, There is no man that hath left house, or parents, or brethren, or wife, or children, for the kingdom of Elohim's (God’s) sake,* *And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.* (Matthew 19:29) Luke gives the reason in different words, *for the kingdom of Elohim's (God’s) sake*, where Matthew has *my name’s sake*. The two belong together, because to leave all for his name is to leave all for the kingdom he preaches.$n$),
(3718,19,29,$n$And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.$n$,'psalms',84,10,$n$*A day in thy courts is better than a thousand.*$n$,$n$Psalm 84:10 — *For a day in thy courts is better than a thousand. I had rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my Elohim (God), than to dwell in the tents of wickedness.* *And every one that has forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life.* (Matthew 19:29) The psalm sets the house of Elohim (God) against a lifetime elsewhere, *a day in thy courts is better than a thousand*, and a doorkeeper's place better than a tent of wickedness. The one who gives up houses and lands for his name has weighed it the same way, and the hundredfold answers that weighing.$n$);
INSERT INTO _s496_b3_t VALUES
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',$n$The mountain where he goes apart to pray$n$,$n$The chapter shows Yahusha (Jesus) going apart twice. When he heard that John was dead, he left by ship for a lonely place, and then, after the crowds had been fed and sent away, he went up a mountain by himself.
INSERT INTO _s496_b3_a VALUES
('gathering-of-the-scattered-twelve-tribes-in-sirach-and-2-esdras-and-tobit','matthew',15,24,24),
('reward-according-to-works-the-post-harvest-sifting-in-sirach-and-2-esdras','matthew',16,27,27),
('the-gates-of-sheol-and-the-resurrection-substance-in-wisdom-of-solomon-and-1-enoch','matthew',16,18,18);
INSERT INTO _s496_b3_m VALUES
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2832,14003,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* Yahuah (the LORD) sends Elijah to the king who has taken Naboth's vineyard, the same errand John carries to Herod.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2833,14003,$n$1 Kings 19:1 — *And Ahab told Jezebel all that Elijah had done, and withal how he had slain all the prophets with the sword.* Ahab tells Jezebel and she threatens Elijah's life, as the woman behind Herod's throne turns on John.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2834,14005,$n$Matthew 21:26 — *But if we shall say, Of men; we fear the people; for all hold John as a prophet.* The rulers questioned by Yahusha (Jesus) fear the people because all hold John as a prophet, as Herod did.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2835,14001,$n$Matthew 11:14 — *And if ye will receive it, this is Elijah, which was for to come.* Yahusha (Jesus) says of John that this is Elijah who was for to come, the word set against Herod's guess.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2836,14001,$n$Matthew 17:12 — *Elijah is come already, and they knew him not, but have done unto him whatsoever they listed. Likewise shall also the Son of Adam suffer of them.* Yahusha (Jesus) says Elijah has come and was ill used, and that the Son of Adam will suffer the same from them.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2837,14001,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper?* Zechariah the priest is moved by the Spirit to charge the people with breaking the commandments, and is put to death for it.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2838,14001,$n$Matthew 23:34 — *some of them ye shall kill and crucify; and some of them shall ye scourge in your synagogues.* Yahusha (Jesus) says he sends prophets and wise men and some will be killed, scourged and driven from city to city.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2839,14001,$n$Revelation 11:7 — *when they shall have finished their testimony, the beast that ascendeth out of the bottomless pit shall make war against them.* The witnesses are killed when they have finished their testimony, a likeness to John's end at Herod's hand.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',2840,14003,$n$Mark 6:14 — *he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* Mark gives Herod's conflicted reaction: he says John is risen from the dead and so these works show forth in him.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2841,14013,$n$Exodus 16:14 — *And when the dew that lay was gone up, behold, upon the face of the wilderness there lay a small round thing, as small as the hoar frost on the ground.* Yahuah (the LORD) gives the manna on the face of the wilderness, the bread of the desert that this crowd now stands in.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2842,14013,$n$Numbers 11:7 — *And the manna was as coriander seed, and the colour thereof as the colour of bdellium.* The manna described as coriander seed is the plain wilderness bread that kept Yashar'el (Israel) alive.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2843,14013,$n$2 Kings 4:42 — *brought the man of Elohim (God) bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley.* A man brings twenty barley loaves to Elisha, who orders them given to the people, the first small feeding in a desert land.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2844,14013,$n$Isaiah 25:6 — *shall Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) make unto all people a feast of fat things.* Yahuah (the LORD) will spread a feast on his mountain, a larger table than the one in the wilderness.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2845,14019,$n$Matthew 26:26 — *took bread, and blessed it, and brake it, and gave it to the disciples.* At the last supper he takes, blesses, breaks and gives bread to the disciples, as he did before the five thousand.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2846,14020,$n$Matthew 19:28 — *ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* Yahusha (Jesus) promises twelve thrones for those who followed him, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2847,14013,$n$John 6:1 — *After these things Yahusha (Jesus) went over the sea of Galilee, which is the sea of Tiberias.* John says the same crossing came when the Passover was near, so the wilderness bread falls in the season of the exodus.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2848,14013,$n$John 6:31 — *Our fathers did eat manna in the desert; as it is written, He gave them bread from heaven to eat.* The crowd says their fathers ate manna in the desert, naming the old bread the feeding recalls.$n$),
('scattered-seed-gathering',2849,14014,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) promises to search for his own sheep and seek them out, the work the shepherd does here.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',2850,14013,$n$Matthew 15:32 — *I have compassion on the multitude, because they continue with me now three days, and have nothing to eat.* He says he has compassion on the crowd and will not send them away hungry, the second feeding in the wilderness.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',2853,14025,$n$Habakkuk 3:15 — *Thou didst walk through the sea with thine horses, through the heap of great waters.* Habakkuk says Yahuah (the LORD) walked through the sea, the same walk the disciples see on the water.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',2855,14027,$n$John 8:58 — *Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Before Abraham was, I am.* He says that before Abraham was, I am, the self-naming that sounds in it is I on the sea.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',2856,14027,$n$John 6:20 — *But he saith unto them, It is I; be not afraid.* John tells the same scene with the same words, it is I, be not afraid.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',2857,14033,$n$Matthew 16:16 — *And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* Peter confesses him as the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God), the same word the boat spoke.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',2858,14022,$n$Matthew 8:23 — *And when he was entered into a ship, his disciples followed him.* He enters a ship, his disciples follow, and a storm rises, the first boat before the walking on the sea.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',3494,14002,$n$Matthew 16:14 — *And they said, Some say that thou art John the Baptist: some, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.* The crowds guess John, Elijah or a prophet, the same kind of guess Herod made about him.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',3495,14002,$n$Mark 6:16 — *But when Herod heard thereof, he said, It is John, whom I beheaded: he is risen from the dead.* Mark gives Herod saying it is John whom he beheaded, risen from the dead.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',3496,14003,$n$Leviticus 18:16 — *Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother’s wife: it is thy brother’s nakedness.* The commandment forbids uncovering a brother's wife, the law John stood on against Herod.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',3497,14003,$n$Leviticus 20:21 — *And if a man shall take his brother’s wife, it is an unclean thing: he hath uncovered his brother’s nakedness; they shall be childless.* Taking a brother's wife is called an unclean thing with childlessness as its penalty, the law behind John's rebuke.$n$),
('matthew-14-death-of-john-the-baptist-the-elijah-pattern-1-kings-19-leviticus-18',3498,14003,$n$1 Kings 21:5 — *But Jezebel his wife came to him, and said unto him, Why is thy spirit so sad, that thou eatest no bread?* Jezebel comes to Ahab and takes the matter into her own hands, as Herodias does with Herod.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',3499,14013,$n$Matthew 14:23 — *he went up into a mountain apart to pray: and when the evening was come, he was there alone.* After sending the crowds away he goes up the mountain alone to pray as evening comes.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',3500,14013,$n$Mark 6:31 — *Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while.* Mark records his invitation to the disciples to come apart into a desert place and rest.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',3501,14019,$n$Luke 24:30 — *And it came to pass, as he sat at meat with them, he took bread, and blessed it, and brake, and gave to them.* At Emmaus he takes, blesses, breaks and gives bread, and the two travellers know him.$n$),
('matthew-14-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-in-the-wilderness-2-kings-4-exodus-16',3502,14020,$n$Revelation 21:14 — *twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.* The wall of the city has twelve foundations carrying the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',3503,14023,$n$Matthew 5:1 — *And seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain: and when he was set, his disciples came unto him:* Seeing the crowds he goes up a mountain and the disciples come to him, the mountain of the Sermon.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',3504,14023,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* He takes Peter, James and John up an high mountain apart, where Moses and Elijah appear with him.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',3505,14023,$n$Matthew 26:36 — *Then cometh Yahusha (Jesus) with them unto a place called Gethsemane, and saith unto the disciples, Sit ye here, while I go and pray yonder.* At Gethsemane he tells the disciples to sit here while he goes to pray.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-mountain-where-he-goes-apart',3506,14023,$n$Matthew 28:16 — *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* The eleven go into Galilee to the mountain where he had appointed them.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',3507,14027,$n$Isaiah 41:10 — *Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy Elohim (God).* Elohim (God) tells Yashar'el (Israel) to fear not because he is with them and is their Elohim (God).$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',3508,14027,$n$Isaiah 41:13 — *For I Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) will hold thy right hand, saying unto thee, Fear not; I will help thee.* Yahuah (the LORD) says he will hold thy right hand and help, saying fear not.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',3509,14033,$n$Psalm 2:7 — *I will declare the decree: Yahuah (LORD) hath said unto me, Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee.* Yahuah (the LORD) declares thou art my Son, the word the men in the boat speak.$n$),
('matthew-14-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-tzitzit-wings-of-torah-numbers-15-malachi-4',3510,14036,$n$Matthew 9:20 — *came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment.* A woman diseased twelve years touches the hem of his garment from behind, the same touch the country seeks.$n$),
('matthew-14-walking-on-the-sea-it-is-i-be-not-afraid-job-9-exodus-3',5521,14025,$n$Psalm 77:20 — *Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.* Yahuah (the LORD) led his people like a flock through the sea by the hand of Moses and Aaron.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2859,15008,$n$Isaiah 29:13 — *this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me, but have removed their heart far from me.* Isaiah's own words, the ones Yahusha (Jesus) says were prophesied of them: lips near, heart far, and a fear of Yahuah (the LORD) taught by the precept of men.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2860,15003,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* The fifth commandment, the word Corban set aside, with its promise of long days in the land.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2862,15006,$n$Mark 7:9 — *And he said unto them, Full well ye reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that ye may keep your own tradition.* Mark's version of the charge, with Corban named and explained as a gift, and the commandment of Elohim (God) set against their own tradition.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2863,15006,$n$Deuteronomy 4:2 — *Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it.* Moses forbids adding to the commandment or taking from it, the line the tradition of the elders crossed in both directions.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2864,15006,$n$Deuteronomy 12:32 — *What thing soever I command you, observe to do it: thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it.* The warning against adding to or taking from what Yahuah (the LORD) commanded, given right after a warning against copying another people's ways.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2865,15009,$n$Daniel 7:25 — *shall wear out the saints of the El Elyon (most High), and think to change times and laws.* A power that thinks to change times and laws: the habit of setting men's commandments where Elohim's (God's) stood, seen full grown.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2866,15009,$n$1 John 2:3 — *And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments.* John's plain test: knowing him is shown by keeping his commandments, not the commandments of men.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2867,15001,$n$Matthew 23:16 — *Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor.* The blind guides and their oath rules, which rank the gold above the temple as Corban ranked a gift above father and mother.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',2868,15001,$n$Galatians 1:14 — *being more exceedingly zealous of the traditions of my fathers.* Paul looking back on his own zeal for the traditions of his fathers, the same tradition the scribes bring to Yahusha (Jesus) here.$n$),
('matthew-15-ye-made-the-commandment-of-none-effect-by-tradition-exodus-20-isaiah-29-sirach-3',3513,15008,$n$Colossians 2:8 — *Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men.* Paul's warning against teaching that follows the tradition of men and not the Messiah (Christ), the same line Isaiah and Yahusha (Jesus) draw.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2879,15011,$n$Mark 7:14 — *And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:* Mark's account of the call to the crowd, set directly after the charge that their tradition made the word of Elohim (God) of none effect.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2880,15018,$n$Genesis 6:5 — *every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.* The heart's own evil, seen before the flood, is the source the saying points to and no washing of hands touches it.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2881,15018,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* The prophet locates the trouble within the heart, where Yahuah (the LORD) searches, and not in what enters the mouth.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2882,15018,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.* The promise of a new heart that answers the heart Matthew names, a heart that walks in his statutes and keeps his judgments.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2883,15011,$n$Acts 10:14 — *But Peter said, Not so, Yahuah (Lord); for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean.* Peter, who asked for this saying to be explained, still says he has never eaten anything common or unclean.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',3514,15011,$n$Acts 10:9 — *Peter went up upon the housetop to pray about the sixth hour.* The vision begins as Peter prays on the housetop and grows hungry, the setting in which Matthew's saying about the mouth must not be read.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2884,15011,$n$Leviticus 11:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and to Aaron, saying unto them,* Where Yahuah (the LORD) begins his word on what may be eaten, a law the saying about unwashen hands leaves standing.$n$),
('matthew-15-out-of-the-heart-proceed-evil-thoughts-unwashen-hands-genesis-6-jeremiah-17-psalm-51',2885,15011,$n$Deuteronomy 14:1 — *Ye are the children of Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God).* The food law sits among words about who the people are, children of Yahuah (the LORD) and a holy people, and Matthew's saying leaves it standing.$n$),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',2886,15013,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* The parable of the field: good seed sown by the man, tares sown among it by his enemy.$n$),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',2887,15014,$n$Matthew 13:13 — *Therefore speak I to them in parables: because they seeing see not; and hearing they hear not, neither do they understand.* The reason given for those who see and do not see, and hear and do not hear, set against the blessing on those who do.$n$),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',3515,15013,$n$Matthew 13:36 — *his disciples came unto him, saying, Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field.* The disciples ask for the parable of the tares, and the answer that follows says plainly whose seed each plant is.$n$),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',3516,15013,$n$Isaiah 60:21 — *they shall inherit the land for ever, the branch of my planting, the work of my hands.* Isaiah names what the Father did plant: his people, the branch of his planting, who inherit the land for ever.$n$),
('matthew-15-plants-the-father-did-not-plant-and-blind-guides',3517,15014,$n$Matthew 23:16 — *Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he is a debtor.* The blind guides named again, with the oath rule that shows what their guiding put first.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2871,15021,$n$Matthew 8:5 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) was entered into Capernaum, there came unto him a centurion, beseeching him,* The centurion who came to him at Capernaum, one of the scattered house found under Rome, as the woman is on the coast.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2872,15021,$n$Ruth 1:1 — *Now it came to pass in the days when the judges ruled, that there was a famine in the land. And a certain man of Beth-lehem-judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab, he, and his wife, and his two sons.* A family of Beth-lehem-judah living in Moab, where Ruth, a woman of Moab, was taken as a wife and came into the line by marriage.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2873,15021,$n$1 Kings 17:8 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came unto him, saying,* The word of Yahuah (the LORD) that sent Elijah to a widow of Zidon, on the same coast Yahusha (Jesus) enters.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2874,15021,$n$2 Kings 5:1 — *Now Naaman, captain of the host of the king of Syria, was a great man with his master, and honourable.* Naaman, a Syrian commander in the land of the scattering, reached by the word of a captive girl of Yashar'el (Israel) in his own house.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2875,15021,$n$Luke 4:25 — *many widows were in Yashar'el (Israel) in the days of Elijah, when the heaven was shut up three years and six months.* His own words at Nazareth naming the widow of Sidon and Naaman, the two places the prophets reached beyond the land.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2877,15028,$n$Hebrews 11:31 — *By faith the harlot Rahab perished not with them that believed not, when she had received the spies with peace.* Rahab's faith honored, a case of its own, since she came into the line afterward by marriage.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',2878,15028,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* His marvel at the centurion's faith, said of a man found outside the assembly in the land, the same praise he gives the woman.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',3520,15027,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* The centurion's lowly asking and the faith Yahusha (Jesus) marvelled at, answering the woman's own low asking at the table.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',3521,15027,$n$Ruth 1:1 — *Now it came to pass in the days when the judges ruled, that there was a famine in the land. And a certain man of Beth-lehem-judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab, he, and his wife, and his two sons.* Ruth's way into a house of Yahudah (Judah) was marriage, a different door from the woman's at the table.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',3518,15024,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* The Shepherd's own promise to search for and seek out his scattered sheep, which he is doing on this coast.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',3519,15024,$n$Jeremiah 50:6 — *My people hath been lost sheep: their shepherds have caused them to go astray.* The lost sheep named by the prophet, led astray by their shepherds, whose return brings both houses together.$n$),
('gathering-of-the-scattered-twelve-tribes-in-sirach-and-2-esdras-and-tobit',2869,15024,$n$Matthew 10:5 — *Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not.* The boundary he gave the twelve is the one he repeats to the woman, the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) first.$n$),
('gathering-of-the-scattered-twelve-tribes-in-sirach-and-2-esdras-and-tobit',2870,15024,$n$Romans 15:8 — *Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) was a minister of the circumcision for the truth of Elohim (God), to confirm the promises made unto the fathers.* Paul restates the mission as a sending to the people of the promises, to confirm what Elohim (God) promised the fathers.$n$),
('gathering-of-the-scattered-twelve-tribes-in-sirach-and-2-esdras-and-tobit',2876,15024,$n$Matthew 28:19 — *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations.* The sending after the cross goes out to every nation, the places the lost sheep were scattered to, and not to a new kind of people.$n$),
('gathering-of-the-scattered-twelve-tribes-in-sirach-and-2-esdras-and-tobit',177460,15024,$n$Romans 11:25 — *blindness in part is happened to Yashar'el (Israel), until the fulness of the Gentiles be come in.* Blindness in part to Yashar'el (Israel) until the fulness of the Gentiles comes in, the lost sheep found in every place they were scattered.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-dumb-speak-the-lame-walk-feeding-the-four-thousand-isaiah-35-psalm-107-exodus-16',3522,15031,$n$Matthew 11:5 — *The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear.* The works he told John to report, the blind seeing and the lame walking, done again in front of the multitude.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-dumb-speak-the-lame-walk-feeding-the-four-thousand-isaiah-35-psalm-107-exodus-16',3524,15036,$n$Matthew 14:19 — *he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.* The first feeding with the same acts, he took, blessed, broke, and gave through the disciples, with five loaves and twelve baskets over.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-dumb-speak-the-lame-walk-feeding-the-four-thousand-isaiah-35-psalm-107-exodus-16',3525,15036,$n$Matthew 26:26 — *Yahusha (Jesus) took bread, and blessed it, and brake it, and gave it to the disciples.* The same taking, blessing, breaking and giving at the last supper, where he says what the bread is and looks to his Father's kingdom.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2888,16017,$n$Matthew 11:25 — *thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.* Yahusha (Jesus) thanks the Father for hiding these things from the wise and giving them to babes, which is how Peter received his answer.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2889,16018,$n$Deuteronomy 32:4 — *a Elohim (God) of truth and without iniquity, just and right is he.* Moses' song calls Elohim (God) himself the Rock, perfect in work and just in all his ways, which is the Rock Peter's confession points to.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2890,16018,$n$Psalm 18:2 — *my Elohim (God), my strength, in whom I will trust; my buckler, and the horn of my salvation.* David calls Yahuah (the LORD) his rock, fortress and deliverer, the strong place the building of Matthew 16 stands on.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2891,16018,$n$1 Corinthians 10:4 — *And did all drink the same spiritual drink: for they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them: and that Rock was Messiah (Christ).* Paul names the Rock that followed the fathers in the wilderness as Messiah (Christ), the same one Peter confessed.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2892,16018,$n$1 Peter 2:4 — *To whom coming, as unto a living stone, disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of Elohim (God), and precious,* Peter writes of a living stone refused by men and chosen of Elohim (God), pointing away from himself to the one he confessed.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2893,16018,$n$Ephesians 2:20 — *And are built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) himself being the chief corner stone;* The house stands on the apostles and prophets with Yahusha (Jesus) as chief corner stone, so the building is as old as the prophets.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2895,16018,$n$Psalm 118:22 — *The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner.* The stone the builders refused becomes the head stone of the corner, the rock Peter confessed and the leaders were about to reject.$n$),
('the-gates-of-sheol-and-the-resurrection-substance-in-wisdom-of-solomon-and-1-enoch',2896,16018,$n$Isaiah 38:10 — *I shall go to the gates of the grave: I am deprived of the residue of my years.* Hezekiah says he is going to the gates of the grave, the shut place of the dead that Yahusha (Jesus) says will not prevail.$n$),
('the-gates-of-sheol-and-the-resurrection-substance-in-wisdom-of-solomon-and-1-enoch',2897,16018,$n$Psalm 9:13 — *thou that liftest me up from the gates of death.* The psalmist is lifted by Yahuah (the LORD) from the gates of death, showing those gates are not where the story stops.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',2900,16019,$n$Matthew 18:18 — *Whatsoever ye shall bind on earth shall be bound in heaven.* The binding and loosing given to Peter is given to the disciples together, set among witnesses and a brother who will not hear.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2901,16021,$n$Isaiah 53:1 — *Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of Yahuah (LORD) revealed?* Isaiah opens the suffering servant with the question who will believe the report, the very report Peter could not take.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2902,16021,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered.* Yahuah (the LORD) of hosts bids the sword smite the Shepherd, so his death is the appointed road and the sheep scatter.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2903,16021,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *they shall look upon me whom they have pierced.* Zechariah has Yahuah (the LORD) speak of a pierced one in Jerusalem, mourned as an only son, the one Yahusha (Jesus) says must die there.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2904,16021,$n$Matthew 17:22 — *And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men:* A second time Yahusha (Jesus) tells them he will be killed and raised, and now adds that he will be betrayed into the hands of men.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2905,16021,$n$Matthew 20:17 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) going up to Jerusalem took the twelve disciples apart in the way, and said unto them,* The third telling comes on the road up to Jerusalem itself, with the twelve taken apart and told what will be done to him.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2906,16022,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* The devil showed him all the kingdoms and their glory with no cross, the same offer Peter makes in saying this shall not be.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2907,16021,$n$Galatians 3:13 — *Messiah (Christ) hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us.* Paul says Messiah (Christ) took the curse of hanging on a tree, the covenant's judgment on the breakers, not the commandments.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',2908,16021,$n$Romans 6:14 — *For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace.* Paul says sin shall not rule those brought through his death, and means the door home, not a release from the commandments.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2909,16024,$n$Matthew 10:38 — *And he that taketh not his cross, and followeth after me, is not worthy of me.* The same cost of discipleship was named when he sent out the twelve: whoever will not take his cross and follow is not worthy of him.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2910,16025,$n$John 12:25 — *He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal.* John gives the same sentence and names the life let go and the life kept, the one in this world and the life eternal.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2911,16026,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* The devil offered all the kingdoms and their glory and was refused, which is the bargain Yahusha (Jesus) says no man should make.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2912,16027,$n$Matthew 25:31 — *then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory.* The Son of Adam comes in his glory with his angels and sits on his throne, where each is rewarded and the sheep are divided from the goats.$n$),
('reward-according-to-works-the-post-harvest-sifting-in-sirach-and-2-esdras',2913,16027,$n$Matthew 13:36 — *his disciples came unto him, saying, Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field.* The disciples ask the parable of the tares, and the Son of Adam who sows the seed is the one who comes with the reapers.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2914,16028,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* Six days later Peter, James and John are taken up the mountain and shown a foretaste of the Son of Adam in his glory.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',2915,16028,$n$2 Peter 1:16 — *but were eyewitnesses of his majesty.* Peter, who stood on the mountain, says they were eyewitnesses of his majesty and followed no devised fables.$n$),
('matthew-16-no-sign-but-the-prophet-jonah-the-signs-of-the-times-jonah-1',3526,16004,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah.* Earlier Yahusha (Jesus) answered the scribes and Pharisees with the very same words about the one sign of Jonah.$n$),
('matthew-16-no-sign-but-the-prophet-jonah-the-signs-of-the-times-jonah-1',3527,16004,$n$Isaiah 1:21 — *How is the faithful city become an harlot! it was full of judgment; righteousness lodged in it; but now murderers.* Isaiah laments the faithful city turned harlot, the prophets' picture of covenant unfaithfulness that Yahusha (Jesus) calls adulterous.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',3528,16016,$n$Matthew 14:33 — *Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth thou art the Son of Elohim (God).* After he walked on the sea the men in the boat worshipped him as the Son of Elohim (God), the first sounding of Peter's confession.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',3531,16017,$n$John 6:44 — *No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him: and I will raise him up at the last day.* No one comes to the Son unless the Father draws him, which is what Yahusha (Jesus) says happened to Peter.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',3532,16017,$n$Galatians 1:15 — *But when it pleased Elohim (God), who separated me from my mother’s womb, and called me by his grace,* Paul says Elohim (God) himself separated and called him and revealed his Son in him, as the Father revealed him to Peter.$n$),
('the-gates-of-sheol-and-the-resurrection-substance-in-wisdom-of-solomon-and-1-enoch',3533,16018,$n$1 Corinthians 15:54 — *Death is swallowed up in victory.* Paul says that when the dead are raised incorruptible, death is swallowed up in victory, which is why the gates cannot prevail.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',3534,16021,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah.* Yahusha (Jesus) once told the Pharisees they would have only the sign of Jonah, and now he says plainly what it means.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',3535,16024,$n$Luke 14:27 — *And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.* Luke gives the same saying and says whoever does not bear his cross and come after him cannot be his disciple.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',3536,16024,$n$John 12:25 — *He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal.* John sets the cross-bearing sentence beside the grain of wheat that dies and bears fruit, the life lost and kept.$n$),
('reward-according-to-works-the-post-harvest-sifting-in-sirach-and-2-esdras',3538,16027,$n$Matthew 7:21 — *but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.* The ones who enter the kingdom are those who do the Father's will, not those who only say Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord).$n$),
('reward-according-to-works-the-post-harvest-sifting-in-sirach-and-2-esdras',3539,16027,$n$Psalm 62:12 — *Also unto thee, O Yahuah (Lord), belongeth mercy: for thou renderest to every man according to his work.* David says mercy belongs to Yahuah (the LORD) and that he renders to every man according to his work.$n$),
('reward-according-to-works-the-post-harvest-sifting-in-sirach-and-2-esdras',3540,16027,$n$Romans 2:6 — *Who will render to every man according to his deeds:* Paul states that Elohim (God) will render to every man according to his deeds, the same standard the Son of Adam names.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',124247,16028,$n$Zechariah 14:5 — *and Yahuah Elohai (the LORD my God) shall come, and all the saints with thee.* Zechariah says Yahuah (the LORD) comes and all the saints with him, naming who comes beside the angels.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3541,17001,$n$Matthew 16:28 — *till they see the Son of Adam coming in his kingdom.* The saying just before the mountain: some of those standing there will see the Son of Adam in his kingdom before they die.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3542,17001,$n$Exodus 24:15 — *And Moses went up into the mount, and a cloud covered the mount.* A cloud covers the mount of Yahuah (the LORD) when Moses goes up, the first of the mountain clouds this chapter answers.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3543,17001,$n$Exodus 19:10 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Moses, Go unto the people, and sanctify them to day and to morrow, and let them wash their clothes,* Yahuah (the LORD) tells Moses to have the people wash their clothes and be ready, for he will come down upon the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3546,17002,$n$Daniel 10:5 — *Then I lifted up mine eyes, and looked, and behold a certain man clothed in linen, whose loins were girded with fine gold of Uphaz:* Daniel sees a man clothed in linen, the shining figure whose sight takes the strength out of the one who looks.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3547,17002,$n$Revelation 1:13 — *one like unto the Son of Adam, clothed with a garment down to the foot.* John sees one like the Son of Adam in a long garment, the same shining Yahusha (Jesus) he saw on the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3548,17002,$n$Philippians 2:6 — *Who, being in the form of Elohim (God), thought it not robbery to be equal with Elohim (God):* Paul says Yahusha (Jesus) was in the form of Elohim (God) before he took on the form of a servant.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3549,17003,$n$Deuteronomy 34:5 — *So Moses the servant of Yahuah (LORD) died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of Yahuah (LORD).* Moses the servant of Yahuah (the LORD) died in Moab according to his word, so the man on the mountain is one who has died.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3550,17003,$n$2 Kings 2:11 — *there appeared a chariot of fire, and horses of fire, and parted them both asunder; and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.* Elijah is parted from Elisha by a chariot of fire and goes up by a whirlwind, so the other man on the mountain is one who was taken, not buried.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3552,17003,$n$Luke 9:31 — *Who appeared in glory, and spake of his decease which he should accomplish at Jerusalem.* Luke gives the subject of the talk with Moses and Elijah, his death at Jerusalem.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3553,17003,$n$Hebrews 11:39 — *And these all, having obtained a good report through faith, received not the promise:* The faithful of old, Moses and Elijah among them, have not yet received the promise, and they are not made perfect without us.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3554,17004,$n$Leviticus 23:33 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* This verse opens the Torah's instruction on the feast of tabernacles, the dwelling in booths Peter's word calls to mind.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3555,17004,$n$Zechariah 14:16 — *shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* The feast of tabernacles is still kept in the day of the King, when the nations left alive go up to worship.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3556,17004,$n$John 1:14 — *And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us.* John says the Word dwelt among us and they beheld his glory, the glory the three beheld on the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3557,17004,$n$Revelation 21:3 — *Behold, the tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people.* At the end the tabernacle of Elohim (God) is with men and he dwells with them, the dwelling Peter's booths point toward.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3561,17005,$n$Matthew 3:17 — *And lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.* The Father's voice over the Son at the Jordan is the voice that speaks again out of the cloud.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3562,17005,$n$Exodus 13:21 — *went before them by day in a pillar of a cloud, to lead them the way.* Yahuah (the LORD) led Yashar'el (Israel) by a pillar of cloud, the cloud that speaks over the Son on the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3563,17005,$n$Exodus 40:34 — *Then a cloud covered the tent of the congregation, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) filled the tabernacle.* The cloud covers the tent and the glory of Yahuah (the LORD) fills the tabernacle, the glory that overshadows the three.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3564,17005,$n$1 Kings 8:10 — *And it came to pass, when the priests were come out of the holy place, that the cloud filled the house of Yahuah (LORD),* The cloud fills the house of Yahuah (the LORD) at its dedication, so that the priests cannot stand.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3565,17005,$n$Isaiah 4:5 — *will create upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, and upon her assemblies, a cloud and smoke by day, and the shining of a flaming fire by night.* Yahuah (the LORD) will make a cloud by day and a flaming fire by night over mount Zion, the glory covered by a defence.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3566,17005,$n$Acts 3:22 — *A prophet shall Yahuah Elohaychem (the Lord your God) raise up unto you of your brethren.* Peter reads the Father's words from the cloud as Moses' promise of a prophet like himself, whom the people are to hear.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3567,17005,$n$2 Peter 1:17 — *when there came such a voice to him from the excellent glory.* Peter himself testifies that he heard the Father's voice from the excellent glory on the holy mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3568,17006,$n$Daniel 8:17 — *when he came, I was afraid, and fell upon my face.* Daniel falls on his face before the messenger, the same fall of fear the three make on the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3569,17006,$n$Ezekiel 1:28 — *This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of Yahuah (LORD). And when I saw it, I fell upon my face, and I heard a voice of one that spake.* Ezekiel sees the glory of Yahuah (the LORD), falls on his face, and hears a voice speaking.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3570,17006,$n$Isaiah 6:5 — *for mine eyes have seen the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* Isaiah cries woe at the sight of the King, the same undone fear the three show when the voice speaks.$n$),
('matthew-17-transfiguration-this-is-my-beloved-son-hear-ye-him-exodus-34-deuteronomy-18-malachi-4',3571,17006,$n$Revelation 1:17 — *And when I saw him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying unto me, Fear not.* John falls as dead before him and is told Fear not, as he was raised on the mountain.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3572,17009,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah.* The only sign given to that generation is the sign of Jonah, the three days that the charge to silence looks toward.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3573,17009,$n$Matthew 16:20 — *Then charged he his disciples that they should tell no man that he was Yahusha (Jesus) the Messiah (Christ).* Yahusha (Jesus) charges the disciples to tell no man he was the Messiah (Christ), and goes on to speak of his death.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3575,17011,$n$Matthew 11:14 — *And if ye will receive it, this is Elijah, which was for to come.* Yahusha (Jesus) names John as Elijah, which was for to come, to those who will receive it.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3576,17011,$n$Matthew 14:1 — *At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Yahusha (Jesus),* Herod hears of Yahusha (Jesus) and takes him for John risen, the John he bound for speaking against his marriage.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3577,17011,$n$Luke 1:17 — *And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children.* Gabriel says John will come in the spirit and power of Elijah, turning hearts and preparing a people.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3578,17011,$n$Revelation 11:3 — *I will give power unto my two witnesses, and they shall prophesy a thousand two hundred and threescore days.* Two witnesses are given power and prophesy in sackcloth, carrying Elijah's marks into the days still ahead.$n$),
('matthew-17-elias-is-come-already-restore-all-things-malachi-4-sirach-48',3579,17011,$n$Acts 3:21 — *until the times of restitution of all things, which Elohim (God) hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began.* Peter says heaven keeps Yahusha (Jesus) until the times of restitution of all things, the restoring the prophets spoke.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3580,17017,$n$Deuteronomy 32:5 — *They have corrupted themselves, their spot is not the spot of his children: they are a perverse and crooked generation.* Moses names the generation that corrupted itself a perverse and crooked generation, the word Yahusha (Jesus) uses.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3581,17017,$n$Deuteronomy 32:20 — *for they are a very froward generation, children in whom is no faith.* Yahuah (the LORD) says of the froward generation that they are children in whom is no faith.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3582,17017,$n$Psalm 78:8 — *a generation that set not their heart aright, and whose spirit was not stedfast with Elohim (God).* The psalm calls the fathers a stubborn and rebellious generation that set not their heart aright.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3583,17017,$n$Psalm 95:10 — *Forty years long was I grieved with this generation, and said, It is a people that do err in their heart, and they have not known my ways:* Yahuah (the LORD) was grieved forty years with that generation, a people who err in heart and have not known his ways.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3584,17020,$n$Matthew 13:31 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and sowed in his field:* The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed, the same seed Yahusha (Jesus) names for faith.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3585,17020,$n$Matthew 21:21 — *If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; it shall be done.* Yahusha (Jesus) repeats the mountain saying after the fig tree withers, with the condition that they doubt not.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3586,17020,$n$1 Corinthians 13:2 — *though I have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing.* Paul says that all faith, even enough to remove mountains, is nothing without charity.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3587,17021,$n$Matthew 6:16 — *when ye fast, be not, as the hypocrites, of a sad countenance.* Yahusha (Jesus) teaches that when his hearers fast, they do not do it to be seen.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3588,17021,$n$Isaiah 58:6 — *to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke.* Isaiah says the fast Yahuah (the LORD) chose is loosing bands, undoing burdens and freeing the oppressed.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3589,17021,$n$Acts 13:2 — *As they ministered to Yahuah (Lord), and fasted, the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul.* At Antioch the men fasted while they ministered, and the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) set Barnabas and Saul apart.$n$),
('matthew-17-faith-as-a-grain-of-mustard-seed-remove-this-mountain-zechariah-4-isaiah-40',3590,17021,$n$Acts 14:23 — *And when they had ordained them elders in every church, and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to Yahuah (Lord), on whom they believed.* Paul and Barnabas pray with fasting when they appoint elders and commit them to Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3591,17022,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* The first foretelling that he must go to Jerusalem, suffer, be killed and be raised the third day.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3592,17022,$n$Matthew 20:17 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) going up to Jerusalem took the twelve disciples apart in the way, and said unto them,* The third foretelling, made to the twelve on the road up to Jerusalem, names who will condemn him.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3593,17022,$n$Isaiah 53:6 — *All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and Yahuah (LORD) hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.* Yahuah (the LORD) lays the iniquity of the sheep who have gone astray on the one who is betrayed.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3594,17022,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* The sword is called against the shepherd, the sheep are scattered, and Yahuah's (the LORD's) hand turns upon the little ones.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3595,17024,$n$Exodus 30:11 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* This verse opens the Torah's instruction on the half shekel that each one numbered gives to the sanctuary.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3596,17024,$n$Jonah 1:17 — *Now Yahuah (LORD) had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.* Yahuah (the LORD) prepares a great fish, as he has one ready with the coin for Peter.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3597,17024,$n$John 8:35 — *And the servant abideth not in the house for ever: but the Son abideth ever.* The servant does not abide in the house for ever, but the Son abides ever.$n$),
('matthew-17-tribute-money-the-children-are-free-half-shekel-exodus-30-psalm-24',3598,17024,$n$Romans 14:13 — *that no man put a stumblingblock or an occasion to fall in his brother’s way.* Paul asks that no man put a stumblingblock in his brother's way, the care that sends Peter for the coin.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3599,18001,$n$Matthew 19:14 — *But Yahusha (Jesus) said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven.* Yahusha (Jesus) tells the disciples to let the children come, for the kingdom is of such, the same answer he gave when they asked who is greatest.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3600,18001,$n$Psalm 131:1 — *Yahuah (LORD), my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty: neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me.* The psalmist keeps clear of great matters and quiets himself like a weaned child, the opposite of the disciples' question about who is greatest.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3601,18001,$n$Jeremiah 31:18 — *Thou hast chastised me, and I was chastised, as a bullock unaccustomed to the yoke: turn thou me, and I shall be turned.* Ephraim asks Yahuah (the LORD) to turn him and is then called a pleasant child, which is the turning Yahusha (Jesus) asks of the disciples.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3602,18004,$n$Proverbs 22:4 — *By humility and the fear of Yahuah (LORD) are riches, and honour, and life.* Honour and life come by humility and the fear of Yahuah (the LORD), the proverb's answer to who is greatest.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3603,18004,$n$Isaiah 57:15 — *I dwell in the high and holy place, with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble.* The One who inhabits eternity says he dwells with the contrite and humble, which is where the humbled child stands.$n$),
('the-greatness-as-humility-and-the-proud-cast-down-in-the-wisdom-stream',3604,18004,$n$1 Peter 5:5 — *be clothed with humility: for Elohim (God) resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble.* Peter later tells them to be clothed with humility because Elohim (God) resists the proud, the same teaching put into a letter.$n$),
('the-watcher-rebellion-as-the-stumbling-block-architecture-in-1-enoch',3605,18006,$n$Matthew 5:29 — *it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell.* The Sermon uses the same word offend for the eye and the right hand, with the same end in hell for the whole body.$n$),
('the-watcher-rebellion-as-the-stumbling-block-architecture-in-1-enoch',3606,18006,$n$Mark 9:42 — *it is better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he were cast into the sea.* Mark sets the millstone right after the cup of water given in his name, so what is done to the little ones is counted either way.$n$),
('the-watcher-rebellion-as-the-stumbling-block-architecture-in-1-enoch',3607,18006,$n$Luke 17:1 — *Then said he unto the disciples, It is impossible but that offences will come: but woe unto him, through whom they come!* Luke gives the woe on offences and the millstone, then follows straight with rebuking and forgiving a brother.$n$),
('the-watcher-rebellion-as-the-stumbling-block-architecture-in-1-enoch',3610,18008,$n$Isaiah 66:24 — *for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh.* Isaiah's closing verse puts the worm and the fire on the carcases of the men who transgressed, the fire the warning in Matthew points to.$n$),
('the-watcher-rebellion-as-the-stumbling-block-architecture-in-1-enoch',3611,18008,$n$Jeremiah 7:31 — *And they have built the high places of Tophet, which is in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to burn their sons and their daughters in the fire.* The valley of the son of Hinnom, where Tophet was built and then renamed the valley of slaughter, is the place Yahusha (Jesus) calls by name.$n$),
('resurrection-keeps-the-form',3608,18008,$n$Matthew 5:29 — *it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell.* The Sermon weighs one member against the whole body in the same way, so a man enters life short of a member rather than lose all.$n$),
('resurrection-keeps-the-form',3609,18008,$n$Mark 9:43 — *it is better for thee to enter into life maimed, than having two hands to go into hell.* Mark says enter into life maimed and names the fire that is not quenched, the other end of the same saying.$n$),
('resurrection-keeps-the-form',133759,18008,$n$John 20:27 — *Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side.* The risen Yahusha (Jesus) shows Thomas his hands and side, so the wounds are kept and the body rises in the form it had.$n$),
('resurrection-keeps-the-form',133760,18008,$n$Isaiah 56:5 — *a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.* Yahuah (the LORD) gives the eunuch a place and an everlasting name in his house, so a body lacking something is kept and not shut out.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3612,18010,$n$Psalm 91:11 — *For he shall give his angels charge over thee, to keep thee in all thy ways.* Angels are given charge over the one who makes Yahuah (the LORD) his refuge, which is what the angels of the little ones do.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3613,18010,$n$Daniel 10:13 — *lo, Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me; and I remained there with the kings of Persia.* Michael comes to help the messenger against the prince of Persia, so angels stand and contend on behalf of peoples.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3614,18010,$n$Daniel 10:21 — *But I will shew thee that which is noted in the scripture of truth: and there is none that holdeth with me in these things, but Michael your prince.* Michael is named as the prince of Daniel's people, an angel who stands for them, as angels stand for the little ones.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3615,18010,$n$Hebrews 1:14 — *Are they not all ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation?* Hebrews says what angels do, that they are ministering spirits sent forth to serve those who inherit.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3616,18012,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* Yahuah (the LORD) says he himself will search for his sheep and seek them out, the Shepherd behind the man who leaves the ninety and nine.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3617,18012,$n$Psalm 119:176 — *I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant; for I do not forget thy commandments.* A lost sheep who has not forgotten the commandments asks to be sought, which is the sheep the shepherd goes after.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3618,18012,$n$Isaiah 53:6 — *we have turned every one to his own way; and Yahuah (LORD) hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.* Yahuah (the LORD) lays the iniquity of the straying sheep on the Shepherd who goes after them.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3619,18012,$n$Jeremiah 50:6 — *My people hath been lost sheep: their shepherds have caused them to go astray, they have turned them away on the mountains: they have gone from mountain to hill, they have forgotten their restingplace.* The lost sheep are the people whose own shepherds caused them to go astray, Yashar'el (Israel) and Yahudah (Judah) together.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3620,18012,$n$Luke 15:3 — *And he spake this parable unto them, saying,* Luke tells the same parable of the lost sheep to the Pharisees who murmured that he receives sinners.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3621,18012,$n$2 Peter 3:9 — *Yahuah (Lord) is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.* Peter says Yahuah (the LORD) is not willing that any should perish, the Father's will in the parable put another way.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3622,18012,$n$Matthew 10:6 — *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* Yahusha (Jesus) sends the twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), the flock the strayed sheep belongs to.$n$),
('the-lord-of-the-sheep-and-the-gathering-in-the-animal-apocalypse',3623,18012,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* He says again that he is sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), the sheep of the parable.$n$),
('the-reproof-and-restoration-procedure-in-sirach',3624,18015,$n$Leviticus 19:17 — *Thou shalt not hate thy brother in thine heart: thou shalt in any wise rebuke thy neighbour, and not suffer sin upon him.* Moses already says to rebuke a neighbour and not hate a brother in the heart, which is the private first step Matthew gives.$n$),
('the-reproof-and-restoration-procedure-in-sirach',3625,18015,$n$Deuteronomy 19:15 — *at the mouth of two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses, shall the matter be established.* A matter is established only at the mouth of two or three witnesses, the rule Yahusha (Jesus) takes up at the second step.$n$),
('the-reproof-and-restoration-procedure-in-sirach',3626,18015,$n$1 Corinthians 5:1 — *It is reported commonly that there is fornication among you, and such fornication as is not so much as named among the Gentiles, that one should have his father’s wife.* Paul brings a matter already public before the assembly and wants the man taken away from among them.$n$),
('the-reproof-and-restoration-procedure-in-sirach',3627,18015,$n$Galatians 6:1 — *Brethren, if a man be overtaken in a fault, ye which are spiritual, restore such an one in the spirit of meekness; considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted.* Paul says to restore a fallen brother in meekness, which is the aim of every step, to gain the brother.$n$),
('the-reproof-and-restoration-procedure-in-sirach',3628,18015,$n$1 Timothy 5:19 — *Against an elder receive not an accusation, but before two or three witnesses.* The rule of two or three witnesses is applied to an accusation against an elder.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3629,18018,$n$Matthew 16:19 — *whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.* The same words of binding and loosing given to Peter go here to the disciples together.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3630,18018,$n$John 20:21 — *as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you.* He sends the disciples as the Father sent him and speaks of sins remitted and retained, the same binding and loosing.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3631,18018,$n$Acts 15:1 — *And certain men which came down from Judæa taught the brethren, and said, Except ye be circumcised after the manner of Moses, ye cannot be saved.* When teachers from Judæa said circumcision was needed to be saved, the matter went up to the apostles and elders and not to one man.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3632,18019,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it.* Those who feared Yahuah (the LORD) and spoke to one another were heard by him and written in a book of remembrance.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3633,18019,$n$Matthew 28:20 — *Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.* He promises to be with them to the end, while they teach to observe all that he commanded.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3634,18019,$n$John 5:14 — *Afterward Yahusha (Jesus) findeth him in the temple, and said unto him, Behold, thou art made whole: sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee.* The man made whole is told to sin no more, so what the Father does at the Son's hand comes with the commandment.$n$),
('matthew-18-binding-and-loosing-by-two-or-three-witnesses',3635,18019,$n$Hebrews 13:5 — *for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.* The promise never to leave or forsake is the presence of the one who is the same yesterday, today and for ever.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3636,18021,$n$Genesis 4:23 — *I have slain a man to my wounding, and a young man to my hurt.* Lamech's seventy and sevenfold vengeance is the number Yahusha (Jesus) turns into forgiveness.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3637,18021,$n$Luke 17:3 — *Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him.* Luke has the same count with rebuke first and forgiveness when the brother repents.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3638,18021,$n$Psalm 103:8 — *Yahuah (LORD) is merciful and gracious, slow to anger, and plenteous in mercy.* Yahuah (the LORD) is merciful, slow to anger and plenteous in mercy, the pattern behind the king's pardon.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3639,18021,$n$Micah 7:18 — *Who is a Elohim (God) like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage.* Yahuah (the LORD) pardons iniquity for the remnant of his heritage and delights in mercy, as the king does in the parable.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3640,18023,$n$Matthew 6:14 — *For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you:* The Sermon puts the king's lesson in a line, that the Father forgives those who forgive.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3641,18023,$n$Matthew 7:1 — *Judge not, that ye be not judged.* The warning not to judge comes back on the servant, who is measured by his own measure.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3642,18023,$n$James 2:13 — *For he shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy; and mercy rejoiceth against judgment.* James says judgment without mercy falls on the one who showed none, while mercy rejoices against judgment.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3643,18023,$n$Ephesians 4:32 — *And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as Elohim (God) for Messiah's (Christ’s) sake hath forgiven you.* Paul tells them to forgive as Elohim (God) has forgiven, which is the order of the parable.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3644,18023,$n$Colossians 3:13 — *Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Messiah (Christ) forgave you, so also do ye.* Paul tells those with a quarrel to forgive as Messiah (Christ) forgave, which is the servant's duty in the parable.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3645,18023,$n$Proverbs 21:13 — *Whoso stoppeth his ears at the cry of the poor, he also shall cry himself, but shall not be heard.* The one who stops his ears at a cry will cry himself and not be heard, as the unforgiving servant finds.$n$),
('forgive-and-be-forgiven-the-mercy-met-with-mercy-architecture',3646,18023,$n$James 5:16 — *Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much.* James has brothers confess to each other and pray, and says sins committed are forgiven.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3647,19001,$n$Matthew 4:23 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease among the people.* Matthew 4:23 shows the work he began in Galilee, teaching, preaching the kingdom and healing the people, which he carries across the Jordan.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3648,19001,$n$Joshua 1:1 — *Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Joshua the son of Nun.* After Moses dies, Yahuah (the LORD) tells Joshua to cross the Jordan into the land given to the children of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3649,19004,$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.* Elohim (God) made man in his own image, male and female, and that first making is the ground Yahusha (Jesus) answers from.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3650,19004,$n$Genesis 2:24 — *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* The first marriage, a man leaving his father and mother to cleave to his wife as one flesh, is the beginning he appeals to.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3651,19004,$n$Malachi 2:14 — *Yahuah (LORD) hath been witness between thee and the wife of thy youth.* Yahuah (the LORD) is witness between a man and the wife of his youth, and she is his companion and the wife of his covenant.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3652,19004,$n$1 Corinthians 6:16 — *What? know ye not that he which is joined to an harlot is one body? for two, saith he, shall be one flesh.* Paul quotes the one flesh of Genesis to show that a union of two bodies is a real joining, not a light thing.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3653,19004,$n$Ephesians 5:31 — *shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh.* Paul repeats the creation sentence, a man joined to his wife and the two one flesh, as the ground for how husbands and wives live.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3654,19007,$n$Deuteronomy 24:1 — *then let him write her a bill of divorcement, and give it in her hand.* Moses wrote a bill of divorcement into the law to govern a thing that was already being done, not to make it a right.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3655,19007,$n$Deuteronomy 17:14 — *and shalt say, I will set a king over me, like as all the nations that are about me.* Moses sets rules for a king the people will ask for like the nations, the same kind of allowance made because of the people's hearts.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3656,19007,$n$1 Samuel 8:5 — *now make us a king to judge us like all the nations.* Yashar'el's (Israel's) elders ask for a king like the nations, and it is allowed even though it is a turning from Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3657,19009,$n$Matthew 5:31 — *It hath been said, Whosoever shall put away his wife, let him give her a writing of divorcement:* The Sermon quotes the same bill of divorcement and answers it, as Yahusha (Jesus) answers the Pharisees here.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3658,19009,$n$Mark 10:11 — *And he saith unto them, Whosoever shall put away his wife, and marry another, committeth adultery against her.* Mark records the same saying and adds that the adultery is committed against the wife who was put away.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3659,19009,$n$Luke 16:18 — *Whosoever putteth away his wife, and marrieth another, committeth adultery: and whosoever marrieth her that is put away from her husband committeth adultery.* Luke gives the same saying on divorce straight after the word that not one tittle of the law will fail.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3660,19009,$n$1 Corinthians 7:10 — *And unto the married I command, yet not I, but Yahusha (Lord), Let not the wife depart from her husband:* Paul gives Yahusha's (Jesus's) own word to the married, that the wife is not to depart from her husband and the husband not to put her away.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3661,19010,$n$Isaiah 56:3 — *neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree.* Yahuah (the LORD) tells the eunuch he is no dry tree, and will give him a name better than sons and daughters.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3662,19010,$n$1 Corinthians 7:7 — *For I would that all men were even as I myself. But every man hath his proper gift of Elohim (God), one after this manner, and another after that.* Paul says each man has his own gift from Elohim (God), one married and another not, as Yahusha (Jesus) says the saying is given to some.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3663,19010,$n$1 Corinthians 7:32 — *He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to Yahusha (Lord).* Paul says the unmarried man cares for the things of Yahusha (Jesus), the reason Yahusha (Jesus) gives for those who stay single for the kingdom.$n$),
('creation-ordinance-marriage-and-the-blessed-eunuch-in-jubilees-tobit-and-wisdom-of-solomon',3664,19010,$n$Revelation 14:4 — *These are they which follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth.* John sees a company kept apart for the Lamb, following him wherever he goes, as firstfruits redeemed from among men.$n$),
('receiving-the-children-and-parental-instruction-in-sirach-and-tobit',3665,19013,$n$Matthew 18:1 — *At the same time came the disciples unto Yahusha (Jesus), saying, Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?* The disciples asked who was greatest, and Yahusha (Jesus) set a little child in the middle of them as the answer.$n$),
('receiving-the-children-and-parental-instruction-in-sirach-and-tobit',3666,19013,$n$Mark 10:13 — *And they brought young children to him, that he should touch them: and his disciples rebuked those that brought them.* Mark tells the same scene, the children brought to be touched and the disciples rebuking those who brought them.$n$),
('receiving-the-children-and-parental-instruction-in-sirach-and-tobit',3667,19013,$n$Luke 18:15 — *And they brought unto him also infants, that he would touch them: but when his disciples saw it, they rebuked them.* Luke tells the same scene with infants carried to him and the disciples rebuking them.$n$),
('receiving-the-children-and-parental-instruction-in-sirach-and-tobit',3668,19013,$n$Psalm 8:2 — *Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength because of thine enemies, that thou mightest still the enemy and the avenger.* The psalm says strength is ordained from the mouth of babes and sucklings, the little ones the disciples were turning away.$n$),
('receiving-the-children-and-parental-instruction-in-sirach-and-tobit',3669,19013,$n$Isaiah 11:6 — *and a little child shall lead them.* Isaiah's kingdom has the wolf dwelling with the lamb and a little child leading them.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3670,19016,$n$Deuteronomy 30:15 — *See, I have set before thee this day life and good, and death and evil;* Moses sets life and good beside death and evil before the people and ties life to keeping the commandments.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3671,19016,$n$Leviticus 18:5 — *Ye shall therefore keep my statutes, and my judgments: which if a man do, he shall live in them: I am Yahuah (LORD).* Leviticus says the man who does Yahuah's (the LORD's) statutes and judgments shall live in them.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3672,19016,$n$Ezekiel 20:11 — *And I gave them my statutes, and shewed them my judgments, which if a man do, he shall even live in them.* Yahuah (the LORD) says of the statutes he gave out of Egypt that a man who does them shall even live in them.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3673,19016,$n$Proverbs 4:13 — *Take fast hold of instruction; let her not go: keep her; for she is thy life.* Proverbs tells the son to hold fast to instruction and keep it, because it is his life.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3674,19016,$n$Proverbs 7:2 — *Keep my commandments, and live; and my law as the apple of thine eye.* Proverbs says in one line to keep the commandments and live, and to hold the law as the apple of the eye.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3675,19016,$n$John 2:3 — *And when they wanted wine, the mother of Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, They have no wine.* At Cana his mother tells the servants to do whatever he says, as the young man is now told what to do.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3676,19016,$n$Revelation 22:14 — *Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life.* Revelation blesses those who do his commandments with right to the tree of life and entry into the city.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3677,19018,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land.* The fifth commandment, to honour father and mother with long days in the land, is the one Yahusha (Jesus) adds to his list.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3678,19018,$n$Leviticus 19:18 — *Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people, but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am Yahuah (LORD).* Leviticus holds the word to love your neighbour as yourself, spoken in the name of Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3679,19018,$n$Deuteronomy 5:16 — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee.* Moses repeats the command to honour father and mother, as Yahuah (the LORD) commanded, that it may go well in the land.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3680,19021,$n$Matthew 5:48 — *Be ye therefore perfect, even as your Father which is in heaven is perfect.* The Sermon calls his hearers to be perfect as their Father in heaven is perfect, the same word he gives the young man.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3681,19021,$n$Genesis 17:1 — *walk before me, and be thou perfect.* Yahuah (the LORD) tells Abram to walk before him and be perfect, long before Sinai.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3682,19021,$n$Deuteronomy 18:13 — *Thou shalt be perfect with Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God).* Moses tells the people to be perfect with Yahuah (the LORD) their Elohim (God), apart from the ways of the nations.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3683,19021,$n$Luke 12:33 — *Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.* Luke has the same word to sell and give alms and lay up a treasure in the heavens that fails not.$n$),
('keep-the-commandments-as-life-and-lay-up-treasure-in-heaven-in-sirach-tobit-and-testaments-of-levi',3684,19021,$n$Acts 4:32 — *And the multitude of them that believed were of one heart and of one soul: neither said any of them that ought of the things which he possessed was his own; but they had all things common.* Acts shows the believers of one heart and soul holding all things common, as Yahusha (Jesus) asked the young man to part with his goods.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3685,19023,$n$Proverbs 30:8 — *Remove far from me vanity and lies: give me neither poverty nor riches; feed me with food convenient for me:* A man in Proverbs asks Yahuah (the LORD) for neither poverty nor riches, only the food he needs.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3686,19023,$n$Jeremiah 9:23 — *Thus saith Yahuah (LORD), Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, neither let the mighty man glory in his might, let not the rich man glory in his riches:* Yahuah (the LORD) tells the wise, the mighty and the rich not to glory in their wisdom, might or riches.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3687,19023,$n$Psalm 49:6 — *They that trust in their wealth, and boast themselves in the multitude of their riches;* The psalm names those who trust in their wealth, and says none of them can redeem a brother.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3688,19023,$n$James 5:1 — *Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries that shall come upon you.* James tells the rich to weep and howl for the miseries coming on them.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3689,19023,$n$1 Timothy 6:9 — *they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare.* Paul says those who want to be rich fall into a snare and into lusts that drown men in destruction.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3690,19025,$n$Genesis 18:14 — *Is any thing too hard for Yahuah (LORD)? At the time appointed I will return unto thee.* Yahuah (the LORD) asks whether anything is too hard for him, when a son is promised to Sarah.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3691,19025,$n$Jeremiah 32:17 — *thou hast made the heaven and the earth by thy great power and stretched out arm, and there is nothing too hard for thee.* Jeremiah confesses that the maker of heaven and earth, by his great power, has nothing too hard for him.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3692,19025,$n$Jeremiah 32:27 — *Behold, I am Yahuah (LORD), the Elohim (God) of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?* Yahuah (the LORD), the Elohim (God) of all flesh, himself asks whether anything is too hard for him.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3693,19025,$n$Job 42:2 — *I know that thou canst do every thing, and that no thought can be withholden from thee.* Job confesses that Yahuah (the LORD) can do everything and that no thought is hidden from him.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3694,19025,$n$Luke 1:37 — *For with Elohim (God) nothing shall be impossible.* Luke records that with Elohim (God) nothing shall be impossible, the same word Yahusha (Jesus) gives the disciples.$n$),
('woes-on-the-rich-and-riches-cannot-save-in-1-enoch',3695,19025,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you.* Yahuah (the LORD) promises a new heart and a new spirit in place of the stony heart, doing what man cannot.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3696,19028,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days.* Daniel sees one like the Son of Adam come to the Ancient of days to receive the everlasting kingdom.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3697,19028,$n$Matthew 25:31 — *then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory.* Matthew places the Son of Adam coming in his glory and sitting upon the throne of his glory.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3698,19028,$n$Isaiah 65:17 — *For, behold, I create new heavens and a new earth: and the former shall not be remembered, nor come into mind.* Yahuah (the LORD) creates new heavens and a new earth, the renewing Matthew calls the regeneration.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3699,19028,$n$Isaiah 66:22 — *For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me, saith Yahuah (LORD), so shall your seed and your name remain.* The new heavens and new earth remain before Yahuah (the LORD), and so does the seed and name of his people.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3700,19028,$n$Acts 3:21 — *Whom the heaven must receive until the times of restitution of all things, which Elohim (God) hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began.* The heaven holds him until the times of restitution of all things, spoken by all the holy prophets since the world began.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3701,19028,$n$Romans 8:18 — *For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.* Paul says the sufferings of this time are not worth comparing with the glory to be revealed in those who follow him.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3702,19028,$n$Revelation 21:1 — *I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth were passed away.* John sees a new heaven and a new earth, the first ones passed away, the world made new that Matthew's regeneration points to.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3703,19028,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* The city's twelve gates carry the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3704,19028,$n$Revelation 21:14 — *And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.* The wall of the city has twelve foundations with the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3705,19028,$n$Revelation 4:4 — *round about the throne were four and twenty seats: and upon the seats I saw four and twenty elders sitting, clothed in white raiment.* Twenty-four seats stand round the throne, with elders in white and crowns on them.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3706,19028,$n$Ezekiel 37:15 — *The word of Yahuah (LORD) came again unto me, saying,* The word of Yahuah (the LORD) comes again to Ezekiel, opening the vision of the two sticks joined into one.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3707,19028,$n$Isaiah 11:11 — *set his hand again the second time to recover the remnant of his people, which shall be left, from Assyria, and from Egypt.* Yahuah (the Lord) sets his hand a second time to recover the remnant of his people from every place they were scattered.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3708,19028,$n$Jeremiah 31:31 — *I will make a new covenant with the house of Yashar'el (Israel), and with the house of Yahudah (Judah).* Yahuah (the LORD) promises a new covenant with both the house of Yashar'el (Israel) and the house of Yahudah (Judah).$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3709,19028,$n$Ezekiel 47:1 — *Afterward he brought me again unto the door of the house; and, behold, waters issued out from under the threshold of the house eastward: for the forefront of the house stood toward the east, and the waters came down from under from the right side of the house, at the south side of the altar.* Ezekiel's vision of the restored land begins at the door of the house with waters flowing from under the threshold.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3710,19028,$n$James 1:1 — *to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.* James addresses his letter to the twelve tribes scattered abroad, treating them as a real people still.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3711,19028,$n$Acts 26:7 — *our twelve tribes, instantly serving Elohim (God) day and night, hope to come.* Paul says the twelve tribes, serving Elohim (God) day and night, hope to come to the promise.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3712,19028,$n$1 Corinthians 6:2 — *Do ye not know that the saints shall judge the world? and if the world shall be judged by you, are ye unworthy to judge the smallest matters?* Paul reminds the Corinthians that the saints shall judge the world.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3713,19028,$n$Revelation 20:4 — *I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them.* John sees thrones with those who held to the witness of Yahusha (Jesus) seated on them, judging and reigning with Messiah (Christ).$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3714,19028,$n$Luke 22:28 — *Ye are they which have continued with me in my temptations.* Yahusha (Jesus) tells those who stayed with him in his trials that a kingdom is appointed them, with thrones over the twelve tribes.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3715,19029,$n$Matthew 20:1 — *the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers.* The parable of the householder hiring labourers into his vineyard follows and works out the saying that the first shall be last.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3716,19029,$n$Mark 10:29 — *no man that hath left house, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands.* Mark records the same promise to those who have left house, family and lands for his sake and the gospel's.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3717,19029,$n$Luke 18:29 — *no man that hath left house, or parents, or brethren, or wife, or children, for the kingdom of Elohim's (God’s) sake.* Luke records the same promise to those who left house, family and children for the kingdom of Elohim (God).$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',3718,19029,$n$Psalm 84:10 — *I had rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my Elohim (God), than to dwell in the tents of wickedness.* A day in the courts of Elohim (God) is better than a thousand, and a doorkeeper's place better than the tents of wickedness.$n$);
